= Thermodynamic system
{parent=Thermodynamics}

A thermodynamic system is the part of the physical world selected for an energy and matter balance. Its boundary determines which transfers count as <heat>, <work>, or transported matter.

= Simple compressible system
{parent=Thermodynamic system}

A <thermodynamic system> for which the reversible mechanical <work> is entirely <pressure>-<volume (thermodynamics)> work. At fixed composition its <internal energy> is specified by <entropy> and <volume (thermodynamics)>; additional work modes require additional state variables.

= Thermodynamic equilibrium
{parent=Thermodynamic system}
{wiki}

A <thermodynamic system> is in thermodynamic equilibrium when its macroscopic state has no spontaneous tendency to change through any exchange allowed by its constraints. For an <isolated system>, stable <thermodynamic equilibrium> maximizes <entropy> over the accessible states at fixed total <energy> and conserved material inventories. A wall can forbid an exchange, so always specify what can cross or move. Thermal, mechanical and chemical conditions answer different questions about that same constrained state. \a[https://davidtong.org/teaching/statistical-physics/statmechhtml/S4][David Tong, Statistical Physics, section 4.1: equilibrium and thermal contact]{ref}

For example, two compartments separated by a rigid, impermeable wall that transmits <heat> can reach <thermal equilibrium> while retaining different <pressures> and compositions. Allowing an unloaded piston to move adds a <mechanical equilibrium> condition. Allowing one <chemical species> to cross adds <chemical potential equality for particle exchange>; allowing a <chemical reaction> adds its <chemical equilibrium> condition. A fixed barrier can therefore support a constrained <thermodynamic equilibrium> that ceases to be an equilibrium when the barrier is removed.

Uniformity is not the definition of <thermodynamic equilibrium>. An <isothermal atmosphere> in <hydrostatic equilibrium> can have a common <temperature> while its <pressure> and <mass density> vary with height: the <pressure gradient> balances <gravity>. In an external field, the exchange condition must include the relevant potential <energy>; for charged matter this leads to the <electrochemical potential>. Relativistic gravitational corrections to <thermal equilibrium> are described separately by the <Tolman temperature law>.

A <steady state> only says that the macroscopic description is constant in time. A bar maintained between hot and cold <thermal reservoirs> has steady <heat flux> and produces <entropy>, so it is not in <thermodynamic equilibrium>. Even matching <temperatures> at one perfect-contact interface can coexist with <heat transfer> through the surrounding material; matching those two local values does not establish equilibrium of the whole apparatus. See <thermal conduction> for that spatial transport problem.

The useful simplification is that equilibrium conditions predict an endpoint without computing every collision or flow during relaxation. The <first law of thermodynamics> supplies the conserved quantities, and the <second law of thermodynamics> supplies the direction of change. For a system held at fixed <temperature> and <volume (thermodynamics)>, the corresponding criterion is minimization of <Helmholtz free energy>; at fixed <temperature> and <pressure>, it is minimization of <Gibbs free energy>, with the material and exchange constraints still imposed.

= Mechanical equilibrium
{parent=Thermodynamic equilibrium}
{wiki}

Mechanical equilibrium requires <force balance> for the mechanical displacements that are allowed. Two uniform fluids separated by a freely moving, unloaded, frictionless piston with equal face <areas> require equal <pressures>. A supported piston can sustain a <pressure> difference, and a fluid in <gravity> instead obeys <hydrostatic equilibrium>. For an upward height coordinate $z$, that balance is $dp/dz=-\rho g$, where $\rho$ is <mass density> and $g$ is the downward gravitational acceleration. Mechanical equilibrium alone does not establish <thermal equilibrium> or <chemical equilibrium>.

= Closed system
{parent=Thermodynamic system}

A closed thermodynamic system exchanges no matter with its surroundings, but can exchange <energy> through <heat> or <work>. A sealed cylinder with a moving piston is a typical idealized example.

= Isolated system
{parent=Thermodynamic system}

An isolated thermodynamic system exchanges neither matter nor <energy> with its surroundings. Its total <internal energy> is fixed, while the <second law of thermodynamics> constrains its total <entropy>.

= Quasistatic process
{parent=Thermodynamics}

= Quasi-static process
{synonym}

A quasistatic process is an ideal limit in which a changing <thermodynamic system> passes through a sequence of states arbitrarily close to <thermodynamic equilibrium>. Its intermediate <pressure>, <temperature> and <volume (thermodynamics)> are therefore well defined and obey its <equation of state>. Driving must be slow compared with the system's own equilibration, rather than merely taking a long time on a clock. A <reversible thermodynamic process> additionally allows both system and surroundings to be restored without net change; <friction> can produce <entropy> even in a <quasistatic process>. \a[https://davidtong.org/teaching/statistical-physics/statmechhtml/S4][Tong, Statistical Physics, §§4.2–4.3: quasi-static and reversible processes]{ref}

For a computed example, take a fixed <amount of substance> $n$ of <ideal gas> in a sealed cylinder with a frictionless piston, in contact with a <heat bath> at absolute <temperature> $T$. Reduce the external load in infinitesimal steps so that the piston expands from $V_0$ to $2V_0$. In the reversible limit, the gas <pressure> exceeds the external <pressure> by an infinitesimal amount during expansion, and the <heat transfer> occurs across an infinitesimal <temperature> difference. Exact <mechanical equilibrium> would give no driving <force>; the limiting imbalance supplies motion while preserving equilibrium intermediate states. \a[https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-2/pages/3-4-thermodynamic-processes][OpenStax, §3.4: a piston unloaded in infinitesimal steps]{ref}

Along this reversible <isothermal expansion>, the <ideal gas law> gives
$$
p(V)=\frac{nRT}{V},\qquad nRT=1\,\mathrm{kJ},\qquad V_0=1\,\mathrm L=10^{-3}\,\mathrm{m^3},\qquad
p_0=\frac{nRT}{V_0}=1\,\mathrm{MPa}.
$$
Here $R$ is the <molar gas constant>; specifying $nRT$ fixes an <energy> scale, without choosing $n$ and $T$ separately. The initial and final equilibrium states are $(V_0,p_0,T)$ and $(2V_0,p_0/2,T)$. Count <work> delivered to the surroundings as positive. The reversible <work> is the shaded <area> beneath the <pressure>-<volume (thermodynamics)> curve:
$$
W_{\rm out,rev}=\int_{V_0}^{2V_0}p(V)\,dV
=nRT\ln2\approx0.693147\,\mathrm{kJ}.
$$
The <integral> of $1/V$ supplies the <natural logarithm>. Since <internal energy of an ideal gas> depends only on <temperature>, $\Delta U=0$; the <first law of thermodynamics>, $\Delta U=Q_{\rm in}-W_{\rm out}$, gives $Q_{\rm in,rev}=0.693147\,\mathrm{kJ}$ to the displayed precision. The <heat bath> replaces the <energy> delivered as <work>. \a[https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-2/pages/3-2-work-heat-and-internal-energy][OpenStax, §3.2: pressure-volume work and the isothermal integral]{ref}

\Image[quasistatic-isothermal-pv-work.png]
{title=Reversible <isothermal expansion>: <work> is the <area> beneath the equilibrium <pressure> curve}
{description=Fixed <amount of substance> of <ideal gas>, with $nRT=1\,\mathrm{kJ}$ and $V_0=1\,\mathrm L$. The blue curve gives every intermediate equilibrium state; its shaded <area> is $0.693147\,\mathrm{kJ}$. The arrow indicates expansion, without specifying a time dependence.}

Now start from the same initial state, but abruptly lower the external <pressure> to the constant value $p_{\rm ext}=p_0/2=0.5\,\mathrm{MPa}$. There is a finite initial <pressure> imbalance, so this is an irreversible expansion rather than a <quasistatic process>. Assume the piston begins and ends at rest, has no net change in stored <energy>, and has no external <friction>; relaxation occurs within the <gas> and its <heat bath>. After motion has settled and the <heat bath> has restored <temperature> $T$, <mechanical equilibrium> and the <ideal gas law> give $V_f=nRT/p_{\rm ext}=2V_0$. The <work> delivered to the constant load is
$$
W_{\rm out,load}=p_{\rm ext}(V_f-V_0)
=(0.5\,\mathrm{MPa})(1\,\mathrm L)=0.5\,\mathrm{kJ},\qquad
\Delta U=0,\qquad Q_{\rm in,load}=0.5\,\mathrm{kJ}.
$$
This <work> follows directly from load <force> times piston <displacement>, using $dV=A\,dx$ for piston <area> $A$. Intermediate gas states need not have a single equilibrium <pressure> or <temperature>. In particular, neither $p(V)=nRT/V$ at the bath <temperature> nor $p(V)=p_{\rm ext}$ describes the actual gas during this irreversible motion. The rectangle below plots the known external load; its upper edge is not an equilibrium gas path.

\Image[reversible-versus-constant-load-work.png]
{title=The same equilibrium endpoints give different <work> for reversible and constant-load expansion}
{description=The blue curve is the reversible <ideal gas> path. The dashed orange line is constant external <pressure>, whose rectangle gives $0.5\,\mathrm{kJ}$ of load <work>. The additional blue shading is $0.193147\,\mathrm{kJ}$ available in the reversible case. Black points mark the common gas endpoints; the open orange point marks the initial external load, and the dotted gap indicates the initial imbalance. No irreversible internal gas path is drawn.}

The <entropy> accounting makes the difference precise. Because <entropy> is a <state function>, both expansions have the same gas <entropy> change, computed along the reversible <isothermal expansion>:
$$
\Delta S_{\rm gas}=\int_{V_0}^{2V_0}\frac{\delta Q_{\rm rev}}{T}=nR\ln2.
$$
The <thermal reservoir> stays at $T$ and loses <heat> $Q_{\rm in}=W_{\rm out}$, so $\Delta S_{\rm bath}=-W_{\rm out}/T$. Assume the ideal load stores <work> without an <entropy> change. The combined <entropy production> is therefore
$$
\Delta S_{\rm total}=nR\ln2-\frac{W_{\rm out}}{T}
=\begin{cases}
0,&\text{reversible expansion},\\
nR(\ln2-1/2)>0,&\text{constant external load}.
\end{cases}
$$
No numerical <entropy> unit is implied by $nRT=1\,\mathrm{kJ}$ alone: $nR$ or $T$ must also be specified. The lost <work> opportunity is $T\Delta S_{\rm total}=0.193147\,\mathrm{kJ}$ for this comparison. This calculation uses equilibrium endpoints and the reservoir's <temperature>, without assigning equilibrium intermediate states to the irreversible gas. \a[https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-2/pages/4-6-entropy][OpenStax, §4.6: entropy as a state function and reversible reference paths]{ref}

For another boundary choice, let the same <ideal gas> expand into an initially evacuated compartment of <volume (thermodynamics)> $V_0$ inside a rigid, insulated vessel of total <volume (thermodynamics)> $2V_0$. Removing the partition causes <free expansion>: no load is moved and no <heat> crosses the outer boundary, so $W_{\rm out}=Q_{\rm in}=0$ and $\Delta U=0$. After equilibration the <temperature> is again $T$, the <pressure> is $p_0/2$, and the gas <entropy> has increased by $nR\ln2$. The outer vessel has fixed <volume (thermodynamics)>; the gas's accessible <volume (thermodynamics)> doubles. Restoring the initial gas state requires compression and removal of <heat>, changing the surroundings. Equal endpoints do not make <free expansion> reversible. \a[https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-2/pages/4-1-reversible-and-irreversible-processes][OpenStax, §4.1: insulated free expansion and restoration]{ref}

A slowly moving piston with dry <friction> supplies a different caution: its gas can remain arbitrarily close to <thermodynamic equilibrium>, yet rubbing still converts <work> into <heat> and produces <entropy>. If the frictional <force> has a nonzero slow-motion limit, its dissipated <energy> over a fixed travel does not vanish with speed. Reversing the motion reverses <friction>, so infinitesimally changing the external load cannot retrace the same operation and restore the surroundings. The <heat capacity> calculations additionally exclude <friction> and other dissipative <work>. \a[https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-2/pages/3-4-thermodynamic-processes][OpenStax, §3.4: a quasi-static piston with friction]{ref}

The usefulness of a <quasistatic process> is that it replaces detailed motion by a readable sequence of equilibrium states. An <equation of state> and an <integral> then turn a <pressure>-<volume (thermodynamics)> drawing into an <energy> budget. The beautiful distinction is that <internal energy> and <entropy> depend on the endpoints, while <heat> and <work> record how the change was performed. Reversible benchmarks expose how much <work> an irreversible device sacrifices, while <entropy production> explains why simply making it slower need not recover that opportunity.

= State function
{parent=Thermodynamics}

A state function depends on the current equilibrium state, rather than the path used to reach it. <Internal energy> and <entropy> are state functions; transferred <heat> and <work> depend on the process.

= Heat engine
{parent=Thermodynamics}

A heat engine operates cyclically, taking in <heat>, delivering net <work>, and rejecting the remaining <heat>. For two <thermal reservoirs>, the <first law of thermodynamics> gives $W=Q_h-Q_c$ and <Carnot's theorem> bounds its <thermal efficiency>.

= Refrigerator
{parent=Thermodynamics}

A refrigerator consumes <work> to transfer <heat> from a colder region to warmer surroundings. Its useful output is the <heat> extracted from the colder region; this transfer obeys the <second law of thermodynamics> because the device requires an additional input of <energy>.

= Heat pump
{parent=Thermodynamics}

A heat pump consumes <work> to transfer <heat> to a warmer region. Its useful output is the <heat> delivered to that region; the same thermodynamic cycle can be described as a <refrigerator> when the cold-side extraction is the objective.

= Free expansion
{parent=Thermodynamics}

Free expansion lets a gas occupy an initially empty region without pushing against an external load. In an insulated <isolated system>, transferred <heat> and external <work> are both zero, so the <internal energy> is unchanged; the process is generally irreversible and can increase <entropy>.

= Thermodynamic phase
{parent=Thermodynamics}

A thermodynamic phase is a form of macroscopic equilibrium characterized by its structure, symmetries and thermodynamic response. Away from <phase transitions>, its <free-energy density> varies smoothly with the control parameters. Different phases can share a <phase coexistence> boundary. An ordered magnetic phase is distinguished from a disordered phase by its spontaneous <order parameter>; positive and negative ordered states are different <pure thermodynamic phases> related by <spin inversion symmetry>.

= Phase fraction
{parent=Thermodynamic phase}
{title2=$x_\beta=m_\beta/m$}

A phase fraction is the proportion of a mixture occupying a specified <thermodynamic phase>. State the basis: by <mass>, $x_\beta=m_\beta/\sum_\alpha m_\alpha$; by <volume (thermodynamics)>, divide the occupied <volume (thermodynamics)> by the total <volume (thermodynamics)>. These fractions need not agree when the <mass densities> differ. The <latent heat> <heating curve> uses the <liquid> fraction by <mass>, whereas <liquid fraction> in a <mushy layer> is defined by <volume (thermodynamics)>.

= Laws of thermodynamics
{parent=Thermodynamics}
{wiki}

The laws organize the equilibrium and process constraints of <thermodynamics>: the <zeroth law of thermodynamics> makes thermal equilibrium transitive, the <first law of thermodynamics> expresses energy balance, the <second law of thermodynamics> restricts entropy changes, and the <third law of thermodynamics> describes the zero-temperature limit. Their analogy with horizon mechanics becomes quantitative through <Hawking temperature> and <Bekenstein-Hawking entropy>.

= Zeroth law of thermodynamics
{parent=Laws of thermodynamics}
{wiki}

If two systems are each in <thermal equilibrium> with a third, they are in thermal equilibrium with each other. Equilibrium classes can therefore be labelled by a common <thermodynamic temperature>. The corresponding <zeroth law of black-hole mechanics> states constancy of <surface gravity> over an equilibrium horizon; <Hawking radiation> identifies that quantity with physical temperature up to its universal conversion factor.

= Compressibility
{parent=Thermodynamics}
{title2=$\kappa_X=-V^{-1}(\partial V/\partial P)_{X,N}$}
{wiki}

Compressibility measures how readily a sample's <volume (thermodynamics)> decreases when its <pressure> increases. For a <simple compressible system> with fixed particle number $N$ and composition, specify the constraint $X$:
$$
\kappa_X=-\frac1V\left(\frac{\partial V}{\partial P}\right)_{X,N},\qquad
\frac{dV}{V}=-\kappa_X\,dP\quad(X,N\text{ fixed}).
$$
Here $P$ denotes <pressure>, also written $p$ elsewhere. The minus sign makes <compressibility> positive on a mechanically stable equilibrium branch. Its units are inverse <pressure>, $\mathrm{Pa^{-1}}$; its reciprocal $K_X=1/\kappa_X$ is the constrained <bulk modulus>, measured in <pascal (unit)> units. Holding <temperature> fixed gives <isothermal compressibility>; holding <entropy> fixed gives <isentropic compressibility> $\kappa_S$ along an <isentropic process>. The latter describes a reversible <adiabatic compression>, since an irreversible <adiabatic process> can increase <entropy>. \a[https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/3-20-materials-at-equilibrium-sma-5111-fall-2003/e402e3f0f555e15ff3af29b5b44b4e57_notes1.pdf][G. Ceder, MIT thermodynamics notes, section 1.14: compressibility]{ref}

For an <ideal gas>, the <ideal gas law> $PV=Nk_BT$ and the <reversible ideal-gas adiabat> give, at the same equilibrium state,
$$
\kappa_T=\frac1P,\qquad
\kappa_S=\frac1{\gamma P},\qquad
\gamma=\frac{C_P}{C_V}>1.
$$
Here $C_P$ and $C_V$ are the whole sample's <heat capacity at constant pressure> and <heat capacity at constant volume>, and $\gamma$ is the <heat capacity ratio>. Constant $\gamma$ is required for the finite-path formula $PV^\gamma=\mathrm{constant}$. Reversible <adiabatic compression> raises the <temperature>, making the <pressure> rise more steeply than in an <isothermal compression>. Thus keeping <entropy> fixed makes the <gas> harder to compress locally. For a classical <monatomic gas> at $100\,\mathrm{kPa}$, $\gamma=5/3$, so $\kappa_T=10^{-5}\,\mathrm{Pa^{-1}}$ and $\kappa_S=6\times10^{-6}\,\mathrm{Pa^{-1}}$; the corresponding values of the <bulk modulus> are $100\,\mathrm{kPa}$ and $166.67\,\mathrm{kPa}$.

For a smooth, stable single <thermodynamic phase> at positive <temperature>, <Maxwell relations> also imply
$$
\frac{\kappa_T}{\kappa_S}=\frac{C_P}{C_V},\qquad
\kappa_T-\kappa_S=\frac{TV\alpha^2}{C_P},
$$
where $\alpha$ is the <coefficient of thermal expansion>. The <ideal gas> result is one example of this broader link between mechanical and thermal responses. The equality $\kappa_T=\kappa_S$ is possible when $\alpha=0$ and the other responses remain finite. These formulas compare equilibrium <partial derivatives>, rather than arbitrary fast or dissipative compression histories. \a[https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/2-43-advanced-thermodynamics-spring-2024/1Q7kBt43LW4oPrh3pxYjs2xxe7HWsC1oR_transcript.pdf][G. P. Beretta, MIT 2.43, Lecture 7: response-function identities]{ref}

= Isothermal compressibility
{parent=Compressibility}
{title2=$\kappa_T=-V^{-1}(\partial V/\partial P)_{T,N}$}

Isothermal compressibility is the <compressibility> measured at fixed <temperature>, particle number and composition. A slowly compressed sealed piston in contact with a <thermal reservoir> can approach this constraint. For an <ideal gas>, differentiating $V=Nk_BT/P$ at fixed <temperature> gives $(\partial V/\partial P)_{T,N}=-V/P$, hence $\kappa_T=1/P$. A small <pressure> increase $\Delta P$ therefore gives $\Delta V/V\simeq-\Delta P/P$. For a finite change, use the whole <isothermal process> curve $V_2/V_1=P_1/P_2$ instead.

Take one <mole> of classical <monatomic gas>, with stipulated <molar gas constant> $R=8.314\,\mathrm{J\,mol^{-1}\,K^{-1}}$, initially at $T_0=300\,\mathrm K$ and $P_0=100\,\mathrm{kPa}$. The initial <volume (thermodynamics)> is $V_0=nRT_0/P_0=0.024942\,\mathrm{m^3}$. Compressing to $V_0/2$ gives
$$
\begin{aligned}
\text{Isothermal:}\quad &P_2=2P_0=200\,\mathrm{kPa},\quad T_2=300\,\mathrm K,\\
\text{Reversible adiabatic:}\quad &P_2=2^{5/3}P_0\simeq317.48\,\mathrm{kPa},\quad
T_2=2^{2/3}T_0\simeq476.22\,\mathrm K.
\end{aligned}
$$
The <isothermal compression> releases <heat> to the <thermal reservoir>; the reversible <adiabatic compression> retains the input <work> as <internal energy>. Reversing either curve gives the corresponding expansion, with <adiabatic expansion> cooling the <gas>.

\Image[gas-isothermal-adiabatic-compression.png]
{title=An <ideal gas> resists reversible <adiabatic compression> more strongly than <isothermal compression>}
{description=Left: two <pressure>-<volume (thermodynamics)> curves through the same initial state; arrows point toward compression. Right: the corresponding <temperature> changes, including cooling during reversible <adiabatic expansion>. The dotted line marks half the initial <volume (thermodynamics)>. One <mole> of classical <monatomic gas>, with $\gamma=5/3$ and the stipulated $R$ above.}

The equilibrium curvature of <Gibbs free energy> provides another measurement of <isothermal compressibility>:
$$
\kappa_T=-\frac1V\left(\frac{\partial^2G}{\partial P^2}\right)_{T,N}.
$$
This follows from $V=(\partial G/\partial P)_{T,N}$, with $G$ denoting <Gibbs free energy>. Within a stable single <thermodynamic phase>, increasing <pressure> reduces <volume (thermodynamics)> and makes this curvature negative. At <phase coexistence>, changing the <phase fraction> can change the <volume (thermodynamics)> at constant <temperature> and <pressure>; the usual finite single-phase <isothermal compressibility> then need not exist. Near a <critical point>, it can diverge. \a[https://www.feynmanlectures.caltech.edu/I_45.html#Ch45-S3][Feynman, Leighton and Sands, section 45-3: compression and phase coexistence]{ref}

= Isentropic compressibility
{parent=Compressibility}
{title2=$\kappa_S=-V^{-1}(\partial V/\partial P)_{S,N}$}

= Adiabatic compressibility
{synonym}

Isentropic compressibility is the <compressibility> at fixed <entropy>, particle number and composition. The conventional name adiabatic compressibility refers to a reversible <adiabatic process>; an irreversible <adiabatic compression> need not keep <entropy> fixed. For an <ideal gas>, it is $\kappa_S=1/(\gamma P)$, where $\gamma=C_P/C_V$ is the <heat capacity ratio>. It describes a steeper local pressure response than <isothermal compressibility> when $\gamma>1$.

= Maxwell relations
{parent=Thermodynamics}
{c}
{title2=$(\partial S/\partial V)_{T,N}=(\partial P/\partial T)_{V,N}$}
{wiki}

= Maxwell relation
{c}
{synonym}

Maxwell relations turn <pressure>, <volume (thermodynamics)> and <temperature> measurements into information about <entropy>. For example, the <pressure> rise when heating a rigid sealed vessel determines the <entropy> change when expanding the same sample isothermally:
$$
\left(\frac{\partial S}{\partial V}\right)_{T,N}
=\left(\frac{\partial P}{\partial T}\right)_{V,N}.
$$
The quantities on the two sides describe different experiments, yet both are <mixed partial derivatives> of one <Helmholtz free energy>. This is the useful surprise: a mechanical measurement can reveal a thermal property. Similarly, the <coefficient of thermal expansion> gives $(\partial S/\partial P)_{T,N}=-\alpha V$. \a[https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/5-60-thermodynamics-kinetics-spring-2008/7bc33304ab1db561a64b4a6b12e25476_5_60_lecture13.pdf][MIT 5.60, Lecture 13: entropy derivatives from equations of state]{ref}

For a <simple compressible system>, hold particle number $N$ and composition fixed, and allow only <pressure>-<volume (thermodynamics)> <work>. Write $U$ for <internal energy>, $H=U+PV$ for <enthalpy>, $F=U-TS$ for <Helmholtz free energy>, and $G=H-TS$ for <Gibbs free energy>. Their natural-variable differentials are
$$
\begin{aligned}
dU(S,V,N)&=T\,dS-P\,dV, &dH(S,P,N)&=T\,dS+V\,dP,\\
dF(T,V,N)&=-S\,dT-P\,dV, &dG(T,P,N)&=-S\,dT+V\,dP.
\end{aligned}
$$
Thus the first <partial derivatives>, with the subscript recording the variables kept fixed, are
$$
\begin{aligned}
U_S\big|_{V,N}=T,&\quad U_V\big|_{S,N}=-P, &H_S\big|_{P,N}=T,&\quad H_P\big|_{S,N}=V,\\
F_T\big|_{V,N}=-S,&\quad F_V\big|_{T,N}=-P, &G_T\big|_{P,N}=-S,&\quad G_P\big|_{T,N}=V.
\end{aligned}
$$
The <symmetry of second derivatives> produces all four standard <Maxwell relations>, including their signs:
$$
\begin{aligned}
U:\quad\left(\frac{\partial T}{\partial V}\right)_{S,N}
&=-\left(\frac{\partial P}{\partial S}\right)_{V,N},\\
H:\quad\left(\frac{\partial T}{\partial P}\right)_{S,N}
&=\left(\frac{\partial V}{\partial S}\right)_{P,N},\\
F:\quad\left(\frac{\partial S}{\partial V}\right)_{T,N}
&=\left(\frac{\partial P}{\partial T}\right)_{V,N},\\
G:\quad\left(\frac{\partial S}{\partial P}\right)_{T,N}
&=-\left(\frac{\partial V}{\partial T}\right)_{P,N}.
\end{aligned}
$$
For a brief derivation of the $F$ relation, differentiate $F_T=-S$ with respect to $V$ and $F_V=-P$ with respect to $T$: $-S_V=F_{TV}=F_{VT}=-P_T$. Each other row follows in the same way. Assume continuous second <partial derivatives> of the relevant <thermodynamic potential> in the region considered. At a <phase transition>, the equilibrium potential may lose this smoothness; apply the identities within each smooth <thermodynamic phase>, and handle <latent heat> and <phase coexistence> separately. If particle numbers vary, the potential differentials acquire <chemical potential> terms, so the fixed-$N$ constraints must remain explicit. \a[https://davidtong.org/teaching/statistical-physics/statmechhtml/S4#S4.SS4.SSS2][David Tong, Statistical Physics, section 4.4.2: the four Maxwell relations]{ref}

For an <ideal gas> with fixed $N$, the <ideal gas law> gives $P=Nk_BT/V$. At fixed <volume (thermodynamics)>, heating changes the <pressure> at rate $Nk_B/V$. Consequently an <isothermal expansion> at positive <temperature> has
$$
\Delta S=\int_{V_1}^{V_2}\left(\frac{\partial P}{\partial T}\right)_{V,N}\,dV
=Nk_B\ln\frac{V_2}{V_1}.
$$
For one <mole> with $R=8.314\,\mathrm{J\,mol^{-1}\,K^{-1}}$ and $V_2=2V_1$, this is $\Delta S=R\ln2\simeq5.763\,\mathrm{J\,K^{-1}}$. At $300\,\mathrm K$, a reversible <isothermal expansion> absorbs $Q=T\Delta S\simeq1728.85\,\mathrm J$ and performs equal <work>, since its <internal energy> is unchanged. The <entropy> difference is a <state function> difference: the same equilibrium endpoints give the same $\Delta S$ in an insulated <free expansion>, although there $Q=0$ and the process is irreversible.

\Image[maxwell-entropy-pressure-derivatives.png]
{title=<Ideal gas> <entropy> slopes equal measured <pressure> responses to heating}
{description=Left: <entropy> relative to one common reference state $(T_0,V_0)$ for a classical <monatomic gas>, $[S(T,V)-S(T_0,V_0)]/(Nk_B)=\frac32\ln(T/T_0)+\ln(V/V_0)$, with $T_0=300\,\mathrm K$. Each fixed-<temperature> curve gains $\ln2$ on doubling the <volume (thermodynamics)>. Right: both <partial derivatives>, multiplied by $V_0/(Nk_B)$, equal $V_0/V$; circles are independent finite differences of <entropy>, while the line is the <pressure> response.}

The same <Maxwell relations> let thermal measurements determine the <difference between constant-pressure and constant-volume heat capacities>. In a smooth single <thermodynamic phase>,
$$
dS=\frac{C_V}{T}\,dT+\frac{\alpha}{\kappa_T}\,dV
=\frac{C_P}{T}\,dT-\alpha V\,dP,
$$
using <heat capacity at constant volume>, <heat capacity at constant pressure>, <coefficient of thermal expansion> and <isothermal compressibility>. Along fixed <pressure>, $dV=\alpha V\,dT$, so
$$
C_P-C_V=\frac{TV\alpha^2}{\kappa_T}.
$$
Thus measured expansion and compression responses determine a <heat capacity> difference without measuring both <heat capacities> separately. They do not determine the absolute <heat capacities> or absolute <entropy> by themselves; an independent thermal measurement supplies that missing information. For an <ideal gas>, $\alpha=1/T$ and $\kappa_T=1/P$ reduce the result to $C_P-C_V=PV/T=Nk_B$, which is <Mayer's relation>.

When the necessary higher <partial derivatives> exist and commute, differentiating the <Maxwell relations> once more also gives
$$
\left(\frac{\partial C_V}{\partial V}\right)_{T,N}
=T\left(\frac{\partial^2P}{\partial T^2}\right)_{V,N},\qquad
\left(\frac{\partial C_P}{\partial P}\right)_{T,N}
=-T\left(\frac{\partial^2V}{\partial T^2}\right)_{P,N}.
$$
Integrating these slopes determines how a <heat capacity> changes with <volume (thermodynamics)> or <pressure> at fixed <temperature>. For an <ideal gas>, both right-hand sides vanish because the <ideal gas law> is linear in <temperature> at either fixed <volume (thermodynamics)> or fixed <pressure>. Consequently $C_V$ and $C_P$ depend only on <temperature> at fixed $N$, although they need not be constant in <temperature>. \a[https://davidtong.org/teaching/statistical-physics/statmechhtml/S4#S4.SS4.SSS2][Tong, section 4.4.2: heat-capacity changes from thermal pressure and volume curves]{ref}

Historically, <James Clerk Maxwell>'s \i[Theory of Heat], first published in 1871, presents these connections using the geometry of <isothermal processes> and reversible <adiabatic processes>. His fourth edition, page 167, explicitly relates expansion on heating at fixed <pressure> to <heat> emitted during an <isothermal compression>: the modern $G$ relation gives $\delta Q_{\rm rev}=T\,dS=-T\alpha V\,dP$. This explains why a substance with positive <coefficient of thermal expansion> emits <heat> when compressed isothermally, whereas one with negative coefficient absorbs it. \a[https://www.cambridge.org/core/books/theory-of-heat/7052E2DAA4CD862126D5105ABBFFAD0F/listing][Maxwell, Theory of Heat, publication record and chapter IX]{ref} \a[https://en.wikisource.org/wiki/Page:Theory_of_Heat,_James_Clerk_Maxwell,_Fourth_Edition.djvu/183][Maxwell, Theory of Heat, fourth edition, page 167]{ref}

= Diffusion
{parent=Thermodynamics}
{title2=$\mathbf j=-D\nabla C$}

Diffusion is the spreading of heat or material by microscopic random motion. A <concentration> gradient produces flux down that gradient; with uniform <diffusion coefficient> $D$, conservation gives the <diffusion equation> $C_t=D\nabla^2C$. In a moving carrier, <advection> must also be included. Physical diffusion is a transport mechanism, distinct from the general probabilistic class of <Markov diffusion>.

= Molecular diffusion
{parent=Diffusion}

Molecular diffusion transports material through random molecular motion rather than coherent fluid <advection>. Its continuum limit gives a concentration flux $-\kappa\nabla\chi$, with molecular <diffusivity> $\kappa$, and a <diffusion equation> when no bulk motion is present. Molecular diffusion acts on the small scalar gradients produced by stretching, even when the macroscopic effective transport is much faster than molecular transport.

= Rotational diffusion
{parent=Diffusion}
{wiki}
{title2=$D_r$}

Rotational diffusion randomizes orientation. For a unit orientation vector on a sphere, isotropic rotational diffusion contributes $D_r\nabla_p^2f$ to the <Fokker-Planck equation>, where $\nabla_p^2$ is the <Laplace-Beltrami operator>. Its coefficient has units inverse time. It differs from the translational <diffusion coefficient>, whose units are length squared per time.

= Thermal expansion
{parent=Thermodynamics}
{wiki}

Thermal expansion is a change in a body's dimensions as its <temperature> changes under a specified mechanical constraint. A heated <gas> under a freely moving loaded piston usually increases its <volume (thermodynamics)> at fixed <pressure>; heating the same <gas> in a rigid vessel instead raises its <pressure>. The <coefficient of thermal expansion> quantifies the first response. Expansion can be negative, so the name does not assert that every substance grows on heating.

For an <ideal gas> at fixed <pressure> and particle number, $V=Nk_BT/P$ gives $V_2/V_1=T_2/T_1$. Heating from $300\,\mathrm K$ to $330\,\mathrm K$ therefore increases the <volume (thermodynamics)> by exactly $10\%$ within the <ideal gas> model. For a <solid>, specify whether the measurement concerns length or <volume (thermodynamics)>: for uniform expansion in all three directions, the volumetric coefficient is three times the <linear thermal expansion coefficient>, because $V\propto L^3$. Direction-dependent expansion requires the separate dimensional responses. \a[https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/3-20-materials-at-equilibrium-sma-5111-fall-2003/e402e3f0f555e15ff3af29b5b44b4e57_notes1.pdf][G. Ceder, MIT thermodynamics notes, section 1.14: linear and volumetric expansion]{ref}

= Coefficient of thermal expansion
{parent=Thermal expansion}
{title2=$\alpha=V^{-1}(\partial V/\partial T)_{P,N}$}

= Thermal expansion coefficient
{synonym}

The volumetric coefficient of <thermal expansion> is the fractional <volume (thermodynamics)> increase per <temperature> increase at fixed <pressure> and composition:
$$
\alpha=\frac1V\left(\frac{\partial V}{\partial T}\right)_{P,N}
=-\frac1\rho\left(\frac{\partial\rho}{\partial T}\right)_{P,N},\qquad
\rho=\frac mV.
$$
The <mass density> form requires fixed <mass> $m$. Its units are $\mathrm{K^{-1}}$; $\alpha_T$ is another notation for this coefficient. For small <temperature> changes $\theta$ at fixed <pressure>,
$$
V(T_0+\theta)\simeq V(T_0)(1+\alpha(T_0)\theta),\qquad
\rho(T_0+\theta)\simeq\rho(T_0)(1-\alpha(T_0)\theta).
$$
For a finite change in a single <thermodynamic phase>, integrate the possibly varying coefficient: $\ln(V_2/V_1)=\int_{T_1}^{T_2}\alpha(T,P,N)\,dT$. A constant-coefficient approximation gives an exponential, while the displayed linear formula additionally requires $|\alpha\theta|\ll1$.

For an <ideal gas> at fixed particle number,
$$
\alpha=\frac1T,\qquad
\alpha(300\,\mathrm K)=\frac1{300}\,\mathrm{K^{-1}}\simeq3.333\times10^{-3}\,\mathrm{K^{-1}}.
$$
Integrating $1/T$ recovers the exact fixed-<pressure> <thermal expansion> ratio $V_2/V_1=T_2/T_1$. Combining expansion with <isothermal compressibility> gives the local response of any smooth <simple compressible system> of fixed composition,
$$
\frac{dV}{V}=\alpha\,dT-\kappa_T\,dP,\qquad
\left(\frac{\partial P}{\partial T}\right)_{V,N}=\frac\alpha{\kappa_T}.
$$
The ratio has units $\mathrm{Pa\,K^{-1}}$. For the one-<mole> <ideal gas> state in <isothermal compressibility>, it is $P_0/T_0=333.33\,\mathrm{Pa\,K^{-1}}$: a $1\,\mathrm K$ increase at fixed <volume (thermodynamics)> raises the <pressure> by about $333.33\,\mathrm{Pa}$. <Maxwell relations> identify this same ratio as the <entropy> slope with respect to <volume (thermodynamics)> and give $C_P-C_V=TV\alpha^2/\kappa_T$.

Under the <Boussinesq approximation>, the small fractional <mass density> change produces <buoyancy> while <mass density> is otherwise treated as constant. A positive <temperature> excess $\theta$ gives <reduced gravity> $g'=g\alpha_T\theta$ in the convention used in <natural ventilation>. The coefficient therefore converts a <heat> budget into a buoyancy budget. The same measurable expansion response supplies <entropy> changes and <heat capacity> differences through <Maxwell relations>, connecting fluid motion to equilibrium <thermodynamics>.

= Linear thermal expansion coefficient
{parent=Thermal expansion}
{title2=$\alpha_L=L^{-1}(\partial L/\partial T)_P$}

The linear thermal expansion coefficient is the fractional change in a specified <length> per change in <temperature>, at fixed external <pressure> for a sample free to expand. For small changes, $\Delta L/L\simeq\alpha_L\Delta T$. An isotropic sample with the same response in all directions has volumetric <coefficient of thermal expansion> $\alpha=3\alpha_L$, since differentiating $V\propto L^3$ gives $dV/V=3\,dL/L$. Direction-dependent materials require a specified measurement direction. \a[https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-2/pages/1-3-thermal-expansion][OpenStax University Physics 2, §1.3: linear and volumetric expansion]{ref}.

= Stochastic thermodynamics
{parent=Thermodynamics}
{wiki}

Stochastic thermodynamics assigns work, heat, and entropy production to individual fluctuating trajectories while recovering ordinary thermodynamics after ensemble averaging.

= Local detailed balance
{parent=Stochastic thermodynamics}

Local detailed balance states that the logarithm of the probability ratio between a trajectory and its time reverse equals the entropy delivered to the environment in units of $k_B$. For one heat bath, $\log(P_F/P_B)=\Delta Q/(k_BT)$ when positive $\Delta Q$ flows into the bath.

= Microscopic reversibility
{parent=Local detailed balance}
{wiki}

For an equilibrium process, a path and its physically time-reversed path have equal probability after including their equilibrium starting weights. Conditional on their endpoints, this means
$$
\frac{P_F}{P_B}=\exp[-(F_2-F_1)/(k_BT)].
$$
This is the path version of <detailed balance>. A time-even <order parameter> is read backward without changing sign, while a transport flux changes sign. The <Onsager--Machlup path probability> implements this reversal in a coarse-grained stochastic description.

= Entropy production
{title2=$\Delta S_{\rm tot}$}
{parent=Stochastic thermodynamics}
{wiki}

Entropy production is the sum of the entropy changes of a system and its environment. Under local detailed balance it is represented by a forward-to-reversed path-probability logarithm and has nonnegative expectation.

= Thermodynamic entropy flux
{parent=Entropy production}
{title2=$j_\eta=q/\theta$}

Local equilibrium associates the outgoing <heat flux> $q$ with outgoing <entropy> flux $q/\theta$ at positive <temperature> $\theta$. Its <divergence> appears in the <Lagrangian entropy inequality>, with the appropriate nominal flux in reference coordinates.

= Fluctuation theorem
{parent=Entropy production}
{wiki}

A fluctuation theorem relates probabilities of positive and negative entropy production. In a steady-state large-deviation form, the rate function often obeys $I(s)-I(-s)=-s$ in units where $s$ is the entropy-production rate.

= Fluctuation-dissipation relation for a Langevin particle
{title2=$\sigma^2=2\zeta k_BT$}
{parent=Stochastic thermodynamics}

For a Langevin particle with drag coefficient $\zeta$ and white-force covariance amplitude $\sigma^2$, detailed balance at temperature $T$ requires $\sigma^2=2\zeta k_BT$.

= Vector noise normalization in inertial Langevin dynamics
{parent=Fluctuation-dissipation relation for a Langevin particle}
{title2=$\langle\mathbf A(t)\cdot\mathbf A(s)\rangle=(6\gamma k_BT/m^2)\delta(t-s)$}

Divide the inertial <Langevin equation> by mass and let $\zeta=\gamma/m$. An isotropic three-dimensional equilibrium velocity has $\langle|\mathbf u|^2\rangle=3k_BT/m$ by the <equipartition theorem>. Its white acceleration dot-covariance amplitude is therefore $6\zeta k_BT/m$, while the amplitude per Cartesian component is $2\zeta k_BT/m$. A scalar moment formula must use the component variance rather than the trace.

= Boltzmann constant
{title2=$k_B$}
{c}
{parent=Thermodynamics}
{wiki}

The Boltzmann constant converts temperature into an energy scale.

= Boltzmann distribution
{title2=$P_i=Z^{-1}e^{-\beta E_i}$}
{c}
{parent=Thermodynamics}
{wiki}

The <Boltzmann distribution> assigns <probabilities> to individual <microstates> in a <canonical ensemble>, a <statistical ensemble> for thermal contact. The system exchanges <energy> with a <thermal reservoir> at fixed positive absolute <temperature> $T$, while its particle number $N$ and external parameters, such as <volume>, stay fixed. The <thermal reservoir> is large enough that these exchanges barely change its <temperature>; the coupling permits <thermal equilibrium> but contributes negligible interaction <energy>. All the <microstates> considered have the same $N$. No <chemical potential> term is needed in their weights; fixed $N$ does not imply that the <chemical potential> is zero.

For a finite discrete set of <microstates> with <energies> $E_i$, write
$$
p_i=\frac{e^{-\beta E_i}}{Z},\qquad
\beta=\frac1{k_BT},\qquad
Z=\sum_i e^{-\beta E_i},\qquad
\sum_i p_i=1.
$$
Here $k_B$ is the <Boltzmann constant>, $\beta$ is the <inverse temperature>, $e^{-\beta E_i}$ is a <Boltzmann factor>, and $Z$ is the <partition function>. The <Boltzmann factor> is a weight; dividing by the <partition function> makes it a <probability>. \a[https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/5-62-physical-chemistry-ii-spring-2008/ec4f40d5a0fa41df3692f076e850543d_02_562ln08.pdf][MIT 5.62 Physical Chemistry II, spring 2008, lecture 2, pages 4–7: fixed N, V, T and canonical probabilities]{ref}

The useful surprise is that a fixed <temperature> still permits <thermal fluctuations> in the system's <energy>. The <Boltzmann distribution> predicts their <probabilities> and <expected values> without tracking every microscopic exchange. Its <exponential function> also makes rare excited <microstates> sensitive to <temperature>: an <energy> gap much larger than $k_BT$ gives strong <Boltzmann suppression>, while warming increases their relative populations. A population ratio can therefore reveal a gap or serve as a <thermometer>, provided <thermal equilibrium> and the relevant <state degeneracies> are known.

Historically, <Josiah Willard Gibbs>'s \i[Elementary Principles in Statistical Mechanics] (1902) presents the <canonical ensemble> in Chapter IV. His equations 90–92 give an <exponential function> of <energy> and its normalization, in classical <phase space>. This classical formulation exhibits the same weighting principle. \a[https://en.wikisource.org/wiki/Elementary_Principles_in_Statistical_Mechanics][Gibbs (1902), title page and preface]{ref} \a[https://en.wikisource.org/wiki/Elementary_Principles_in_Statistical_Mechanics/Chapter_IV][Gibbs (1902), Chapter IV, equations 90–92]{ref}

= Boltzmann distribution ratios and energy reference
{c}
{parent=Boltzmann distribution}
{title2=$p_i/p_j=e^{-\beta(E_i-E_j)}$}

For two individual <microstates>, the <partition function> cancels:
$$
\frac{p_i}{p_j}=e^{-\beta(E_i-E_j)}.
$$
At positive <temperature>, a higher-<energy> individual <microstate> is less probable, and equal-<energy> individual <microstates> are equally probable. For example, a gap $E_i-E_j=k_BT\ln2$, using the <natural logarithm>, gives $p_i/p_j=1/2$. This comparison concerns individual <microstates>; a whole <macrostate> can contain several of them.

Changing the reference for <energy> leaves every <probability> unchanged. If all <energies> become $E_i'=E_i+C$ for the same constant $C$, then
$$
Z'=e^{-\beta C}Z,\qquad
p_i'=\frac{e^{-\beta(E_i+C)}}{e^{-\beta C}Z}=p_i.
$$
The <partition function> itself changes, but its common factor cancels in the <Boltzmann distribution>. Thus choosing a <ground state> <energy> of zero simplifies calculations without changing populations. The <expected value> of <energy> shifts by $C$, as it must when its reference changes.

= Boltzmann distribution and state degeneracy
{c}
{parent=Boltzmann distribution}
{title2=$P(E)=g(E)e^{-\beta E}/Z$}

Group <microstates> by their <energy> $E$. The <state degeneracy> $g(E)$ counts how many <microstates> have that <energy>; their aggregate <probability> is
$$
P(E)=\sum_{i:E_i=E}p_i=\frac{g(E)e^{-\beta E}}{Z},\qquad
Z=\sum_{\text{distinct }E}g(E)e^{-\beta E}.
$$
Here $P(E)$ is the <probability> of the <macrostate> retaining only <energy>, while $p_i$ is the <probability> of one individual <microstate>. For two such <macrostates>,
$$
\frac{P(E_a)}{P(E_b)}=\frac{g(E_a)}{g(E_b)}e^{-\beta(E_a-E_b)}.
$$
Counting more <microstates> can compensate for a smaller <Boltzmann factor>. Omitting the <state degeneracy> confuses a sum over <microstates> with a sum over distinct <energies>. \a[https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/5-62-physical-chemistry-ii-spring-2008/bda82786b6014d3f468798339f658629_03_562ln08.pdf][MIT 5.62, spring 2008, lecture 3, page 4: degeneracy and the aggregate energy probability]{ref}

= Boltzmann distribution in a four-state system
{c}
{parent=Boltzmann distribution}
{title2=$Z=(1+q)^2$}

Consider two distinguishable, noninteracting <two-level systems>, each with <energies> $0$ and $\Delta>0$. Their four <microstates> $(0,0),(0,1),(1,0),(1,1)$ have total <energies> $0,\Delta,\Delta,2\Delta$. Thus the three <energy> <macrostates> have <state degeneracies> $1,2,1$. This is a stipulated finite model, not a measured material.

Set $q=e^{-\beta\Delta}$. The four individual <Boltzmann factors> are $1,q,q,q^2$, so the <partition function> and aggregate <probabilities> are
$$
Z=1+2q+q^2=(1+q)^2,\qquad
\bigl(P(0),P(\Delta),P(2\Delta)\bigr)=\frac{(1,2q,q^2)}{(1+q)^2}.
$$
Each of the two middle <microstates> has <probability> $q/Z$, while their shared <energy> <macrostate> has <probability> $2q/Z$.

At the <temperature> satisfying $\Delta=k_BT\ln2$, the <Boltzmann factor> is $q=1/2$ and the <partition function> is $Z=9/4$. Therefore
$$
\bigl(p_{00},p_{01},p_{10},p_{11}\bigr)=\left(\frac49,\frac29,\frac29,\frac19\right),\qquad
\bigl(P(0),P(\Delta),P(2\Delta)\bigr)=\left(\frac49,\frac49,\frac19\right).
$$
The middle <energy> is as likely as the <ground state> <energy> because its <state degeneracy> is twice as large. Yet each individual middle <microstate> is only half as likely as the <ground state> <microstate>. The diagram places the four <energies> beside their individual <probabilities>, then shows the sums within each <macrostate>.

\Image[boltzmann-microstates-and-degeneracy.png]
{title=Four <microstates> and three <energy> <macrostates> at a gap of $k_BT\ln2$}

The <expected value> of <energy> follows directly by weighting the three possible <energies>:
$$
\langle E\rangle=0\,P(0)+\Delta P(\Delta)+2\Delta P(2\Delta)
=\frac{2\Delta q+2\Delta q^2}{(1+q)^2}
=\frac{2\Delta q}{1+q}.
$$
At $q=1/2$ this is $2\Delta/3$. An individual <energy> measurement still returns $0$, $\Delta$ or $2\Delta$; the <expected value> describes the <ensemble average>, rather than a fourth possible measured <energy>.

= Boltzmann distribution temperature limits in a finite system
{c}
{parent=Boltzmann distribution}
{title2=$x=k_BT/\Delta$}

Keep the gap $\Delta$ fixed and vary positive <temperature>. With $x=k_BT/\Delta$, the <Boltzmann factor> is $q=e^{-1/x}$. In the four-<microstate> example, as $T\to0^+$, $q\to0$: the unique <ground state> has <probability> tending to one, and the other <microstates> have <probabilities> tending to zero. If several <microstates> instead shared the lowest <energy>, their <state degeneracy> would split that limiting population equally.

As $T\to\infty$ in this fixed finite model, all four individual <Boltzmann factors> tend to one, so all four individual <microstates> have <probability> tending to $1/4$. The three aggregate <probabilities> instead approach
$$
\bigl(P(0),P(\Delta),P(2\Delta)\bigr)\longrightarrow
\left(\frac14,\frac12,\frac14\right).
$$
The equal-population limit applies to this finite set of <microstates>. An infinite collection of <microstates> cannot all have the same positive normalized <probability>, so the statement cannot be extended to an unbounded <energy> spectrum. Real systems may also acquire additional accessible <microstates> as <temperature> rises.

\Image[boltzmann-populations-versus-temperature.png]
{title=Aggregate <Boltzmann distribution> populations versus positive <temperature> for <state degeneracies> 1, 2, 1}

The plotted crossing occurs at $x=1/\ln2$, where $P(0)=P(\Delta)=4/9$. For larger positive <temperatures>, the middle <macrostate> is more probable than the lowest-<energy> <macrostate>, although the lowest-<energy> individual <microstate> remains more probable than either individual middle <microstate>. The <expected value> of <energy> rises from zero toward $\Delta$ in this finite model.

= Statistical ensemble
{parent=Thermodynamics}
{wiki}

A <statistical ensemble> describes hypothetical copies of the same system under specified constraints, with a <probability distribution> over their <microstates>. An <ensemble average> is an <expected value> across those copies. The <canonical ensemble> fixes <temperature> and particle number while allowing <energy> exchange; the <microcanonical ensemble> fixes <energy>, and the <grand canonical ensemble> also allows particle exchange. These choices specify different physical preparations. \a[https://en.wikisource.org/wiki/Elementary_Principles_in_Statistical_Mechanics][Gibbs (1902), preface: ensembles of systems with different microscopic conditions]{ref}

= Temperature
{parent=Thermodynamics}
{wiki}

Temperature is the intensive variable thermodynamically conjugate to entropy; at equilibrium it determines the direction of heat transfer.

= Thermometer
{parent=Temperature}
{wiki}

A thermometer measures <temperature> through a calibrated physical response. Reaching <thermal equilibrium> with the object is essential for an equilibrium reading. A <gas thermometer> uses a gas's pressure or volume response.

= Gas thermometer
{parent=Thermometer}
{title2=$P\propto T\quad(N,V\text{ fixed})$}

In an ideal constant-volume gas thermometer, the <ideal gas law> $PV=Nk_BT$ makes pressure proportional to absolute <temperature> when the particle number and volume remain fixed. Thus $T/T_0=P/P_0$ against a reference state in the <ideal gas> approximation. The <Maxwell speed distribution> describes the microscopic thermal motion underlying this pressure.

= Kelvin
{parent=Temperature}
{title2=$\mathrm K$}

The kelvin is the SI unit of <thermodynamic temperature>, with symbol $\mathrm K$. Its definition fixes the numerical value of the <Boltzmann constant>; it is written without a degree sign.\a[https://www.bipm.org/en/si-base-units/kelvin][BIPM, SI unit kelvin]{ref}

= Degree Celsius
{parent=Temperature}
{title2=${}^\circ\mathrm C$}

The degree Celsius has the same size as the <kelvin>, with a shifted temperature origin: $t/{}^\circ\mathrm C=T/\mathrm K-273.15$. Thus a change of one degree Celsius is a change of one <kelvin>, but temperature ratios in <Carnot's theorem> must use absolute <thermodynamic temperature>.\a[https://www.bipm.org/documents/d/guest/si-brochure-9-2_01][BIPM, SI Brochure, temperature units]{ref}

= Inverse temperature
{parent=Temperature}
{title2=$\beta=1/(k_BT)$}

<Inverse temperature> is $\beta=1/(k_BT)$, where $k_B$ is the <Boltzmann constant> and $T$ is the absolute <temperature>. A <canonical ensemble> weights an energy $E$ by its <Boltzmann factor> $e^{-\beta E}$. Calling couplings dimensionless means absorbing this factor $\beta$ into their energy coefficients. The alternative convention $k_B=1$ makes <inverse temperature> $1/T$; physical and dimensionless couplings must not be mixed.

In a thermal <Euclidean worldline path integral> with $\hbar=1$, $\beta$ is also the circumference of the imaginary-time circle.

= Thermal equilibrium
{parent=Thermodynamics}
{wiki}

Two internally equilibrated systems are in thermal equilibrium if bringing them into contact that permits only <heat> exchange causes no net <heat transfer>. In ordinary nonrelativistic <thermodynamics> without appreciable gravitational temperature shifts, their <thermodynamic temperatures> are equal. Merely preventing <heat transfer> with insulation does not establish <thermal equilibrium>: two insulated bodies can have different <temperatures>. \a[https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-2/pages/1-1-temperature-and-thermal-equilibrium][Samuel J. Ling, William Moebs and Jeff Sanny, OpenStax University Physics Volume 2, section 1.1]{ref}

A <thermometer> illustrates the definition. Its own <temperature> settles to the object's <temperature> after thermal contact, provided its effect on the object is negligible. The existing <zeroth law of thermodynamics> makes this comparison transitive: two objects separately in <thermal equilibrium> with the same <thermometer> are in <thermal equilibrium> with each other. This lets one calibrated scale compare different materials.

Equal <temperatures> do not imply equal <internal energies> or equal <heat capacities>. Nor does <thermal equilibrium> by itself imply <mechanical equilibrium> or <chemical equilibrium>; these require additional allowed exchanges. There is also a distinction between no net exchange in <thermal equilibrium> and a constant <temperature> maintained by equal incoming and outgoing <heat>. The latter can be a <steady state> with ongoing <entropy production>. Microscopic exchanges can continue at <thermal equilibrium>, but their opposing contributions balance on average.

Historically, OpenStax credits the name “zeroth law” to Ralph Fowler in the 1930s: the already numbered laws needed a logically earlier statement about <thermal equilibrium>. \a[https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-2/pages/1-1-temperature-and-thermal-equilibrium][Ling, Moebs and Sanny, section 1.1: naming the zeroth law]{ref}

= Thermal equilibration of two finite bodies
{parent=Thermal equilibrium}
{title2=$T_f=(C_1T_{1,0}+C_2T_{2,0})/(C_1+C_2)$}

Consider two rigid bodies forming an <isolated system>, with fixed composition and constant whole-body <heat capacity at constant volume> values $C_1=1\,\mathrm{kJ\,K^{-1}}$ and $C_2=3\,\mathrm{kJ\,K^{-1}}$. Their initial <temperatures> are $T_{1,0}=300\,\mathrm K$ and $T_{2,0}=400\,\mathrm K$. Assume each body remains internally uniform, their contact stores negligible <energy>, no <phase transition> occurs and no <work> is done or extracted. These are stipulated model values, not material measurements.

Because $dU_i=C_i\,dT_i$, write $U_i=a_i+C_iT_i$ with arbitrary fixed reference constants $a_i$. The conserved relative <internal energy> is
$$
E=U_1+U_2-a_1-a_2=C_1T_1+C_2T_2=1500\,\mathrm{kJ}.
$$
This is an <energy> reference convention, not a claim about actual <heat capacities> down to zero <temperature>. At <thermal equilibrium> the two <temperatures> equal $T_f$, so the <first law of thermodynamics> gives
$$
T_f=\frac{C_1T_{1,0}+C_2T_{2,0}}{C_1+C_2}=375\,\mathrm K,\qquad
\Delta U_1=75\,\mathrm{kJ},\qquad \Delta U_2=-75\,\mathrm{kJ}.
$$
The larger <heat capacity> pulls the common <temperature> closer to its initial value: body 1 warms by $75\,\mathrm K$, while body 2 cools by $25\,\mathrm K$.

\Image[thermal-equilibrium-two-bodies.png]
{title=Two finite bodies approach <thermal equilibrium> at $375\,\mathrm K$}
{description=Ideal model with $C_1=1$ and $C_2=3\,\mathrm{kJ\,K^{-1}}$ and contact conductance $G=0.1\,\mathrm{kJ\,s^{-1}\,K^{-1}}$. The weighted <temperature> stays at $375\,\mathrm K$ and the relative <internal energy> stays at $1500\,\mathrm{kJ}$; the plotted curves are model predictions.}

To predict a timescale, stipulate a constant contact conductance $G=1/R_{\rm th}=0.1\,\mathrm{kJ\,s^{-1}\,K^{-1}}$, the reciprocal of the contact's <thermal resistance>. The rate of <heat transfer> into body 1 is $G(T_2-T_1)$, giving
$$
C_1\dot T_1=G(T_2-T_1),\qquad C_2\dot T_2=-G(T_2-T_1).
$$
Adding proves conservation of $E$. Subtracting gives <exponential decay> of the <temperature> difference, with <time constant>
$$
\tau=\frac{C_1C_2}{G(C_1+C_2)}=7.5\,\mathrm s,\qquad
T_2-T_1=100\,\mathrm K\,e^{-t/\tau},
$$
and therefore
$$
T_1(t)=375\,\mathrm K-75\,\mathrm K\,e^{-t/\tau},\qquad
T_2(t)=375\,\mathrm K+25\,\mathrm K\,e^{-t/\tau}.
$$
Exact equality is approached as $t\to\infty$ in this ideal model. Changing $G$ changes the rate but not $T_f$: equilibrium and the speed of reaching it are separate predictions. Real bodies can have internal <temperature gradients>, changing <heat capacities> and contact losses, so the plotted $7.5\,\mathrm s$ is an assumed model timescale.

= Tolman–Ehrenfest relation
{c}
{parent=Thermal equilibrium}
{title2=$T\sqrt{-k^2}=\mathrm{constant}$}

= Tolman temperature law
{c}
{synonym}

In static <thermal equilibrium>, the local temperature times the stationary lapse is constant. Energy measured locally redshifts to conserved Killing energy by that same lapse; requiring a single equilibrium Boltzmann exponent gives the relation. The <Killing vector field> normalization fixes the constant but not the ratio of temperatures at different locations.

= Boltzmann suppression
{parent=Thermal equilibrium}
{c}
{title2=$e^{-m/T}$}

<Boltzmann suppression> is the exponential reduction of an equilibrium population when creating it costs energy much greater than the temperature. For a nonrelativistic species with negligible <chemical potential>, the leading factor is $e^{-m/T}$, multiplied by its momentum phase-space volume. It concerns the equilibrium abundance; freeze-out can preserve a larger population than the instantaneous equilibrium value at later times.

= Boltzmann suppression of a nonrelativistic relic
{parent=Boltzmann suppression}
{c}
{title2=$n_{\rm eq}\simeq g(mT/2\pi)^{3/2}e^{-m/T}$}

A nonrelativistic species with $g$ states and negligible <chemical potential> has equilibrium density $g(mT/(2\pi))^{3/2}e^{-m/T}$. If particle-antiparticle annihilation maintains equilibrium after the species becomes massive, its eventual freeze-out population can be much smaller than a relativistically decoupled population. The nonrelativistic annihilation rate and any asymmetry determine the final abundance.

= Kinetic equilibrium
{parent=Thermal equilibrium}

Momentum-exchange collisions maintain a common <temperature> and an equilibrium-shaped momentum <probability distribution>. Particle abundances and <chemical potentials> need not have their <chemical equilibrium> values: elastic scattering alone conserves particle number. Thus kinetic equilibrium does not by itself imply either zero <chemical potential> or conserved comoving <entropy>.

= Heat bath
{parent=Thermal equilibrium}
{wiki}

= Thermal reservoir
{synonym}

A heat bath, or thermal reservoir, is an idealized system so large that exchanging a finite amount of <heat> does not appreciably change its <temperature>.

= Entropy maximization under thermal contact
{parent=Thermal equilibrium}
{title2=$\partial S_{\rm tot}/\partial E_1=1/T_1-1/T_2$}

At fixed <volumes> and material inventories, two internally equilibrated bodies that exchange only <heat> within an <isolated system> have fixed total <internal energy> $E=E_1+E_2$. Their total <entropy> is
$$
S_{\mathrm{tot}}(E_1)=S_1(E_1)+S_2(E-E_1).
$$
Using $1/T_i=(\partial S_i/\partial E_i)_{V_i,N_i}$, an interior stationary point obeys
$$
\frac{dS_{\rm tot}}{dE_1}=\frac1{T_1}-\frac1{T_2}=0.
$$
Thus <entropy> stationarity requires matching <thermodynamic temperatures>. Positive values of <heat capacity at constant volume> make this a strict local maximum because
$$
\frac{d^2S_{\rm tot}}{dE_1^2}=-\frac1{C_1T_1^2}-\frac1{C_2T_2^2}<0.
$$
For bodies with positive <heat capacities> throughout the accessible range, this <concavity> gives at most one interior maximum. This is the stability check behind the equal-<temperature> condition; see <entropy concavity and positive heat capacity>. \a[https://davidtong.org/pdfs/teaching/statistical-physics/statphys1.pdf][David Tong, Statistical Physics, sections 1.2.1–1.2.2: entropy maximization, temperature and heat capacity]{ref}

For <thermal equilibration of two finite bodies>, <entropy from heat capacity> gives
$$
\Delta S_{\rm tot}=C_1\ln\frac{T_f}{T_{1,0}}+C_2\ln\frac{T_f}{T_{2,0}}
=\left(\ln\frac{375}{300}+3\ln\frac{375}{400}\right)\mathrm{kJ\,K^{-1}}
\simeq0.02952799\,\mathrm{kJ\,K^{-1}}>0.
$$
Body 1 gains about $0.22314355\,\mathrm{kJ\,K^{-1}}$ of <entropy>, while body 2 loses about $0.19361556\,\mathrm{kJ\,K^{-1}}$. Total <energy> is conserved even though total <entropy> increases. The <entropy> calculation uses reversible comparison paths between the endpoint states; actual exchange across a finite <temperature> difference is irreversible.

\Image[thermal-equilibrium-entropy-maximum.png]
{title=At fixed total <energy>, matching <temperatures> maximize total <entropy>}
{description=The curve is total <entropy> relative to the initial $300\,\mathrm K$, $400\,\mathrm K$ state. Every point satisfies $C_1T_1+C_2T_2=1500\,\mathrm{kJ}$. Only $250\le T_1/\mathrm K\le500$ is shown; the mathematical model's positive-<temperature> domain is $0<T_1/\mathrm K<1500$.}

To construct the plot, vary $T_1$ while enforcing the <energy> constraint, rather than holding $T_2$ fixed. In numerical units of <kelvin>, $T_2/\mathrm K=(1500-T_1/\mathrm K)/3$, and the plotted function is
$$
\mathcal S(T_1)=C_1\ln\frac{T_1}{300\,\mathrm K}+C_2\ln\frac{T_2(T_1)}{400\,\mathrm K},\qquad
\frac{dT_2}{dT_1}=-\frac{C_1}{C_2}.
$$
Here $\mathcal S$ means $S_{\rm tot}-S_{\rm tot,initial}$, so its zero is a reference choice. The <chain rule> yields
$$
\mathcal S'(T_1)=\frac{C_1}{T_1}-\frac{C_1}{T_2},\qquad
\mathcal S''(T_1)=-\frac{C_1}{T_1^2}-\frac{C_1^2}{C_2T_2^2}<0.
$$
The unique maximum is at $T_1=T_2=375\,\mathrm K$. The physical trajectory starts at $T_1=300\,\mathrm K$ and moves toward that maximum; the rest of the curve compares other possible <energy> partitions, not later times in the trajectory.

The contact model also proves the direction of change directly:
$$
\dot S_{\rm tot}=G(T_2-T_1)\left(\frac1{T_1}-\frac1{T_2}\right)
=\frac{G(T_2-T_1)^2}{T_1T_2}\ge0.
$$
For $G>0$ and positive <temperatures>, equality holds exactly at <thermal equilibrium>. The appealing link is that a dynamical <heat transfer> law moves the system uphill on the constrained <entropy> curve, while conservation of <energy> keeps it on that curve. This predicts both why the endpoint is stable and why the reverse separation into hot and cold bodies cannot happen spontaneously in the <isolated system>.

= Entropy
{parent=Thermodynamics}
{title2=$S$}
{wiki}

Entropy is an <extensive quantity> and a <state function> of an equilibrium system, measured in $\mathrm{J\,K^{-1}}$. Differences between equilibrium states are calculated along a <reversible thermodynamic process>:
$$
S_2-S_1=\int_1^2\frac{\delta Q_{\rm rev}}{T}.
$$
Here <heat> entering the system is positive and $T$ is its absolute <thermodynamic temperature>. The reversible path used for calculation need not be the path actually taken. Dividing the actual <heat> by a single <temperature> generally fails for an irreversible process.

For example, put two rigid bodies, each with constant <heat capacity> $C=100\,\mathrm{J\,K^{-1}}$, at $600\,\mathrm K$ and $300\,\mathrm K$ in an otherwise <isolated system>. Assume each body has a uniform internal <temperature>, no <phase transition> occurs, and no <work> is extracted. Their final <thermal equilibrium> is at $450\,\mathrm K$, because the <internal energy> lost by one equals that gained by the other. Using <entropy from heat capacity>,
$$
\Delta S_h=C\log(450/600)=-28.77\,\mathrm{J\,K^{-1}},\qquad
\Delta S_c=C\log(450/300)=40.55\,\mathrm{J\,K^{-1}},
$$
so $\Delta S_{\rm tot}=C\log(9/8)=11.78\,\mathrm{J\,K^{-1}}$. One body's <entropy> decreases while the total increases. \a[https://web.mit.edu/16.unified/www/FALL/thermodynamics/notes/node41.html][MIT, Entropy changes and the hot-brick problem]{ref}

\Image[heat-exchange-entropy.png]
{title=Thermal equilibration of two finite bodies conserves energy and increases total entropy}

In <statistical mechanics>, <Boltzmann's entropy formula> counts equally likely <microstates> compatible with a <macrostate>; <Gibbs entropy> handles a specified <probability distribution> over <microstates>. If the number of equally likely <microstates> doubles, <entropy> increases by $k_B\log2$. The <logarithm> makes multiplicative counts additive for independent systems. These are precise statements about states and probabilities, not a universal measure of visible messiness. Calling <entropy> merely "disorder" obscures the thermal calculation above and hides which <microstates> are being counted. \a[https://web.mit.edu/16.unified/www/FALL/thermodynamics/notes/node57.html][MIT, Statistical entropy and randomness]{ref}

The usefulness of <entropy> is that measurable <heat capacities> determine its changes, and the <second law of thermodynamics> turns those changes into constraints on processes. It also connects macroscopic measurements to microscopic descriptions without requiring us to track every particle.

= Specific entropy
{title2=$s=S/M$}
{parent=Entropy}

Specific entropy is <entropy> per unit mass. For a <perfect gas> with constant <specific heat capacity> $c_v$ at constant volume and <specific-heat ratio> $\gamma$, differences obey
$$
s_2-s_1=c_v\log\left[\frac{p_2}{p_1}\left(\frac{\rho_1}{\rho_2}\right)^\gamma\right],
$$
where $p$ is <pressure> and $\rho$ is <mass density>. Thus a <homentropic flow> has constant $p/\rho^\gamma$.

= Boltzmann's entropy formula
{title2=$S=k_B\log\Omega$}
{c}
{parent=Entropy}
{wiki}

= Boltzmann entropy
{c}
{synonym}

Why does a <gas> spread through an available container? One part of the answer is counting: a nearly uniform <macrostate> can be realized by vastly more <microstates> than a <macrostate> with every particle crowded into one corner. <Boltzmann's entropy formula> turns that multiplicity into <entropy>. The useful insight is that a large <entropy> measures how many microscopic realizations fit a specified macroscopic description, rather than how untidy a picture looks.

For a specified <macrostate> $M$ containing $\Omega(M)$ accessible, equally likely <microstates>, write
$$
S(M)=k_B\ln\Omega(M),\qquad p_i=\frac1{\Omega(M)}\quad(i\text{ compatible with }M).
$$
Here $k_B$ is the <Boltzmann constant>, and $\ln$ is the <natural logarithm>, also written $\log$ in this article's title. The count $\Omega(M)$ is dimensionless; <entropy> has units of <energy> divided by <temperature>. Always specify the <macrostate>, the constraints and the model's microscopic resolution before counting. A <microcanonical ensemble> provides a familiar equilibrium setting for the equal-<probability> assumption. \a[https://web.mit.edu/16.unified/www/FALL/thermodynamics/notes/node57.html][MIT, §7.4: equiprobable microstates and S = k ln Ω]{ref}

The <natural logarithm> compresses an enormous count into a manageable number and makes products of counts into sums of <entropies>; see <additivity of Boltzmann entropy>. With this counting convention, one allowed <microstate> has zero <Boltzmann entropy>. This zero refers to the chosen model and resolution; it does not by itself assign an absolute <entropy> to a real material.

= Boltzmann entropy of a two-box occupancy
{c}
{parent=Boltzmann's entropy formula}
{title2=$S(k)=k_B\ln\binom Nk$}

Take $N=20$ distinguishable, labeled particles and two boxes of equal <volume>. Retain only whether each particle is in the left or right box: use a <bit> $0$ for left and $1$ for right. Each length-20 string is one coarse <microstate> of this toy model. Assume the labels make <independent> side choices with equal <probability>, so the $2^{20}=1\,048\,576$ strings are equiprobable. Equal <volumes> motivate these weights for noninteracting particles with no preference for either box; equal <volumes> alone would not establish them for an arbitrary interacting <gas>.

Now retain only the number $k$ in the left box as the <macrostate>. Choosing which $k$ labels are left gives a <binomial coefficient>, so
$$
\Omega(k)=\binom{20}{k}=\frac{20!}{k!(20-k)!},\qquad
S(k)=k_B\ln\binom{20}{k},\qquad
P(k)=\frac{\binom{20}{k}}{2^{20}}.
$$
The <factorials> count assignments to labels; the <probability> of a <macrostate> sums the <probabilities> of its constituent <microstates>. This is the <binomial distribution> with parameters $20$ and $1/2$. The same <binomial coefficient> appears in the counting of distinguishable <two-level systems>, although here the <macrostate> records occupancy rather than <energy>. \a[https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/8-333-statistical-mechanics-i-statistical-mechanics-of-particles-fall-2013/0cb99318c2ed889316cdcff839759e8d_MIT8_333F13_Lec12.pdf][Mehran Kardar, MIT 8.333 (2013), lecture 12, §IV.C, equations IV.17–18: binomial microstate counts]{ref}

The largest <binomial coefficient> occurs at $k=10$, the balanced <macrostate>. All-left means $k=20$ and supplies just one string. Thus
$$
\begin{aligned}
\Omega(10)&=184\,756,& S(10)/k_B&=\ln184\,756\approx12.126791,\\
\Omega(20)&=1,& S(20)/k_B&=0,\\
P(10)&\approx0.176197,& P(20)&=2^{-20}\approx9.53674\times10^{-7}.
\end{aligned}
$$
There are $184\,756$ balanced strings for every all-left string, even though each individual string has the same <probability>. Exact balance has about $17.6\%$ of the total <probability>; equilibrium reasoning concerns the many <macrostates> near balance too, rather than certainty of exactly ten particles on each side. The diagram compares the counts with their <natural logarithms>.

\Image[boltzmann-entropy-binomial-macrostates.png]
{title=Counts and Boltzmann entropies of the occupancy macrostates of twenty labeled particles}

Four fair, <independent> coins give a smaller worked example with the same <binomial distribution>. If $k$ is the number of heads, the five <macrostates> $k=0,1,2,3,4$ contain respectively $1,4,6,4,1$ <microstates>. Their <probabilities> are respectively $1/16,4/16,6/16,4/16,1/16$. In particular, two heads is one <macrostate> containing six ordered coin sequences, with <Boltzmann entropy> $k_B\ln6$ in this counting analogy. A prescribed ordered sequence has one realization. The next diagram enumerates the same sixteen strings as left/right assignments.

= Boltzmann entropy when a restriction is removed
{c}
{parent=Boltzmann's entropy formula}
{title2=$\Delta S=k_B\ln(\Omega_f/\Omega_i)$}

In <Boltzmann entropy of a two-box occupancy>, impose an initial restriction that all labels are left. There is one allowed coarse <microstate>. Remove that restriction, allowing all occupancy values: the allowed set is now the union of every occupancy <macrostate>, containing $2^N$ strings. If the final description assigns equal <probability> to this whole set, its <Boltzmann entropy> change is
$$
\Omega_i=1,\qquad \Omega_f=\sum_{k=0}^N\binom Nk=2^N,\qquad
\Delta S=k_B\ln\frac{\Omega_f}{\Omega_i}=Nk_B\ln2.
$$
The counting identity is the <binomial theorem>. For twenty labels, $\Delta S=20k_B\ln2\approx13.862944k_B$. This final description fixes neither box occupancy; it is coarser than the particular <macrostate> $k=10$, whose <Boltzmann entropy> is $k_B\ln184\,756$. The two counts answer different questions, so substituting the balanced <macrostate> for the full allowed set would change the calculation.

For four labels, the initial all-left string is `0000`; removing the restriction makes all sixteen strings available. The $k=2$ column has only six strings. Accordingly, the whole allowed set has <Boltzmann entropy> $k_B\ln16=4k_B\ln2$, while the balanced <macrostate> has <Boltzmann entropy> $k_B\ln6$.

\Image[entropy-two-box-configuration-count.png]
{title=Sixteen configurations of four labeled particles grouped by left occupancy, compared with the all-left restriction}

Removing a barrier changes the allowed set immediately, but it does not instantaneously randomize the actual <probability distribution>. Motion and equilibration must make those configurations accessible. The counting result illustrates the configurational contribution to <free expansion>; it does not describe the relaxation time or every contribution to the <entropy> of a real <gas>.

= Additivity of Boltzmann entropy
{parent=Boltzmann's entropy formula}
{title2=$\Omega_{AB}=\Omega_A\Omega_B,\quad S_{AB}=S_A+S_B$}

For two subsystems with independently specified <macrostates>, suppose every allowed <microstate> of $A$ can be paired with every allowed <microstate> of $B$. Their allowed set is a <Cartesian product>. With equal <probability> for each pair, <Boltzmann's entropy formula> gives
$$
\Omega_{AB}=\Omega_A\Omega_B,\qquad
S_{AB}=k_B\ln(\Omega_A\Omega_B)=S_A+S_B.
$$
For example, take two <independent> copies of the toy model, each with two labels choosing between its two boxes. Each copy has four configurations and <Boltzmann entropy> $k_B\ln4$. Combining their <independent> labels gives sixteen configurations and <Boltzmann entropy> $k_B\ln16=2k_B\ln4$. This explains why <entropy> adds even though microscopic counts multiply. \a[https://web.mit.edu/16.unified/www/FALL/thermodynamics/notes/node57.html][MIT, §7.4, equation 7.17: independent state counts multiply]{ref}

The product assumption matters. A constraint shared between the subsystems can forbid some pairs; for example, fixing only their combined <energy> generally requires summing over its possible divisions. See <entropy maximization under thermal contact> for how <thermal equilibrium> selects the dominant division. Statistical <independence> cannot be inferred merely from drawing a boundary between two parts.

= Scope of Boltzmann's entropy formula
{parent=Boltzmann's entropy formula}

The strings in <Boltzmann entropy of a two-box occupancy> are complete <microstates> only within the binary toy model. They retain a <coarse-grained variable> for each particle and omit its exact <position> within a box and its <momentum>. An actual classical <gas> instead occupies continuous <phase space>; obtaining a dimensionless count from a <phase space> volume requires a specified cell convention and the appropriate treatment of particle identity. A <quantum state> count must respect the allowed <quantum states> and <quantum statistics>. Thus $2^N$ is not a general count of <microstates> for a <gas> obeying <quantum ideal-gas statistics>. \a[https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/8-333-statistical-mechanics-i-statistical-mechanics-of-particles-fall-2013/0cdb670a825c6aaea7e229ac40776cbf_MIT8_333F13_Lec13.pdf][Kardar, MIT 8.333 (2013), lecture 13, §§IV.D–E: gas phase space and particle counting]{ref}

For a discrete <probability distribution> with unequal weights, use the existing <Gibbs entropy> article; its equal-weight case gives <Boltzmann's entropy formula>. <Shannon entropy> uses the corresponding information measure, with the logarithm base setting its units. A count alone cannot replace the specified <probability distribution>, as the <Boltzmann distribution> examples demonstrate. \a[https://web.mit.edu/16.unified/www/FALL/thermodynamics/notes/node56.html][MIT, §7.3: statistical entropy for general microstate probabilities]{ref}

Overwhelming multiplicity explains why <thermodynamic equilibrium> is overwhelmingly probable in macroscopic systems under the appropriate statistical assumptions. It does not make low-<entropy> <thermal fluctuations> logically impossible. Nor does counting alone derive irreversible macroscopic evolution: one also needs dynamics, an initial preparation, and a reason the system samples the allowed <microstates> on the timescale of interest. \a[https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/8-333-statistical-mechanics-i-statistical-mechanics-of-particles-fall-2013/0cb99318c2ed889316cdcff839759e8d_MIT8_333F13_Lec12.pdf][Kardar, lecture 12, §§IV.A–B: statistical equilibrium and the limits of counting arguments]{ref}

Historically, Boltzmann's 1877 paper counted microscopic arrangements, called complexions, and related the <natural logarithm> of their number to <entropy>. The Sharp and Matschinsky translation appeared in \i[Entropy] 17 (2015), pages 1971–2009, DOI 10.3390/e17041971. Its notation differs from ours: Boltzmann's permutability measure $\Omega$ is already logarithmic, whereas here $\Omega$ is the count. \a[https://gilles.montambaux.com/files/histoire-physique/Boltzmann-1877-entropy.pdf][Boltzmann (1877), translated by Kim Sharp and Franz Matschinsky (2015), §III after equation 34 and §V]{ref} <Josiah Willard Gibbs>'s \i[Elementary Principles in Statistical Mechanics] (1902) developed the <statistical ensemble> framework; Chapter XIV compares several statistical definitions of <entropy> with <thermodynamics>. \a[https://en.wikisource.org/wiki/Elementary_Principles_in_Statistical_Mechanics][Gibbs (1902), title page and preface]{ref} \a[https://en.wikisource.org/wiki/Elementary_Principles_in_Statistical_Mechanics/Chapter_XIV][Gibbs (1902), Chapter XIV: discussion of thermodynamic analogies]{ref}

= Entropy density
{title2=$s$}
{parent=Entropy}
{wiki}

For a homogeneous equilibrium system with <energy density> $\rho$, <pressure> $P$, <chemical potential> $\mu$, and <number density> $n$, the entropy density is
$$
s=\frac{\rho+P-\mu n}{T}.
$$

= Comoving entropy
{parent=Entropy density}
{title2=$S_c=a^3s$}

Comoving entropy is the <entropy density> multiplied by the physical volume factor $a^3$ for a fixed comoving region in an expanding homogeneous universe. In adiabatic expansion it is conserved. Separate decoupled radiation sectors conserve their own comoving entropy, so disappearance of massive species can heat the still-equilibrated sector relative to a decoupled species.

= Entropy density at zero chemical potential
{parent=Entropy density}
{title2=$s=(\rho+P)/T$}

For an extensive equilibrium system, the <thermodynamic Euler relation> gives $E=TS-PV+\mu N$. At zero <chemical potential>, divide by $V$ to obtain $s=(\rho+P)/T$. Equivalently, $dP=(\rho+P)dT/T$ makes
$$
d\left(\frac{(\rho+P)V}{T}\right)=\frac{d(\rho V)+P\,dV}{T}.
$$
The <first law of thermodynamics> identifies this as $dS$; the <thermodynamic Euler relation> fixes the entropy normalization. For adiabatic expansion satisfying the <cosmological perfect-fluid continuity equation>, the numerator on the right vanishes, proving <cosmological entropy conservation>.

= Nonrelativistic entropy at zero chemical potential
{parent=Entropy density at zero chemical potential}
{title2=$s\sim g m^3(2\pi)^{-3/2}x^{-1/2}e^{-x}$}

For a dilute nonrelativistic gas with $x=m/T\gg1$ and zero <chemical potential>, the <Bose-Einstein distribution> approaches the <Maxwell-Boltzmann distribution>. Expanding $E=m+p^2/(2m)+\cdots$ in the <number density> integral gives $n\sim g(mT/(2\pi))^{3/2}e^{-x}$. Since $\rho=mn+3nT/2+\cdots$ and $P=nT$, the <entropy density at zero chemical potential> gives $s=n(x+5/2+\cdots)$ and hence the displayed leading expression. This includes the internal multiplicity $g$; a single real <scalar field> has $g=1$.

= Second law of thermodynamics
{c}
{parent=Thermodynamics}
{wiki}

For an <isolated system>, the second law requires $\Delta S\ge0$. More generally, include all affected surroundings to obtain
$$
\Delta S_{\rm tot}=\Delta S_{\rm system}+\Delta S_{\rm surroundings}\ge0.
$$
Equality describes the reversible limit; irreversible macroscopic processes produce <entropy>. A <closed system> can lose <entropy> by exporting <heat>, so nondecrease does not apply to that system alone. The <Clausius statement of the second law> forbids a cyclic device whose sole effect is transferring <heat> from cold to hot. The <Kelvin-Planck statement of the second law> forbids a cyclic device whose sole effect is converting <heat> from one <thermal reservoir> completely into <work>. "Sole effect" matters: a <refrigerator> moves <heat> from cold to hot by consuming <work>. \a[https://web.mit.edu/16.unified/www/FALL/thermodynamics/notes/node37.html][MIT, Statements of the second law]{ref}

For direct transfer of $Q=1000\,\mathrm J$ from a <thermal reservoir> at $600\,\mathrm K$ to one at $300\,\mathrm K$, with no <work> extracted,
$$
\Delta S_{\rm tot}=-\frac{1000}{600}+\frac{1000}{300}
=\frac53\,\mathrm{J\,K^{-1}}>0.
$$
The reverse transfer alone would have negative total <entropy> change. Both directions conserve <energy>, but only the hot-to-cold direction is spontaneous. The finite-body example under <entropy> replaces these constant <temperatures> by changing ones.

<Friction>, <free expansion> and <heat transfer> across a finite <temperature> difference all introduce irreversibility. Moving arbitrarily slowly is insufficient if <friction> remains. A <reversible thermodynamic process> must permit restoration of both the system and surroundings. This gives engineers a useful ideal benchmark: irreversibility spends an opportunity to obtain <work>, even though <energy> itself is conserved. \a[https://www.ocw.mit.edu/ans7870/16/16.unified/thermoF03/chapter_7.htm][MIT, Irreversibility and lost opportunities for work]{ref}

= Reversible thermodynamic process
{parent=Second law of thermodynamics}
{title2=$dS=\delta Q_{\rm rev}/T$}

A thermodynamically reversible process can be reversed without a net change of the system and surroundings. Its entropy change equals reversible heat transfer divided by temperature. A reversible <adiabatic process> conserves <entropy>; adiabaticity alone does not forbid internal entropy production in an irreversible evolution.

= Clausius statement of the second law
{c}
{parent=Second law of thermodynamics}

No cyclic device can have as its sole effect the transfer of heat from a colder reservoir to a hotter reservoir.

= Kelvin-Planck statement of the second law
{c}
{parent=Second law of thermodynamics}

No cyclic device can have as its sole effect the extraction of heat from one reservoir and its complete conversion into work.

= Carnot's theorem
{c}
{parent=Second law of thermodynamics}
{title2=$\eta\le1-T_c/T_h$}
{wiki=Carnot's_theorem_(thermodynamics)}

= Carnot theorem
{c}
{synonym}

No cyclic <heat engine> exchanging <heat> only with two fixed <thermal reservoirs> at positive absolute <temperatures> $T_h>T_c$ can exceed the <thermal efficiency> of an engine whose cycle is a <reversible thermodynamic process> between them. All such reversible engines have the same <thermal efficiency>, regardless of their working substance. Let $Q_h>0$ be <heat> absorbed from the hot <thermal reservoir> and $Q_c>0$ the magnitude rejected to the cold one. Then
$$
W=Q_h-Q_c,\qquad
\eta=\frac W{Q_h}\le\eta_C=1-\frac{T_c}{T_h}.
$$
The <work> convention is the one in the <first law of thermodynamics>: $W>0$ is net output by the engine. Use absolute <thermodynamic temperatures>, such as values in <kelvin>; ratios of readings in <degree Celsius> are invalid because their zero is shifted.

For $T_h=600\,\mathrm K$, $T_c=300\,\mathrm K$ and $Q_h=1000\,\mathrm J$, the maximum <thermal efficiency> is $50\%$. A reversible engine delivers $W=500\,\mathrm J$ and rejects $Q_c=500\,\mathrm J$. The <thermal reservoirs> have <entropy> changes $-1000/600$ and $+500/300\,\mathrm{J\,K^{-1}}$, which cancel. The engine's own <entropy> change is zero over its <thermodynamic cycle>.

\Image[carnot-engine-efficiency.png]
{title=Carnot engine energy transfers and the reversible cycle between 600 K and 300 K}

A short derivation now becomes transparent: the <second law of thermodynamics> gives $-Q_h/T_h+Q_c/T_c\ge0$, and the <first law of thermodynamics> gives $W=Q_h-Q_c$. Together they yield the bound and, more precisely,
$$
W=Q_h\left(1-\frac{T_c}{T_h}\right)-T_c\Delta S_{\rm tot}.
$$
At fixed $Q_h,T_h,T_c$, every extra unit of total <entropy production> reduces output by $T_c$ units of <energy>. \a[https://web.mit.edu/16.unified/www/FALL/thermodynamics/notes/node44.html][MIT, Maximum heat-engine work]{ref}

Fixed <thermal reservoirs> are essential. Finite bodies cool and warm, so their initial <temperature> ratio cannot be used throughout an entire extraction process. As a computed contrast, the bodies with equal constant <heat capacity> in the <entropy> example would reach $T_f=\sqrt{600\times300}=424.26\,\mathrm K$ if a reversible engine extracted <work> until their <temperatures> matched. Zero total <entropy> change gives this geometric mean; the <first law of thermodynamics> then gives $W_{\max}=C(600+300-2T_f)=5147.19\,\mathrm J$. Direct contact reaches $450\,\mathrm K$ and extracts no <work>.

Carnot's 1824 book asked whether the useful output of machines driven by <heat> was fundamentally limited and whether changing the working substance could improve that limit. Its reversible comparisons are the historical setting of <Carnot's theorem>. Carnot used the then-current caloric picture, treating <heat> as conserved in its passage through an engine; the modern balance $Q_h-Q_c=W$ should not be read back into that text. \a[https://www.e-rara.ch/zut/doi/10.3931/e-rara-9118][Carnot, Réflexions sur la puissance motrice du feu, 1824, pp. 6–7 and 20–23]{ref}

= Thermodynamic temperature
{parent=Carnot's theorem}
{wiki}

Absolute thermodynamic temperature can be defined so that a reversible engine exchanging heats $Q_h$ and $Q_c$ with reservoirs at $T_h$ and $T_c$ satisfies
$$
\frac{Q_c}{Q_h}=\frac{T_c}{T_h},
\qquad
\eta=1-\frac{T_c}{T_h}.
$$
A choice of one reference temperature fixes the scale.

= Carnot cycle
{c}
{parent=Carnot's theorem}
{title2=$W=(T_h-T_c)\Delta S$}
{wiki}

A Carnot cycle realizes the reversible bound in <Carnot's theorem>. Its four steps for an <ideal gas> working substance are:

* Reversible <isothermal expansion> at $T_h$, absorbing $Q_h$ and increasing <entropy> by $\Delta S$.
* Reversible <adiabatic expansion>, doing <work> while the <temperature> falls from $T_h$ to $T_c$ and <entropy> stays fixed.
* Reversible <isothermal compression> at $T_c$, rejecting $Q_c$ and decreasing <entropy> by $\Delta S$.
* Reversible <adiabatic compression>, requiring <work> to restore $T_h$ at fixed <entropy>.

The <temperature> versus <entropy> plot in the <Carnot's theorem> figure is therefore a rectangle traversed clockwise. Because $\delta Q_{\rm rev}=T\,dS$, its width is $\Delta S=Q_h/T_h$. For the illustrated engine, $\Delta S=5/3\,\mathrm{J\,K^{-1}}$: the upper step absorbs $600\times5/3=1000\,\mathrm J$, the lower step rejects $300\times5/3=500\,\mathrm J$, and the enclosed area is the $500\,\mathrm J$ net <work>. Geometry displays the <thermal efficiency> without solving the gas motion. The two vertical steps are <isentropic processes>, not constant-<temperature> steps. \a[https://web.mit.edu/16.unified/www/FALL/thermodynamics/notes/node24.html][MIT, Four steps of a Carnot cycle]{ref} \a[https://web.mit.edu/16.unified/www/FALL/thermodynamics/notes/node46.html][MIT, Temperature-entropy diagrams]{ref}

Reversible <heat transfer> is a limit of vanishing <temperature> differences; ordinary finite-rate transfer introduces <entropy production>. The <Carnot cycle> is a performance benchmark rather than a guarantee of useful <power>. Running it backward gives the ideal operation of a <refrigerator> or <heat pump>, with <work> supplied to move <heat> toward the hotter <thermal reservoir>. \a[https://web.mit.edu/16.unified/www/FALL/thermodynamics/notes/node25.html][MIT, Reversed cycles and refrigeration]{ref}

= Isothermal process
{parent=Carnot cycle}
{wiki}

An isothermal process is a thermodynamic change at constant <temperature>. In a reversible isothermal step, the working substance remains in thermal equilibrium with a reservoir while exchanging <heat>.

= Boyle's law
{c}
{parent=Isothermal process}
{title2=$pV=\mathrm{constant}$}
{wiki}

= Boyle law
{c}
{synonym}

For a fixed amount of an ideal gas at constant temperature, pressure times volume is constant. Equivalently its density is proportional to pressure. In a dilute bubbly flow, this allows bubble volume and its contribution to <buoyancy> to increase as hydrostatic pressure decreases, provided gas mass is retained and dissolution is negligible.

= Isothermal compression
{parent=Isothermal process}

= Isothermal expansion
{parent=Isothermal process}

= Heat
{parent=Thermodynamics}
{title2=$Q$}
{wiki}

Heat is <energy> transferred across a <thermodynamic system>'s boundary because of a <temperature> difference. It is measured in <joules>, like <work> and <internal energy>. A hot cup transfers <heat> to cooler surroundings; its <temperature> describes its state, while the transferred <heat> describes a process. The cup stores <internal energy>, rather than a substance called <heat>. \a[https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-2/pages/1-4-heat-transfer-specific-heat-and-calorimetry][Ling, Moebs and Sanny, OpenStax, University Physics Volume 2, section 1.4: heat and internal energy]{ref}

For a <closed system> with unchanged bulk <kinetic energy> and <potential energy>, choose $Q>0$ for <heat> entering and $W>0$ for <work> done by the system. The <first law of thermodynamics> gives
$$
\Delta U=Q-W,\qquad dU=\delta Q-\delta W.
$$
Here $U$ is the stored <internal energy>. For example, a sealed piston cylinder receiving $20\,\mathrm{kJ}$ of <heat> and delivering $5\,\mathrm{kJ}$ of <work> gains $15\,\mathrm{kJ}$ of <internal energy>. A <closed system> retains its matter while exchanging <energy>; it need not be an <isolated system>. \a[https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-2/pages/3-3-first-law-of-thermodynamics][Ling, Moebs and Sanny, OpenStax, First law of thermodynamics and sign conventions]{ref}

\Image[heat-work-internal-energy-balance.png]
{title=<Heat> input and <work> output determine the <internal energy> increase}
{description=The dashed boundary encloses a <closed system> in a sealed piston cylinder. The incoming arrow carries $Q=20\,\mathrm{kJ}$ of <heat>; the outgoing arrow carries $W=5\,\mathrm{kJ}$ of <work> done by the system. With unchanged bulk <kinetic energy> and <potential energy>, $\Delta U=15\,\mathrm{kJ}$. No matter crosses the boundary.}

Unlike <internal energy>, <heat> is not a <state function>: different paths between the same equilibrium states can transfer different $Q$, with compensating differences in <work>. The symbol $\delta Q$ records this dependence on the path; there is no stored quantity whose difference is always $Q$. The two paths in <first law of thermodynamics> illustrate this directly. Supplied <heat> can raise <temperature> as <sensible heat>, change a <phase fraction> through <latent heat>, or help supply expansion <work>. Measuring <temperature> alone therefore does not determine transferred <heat>.

For a <reversible thermodynamic process>, $\delta Q_{\rm rev}=T\,dS$, where $T$ is absolute <temperature> and $S$ is <entropy>. This identification requires reversibility: an irreversible <adiabatic process> has $Q=0$ and can nevertheless increase <entropy>. Likewise $Q=\Delta H$, with <enthalpy> $H$, requires the particular fixed-<pressure> conditions in <enthalpy>. These distinctions make <heat> useful as a precise boundary-transfer quantity in <heat engines>, <refrigerators> and heating calculations.

Historically, James Prescott Joule's paddle experiments showed that stirring <water> with input <work> could raise its <temperature>, helping establish <conservation of energy>. The lesson is still useful: a <temperature> rise can result from <work> even without incoming <heat>. \a[https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-2/pages/1-4-heat-transfer-specific-heat-and-calorimetry][Ling, Moebs and Sanny, OpenStax, section 1.4: Joule's paddle experiment]{ref}

= Sensible heat
{parent=Heat}
{title2=$Q_{\rm sens}$}
{wiki}

Sensible heat is transferred <heat> associated with a <temperature> change within a single <thermodynamic phase>. By contrast, <latent heat> can change the <phase fraction> while the <temperature> stays fixed during equilibrium <phase coexistence>. Neither name denotes an additional stored substance: both describe how transferred <heat> participates in a process.

For fixed <mass> $m$ and composition, use the <heat capacity> appropriate to the heating constraint. Under the <quasistatic process> assumptions in <heat capacity>, with only <pressure>-<volume (thermodynamics)> <work> and unchanged bulk <kinetic energy> and <potential energy>, fixed-<pressure> heating gives
$$
Q_{\rm sens}=m\int_{T_1}^{T_2}c_p(T)\,dT,
\qquad Q_{\rm sens}=mc_p(T_2-T_1)\quad\text{if }c_p\text{ is constant}.
$$
Here $c_p$ is <specific heat capacity at constant pressure>; fixed-<volume (thermodynamics)> heating instead uses <specific heat capacity at constant volume> $c_V$. A cooling step has negative $Q_{\rm sens}$ with the sign convention in <heat>. A <temperature> difference has the same numerical value in units of the <kelvin> or <degree Celsius>. The <integral> follows by adding the small transfers $\delta Q=mc_p(T)\,dT$, rather than assuming a constant <heat capacity>.

As a stylized <ice>-to-<water> example for a fixed-composition <closed system> at fixed <pressure> $1\,\mathrm{atm}$, take $m=0.5\,\mathrm{kg}$, an initial <temperature> of $-10\,{}^\circ\mathrm C$, <melting> at $0\,{}^\circ\mathrm C$, and a final <temperature> of $20\,{}^\circ\mathrm C$. Assume constant <specific heat capacity at constant pressure> values $c_{\rm ice}=2.1\,\mathrm{kJ\,kg^{-1}\,K^{-1}}$ and $c_{\rm water}=4.18\,\mathrm{kJ\,kg^{-1}\,K^{-1}}$, and <latent heat of fusion> $L_f=334\,\mathrm{kJ\,kg^{-1}}$. These are stipulated, rounded model values; ignore the container and <heat> losses, retain all the material, and assume only <pressure>-<volume (thermodynamics)> <work> under the <quasistatic process> conditions above. The three steps are
$$
\begin{aligned}
Q_{\rm ice}&=0.5\times2.1\times10=10.5\,\mathrm{kJ},\\
Q_{\rm melt}&=0.5\times334=167\,\mathrm{kJ},\\
Q_{\rm water}&=0.5\times4.18\times20=41.8\,\mathrm{kJ},\\
Q_{\rm total}&=10.5+167+41.8=219.3\,\mathrm{kJ}.
\end{aligned}
$$
Only the first and third terms are <sensible heat>; the middle term is <latent heat>. The plateau begins after $10.5\,\mathrm{kJ}$ and ends after $177.5\,\mathrm{kJ}$ of total supplied <heat>. At $94\,\mathrm{kJ}$ total input, half the <mass> is <liquid> <water> and the <temperature> remains $0\,{}^\circ\mathrm C$. The equilibrium <phase transition> <temperature> depends on <pressure>; this example stipulates a single fixed <pressure>. \a[https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-2/pages/1-5-phase-changes][Ling, Moebs and Sanny, OpenStax, Phase changes: fixed-pressure plateaux and latent heat]{ref}

\Image[sensible-and-latent-heating-curve.png]
{title=Heating $0.5\,\mathrm{kg}$ of <ice> to <water>: <sensible heat> and <latent heat>}
{description=Illustrative fixed-<pressure> <heating curve> using the constant parameters above. The sloping portions raise <temperature>; the long $0\,{}^\circ\mathrm C$ plateau changes the <phase fraction>. Total supplied <heat> is measured from the initial <ice> state and equals the <enthalpy> change under the stated assumptions.}

This split makes heating budgets easy to interpret: <melting> consumes $167/219.3\approx76.2\%$ of the supplied <heat> in this model despite causing no <temperature> rise. Within either single <thermodynamic phase>, the <heating curve> slope is $dT/dQ=1/(mc_p)$; a larger <heat capacity> makes the slope shallower. A flat interval represents changing <phase fraction>, so it cannot be modeled by applying $Q=mc_p\Delta T$ across the <phase transition>. The complementary constructed example in <latent heat> tracks that <phase fraction> explicitly.

= Heat transfer
{parent=Heat}
{wiki}

<Heat transfer> asks how quickly <heat> crosses a boundary and how the <temperature> varies along its path. Its principal mechanisms are <thermal conduction>, <convection> and <thermal radiation>. A warm wall can conduct <heat> through its interior while exchanging <heat> with moving air and emitting <thermal radiation> at its surfaces. Identifying these paths lets us estimate heating requirements and cooling times.

Distinguish the accumulated <heat> $Q$ from its rate $\dot Q=dQ/dt$, a <power> measured in $\mathrm W$. The <heat flux> $q$ is the rate per unit <area>, measured in $\mathrm{W\,m^{-2}}$: a uniform normal <heat flux> across <area> $A$ gives $\dot Q=Aq$. The steady bar in <thermal conduction> carries $q=400\,\mathrm{W\,m^{-2}}$ through $A=0.01\,\mathrm{m^2}$, hence $\dot Q=4\,\mathrm W$; maintained for $60\,\mathrm s$, it transfers $Q=240\,\mathrm J$. This distinction makes a surface's intensity and a whole object's heating load easy to compare.

= Nusselt number
{c}
{parent=Heat transfer}
{title2=$\mathrm{Nu}$}
{wiki}

The <Nusselt number> is the ratio of total <heat transfer> to conductive <heat transfer> across the same length scale: $\mathrm{Nu}=qh/(k_T\Delta T)$, where $q$ is heat flux and $k_T$ is <thermal conductivity>.

= Thermal inertia
{parent=Heat transfer}
{title2=$\Gamma=\sqrt{k\rho c_p}$}

For a homogeneous material, thermal inertia is $\Gamma=\sqrt{k\rho c_p}$, where $k$ is <thermal conductivity>, $\rho$ is density and $c_p$ is specific <heat capacity>. It measures the resistance of the surface temperature to periodic heating and controls the phase lag relevant to the <Yarkovsky effect>.

= Radiative cooling
{parent=Heat transfer}
{wiki}

A body loses <internal energy> by emitting <thermal radiation> faster than it absorbs radiation or receives other heating. In a gravitating gas this can remove pressure support and allow further contraction.

= Compton cooling by the cosmic microwave background
{parent=Radiative cooling}
{title2=$t_C\propto(1+z)^{-4}/x_e$}

Hot electrons can transfer energy to <cosmic microwave background> photons through <Compton scattering>. For a fixed electron fraction $x_e$ and electron temperature well above the radiation temperature, the <optically thin gas cooling time> scales approximately as $(1+z)^{-4}/x_e$. Its volume loss scales with electron density, not with $n_H^2$, because the radiation density is independent of local gas density. The process tends toward the radiation temperature; it heats rather than cools electrons colder than that radiation.

= Dust thermal emission
{parent=Radiative cooling}

Dust grains emit thermal radiation after absorbing energy from radiation or collisions. Collisions can transfer thermal energy from dense gas to grains, whose radiation then cools the gas. This channel requires dust and does not operate in strictly pristine hydrogen-helium material without prior enrichment.

= Inverse Compton cooling
{parent=Radiative cooling}
{title2=$P_e=4\sigma_TcU_\gamma k_B(T_e-T_\gamma)/(m_ec^2)$}

Thermal electrons can transfer energy to lower-energy radiation photons through <Compton scattering>. In the nonrelativistic Thomson regime, the net cooling power per electron is $4\sigma_TcU_\gamma k_B(T_e-T_\gamma)/(m_ec^2)$ for a blackbody photon bath. A sufficiently hot electron population therefore loses energy; a colder population is heated instead. The <cosmic microwave background> radiation energy density grows as $(1+z)^4$, making this channel more important for high-redshift diffuse ionized gas.

= Astrophysical cooling function
{parent=Radiative cooling}
{title2=$\mathcal C=n_H^2\Lambda(T)$}

= Hydrogen-density-squared cooling function
{synonym}

An <astrophysical cooling function> packages radiative losses of a plasma into a temperature-dependent coefficient. In the hydrogen-density-squared convention, the volume energy loss is $n_H^2\Lambda(T)$, so the coefficient has units $\mathrm{erg\,cm^3\,s^{-1}}$. Its dependence on <ionization> state, chemical abundances and radiation field must be specified; a low-density collisional-equilibrium curve is not universal.

= Molecular line cooling
{parent=Astrophysical cooling function}

Radiation from rotational and vibrational transitions of molecules removes gas thermal energy. In primordial gas below the atomic threshold, <molecular hydrogen> is a principal coolant; <hydrogen deuteride> can matter in suitable chemical conditions. Molecular formation, photodissociation, optical depth and density-dependent level populations control the rate. This allows some small systems below the atomic <virial temperature> threshold to form stars.

= Cooling suppression by photoionization
{parent=Astrophysical cooling function}

<Photoionization> changes the ion population, often removing bound-state coolants at temperatures where collisional equilibrium would retain them. It can therefore suppress primordial line-cooling peaks. It also supplies <photoionization heating>. The net loss is cooling minus heating and depends on radiation spectrum, intensity, shielding and gas density. A universal density-independent function of temperature alone is not generally sufficient for irradiated gas.

= Optically thin gas cooling time
{parent=Astrophysical cooling function}
{title2=$t_{\rm cool}=3\chi k_BT/(2n_H\Lambda)$}

= Radiative gas cooling time
{synonym}

For a nonrelativistic ideal gas with $n_{\rm tot}$ thermal particles per volume, divide its thermal energy $3n_{\rm tot}k_BT/2$ by the optically thin volume radiation loss. In the $n_H^2$ cooling convention this becomes $3\chi k_BT/(2n_H\Lambda)$, where $\chi=n_{\rm tot}/n_H$. Density scaling and ionization-dependent particle count must be kept consistent.

= Cooling-to-free-fall equality curve
{parent=Optically thin gas cooling time}
{title2=$n_{H,\rm crit}=\frac{32Gm_p}{3\pi Xf_g}\left(\frac{3\chi k_BT}{2\Lambda}\right)^2$}

Equating the optically thin cooling time to the uniform-sphere free-fall time gives a critical gas density proportional to $T^2/\Lambda^2$. With <hydrogen mass fraction> $X$, particle ratio $\chi$ and gravitating gas fraction $f_g$, it is $[32Gm_p/(3\pi Xf_g)][3\chi k_BT/(2\Lambda)]^2$. At fixed temperature, gas above this density cools faster than free fall. A smaller gravitating gas fraction moves the boundary upward because gravity is faster at a fixed gas density.

= Primordial atomic cooling curve
{parent=Astrophysical cooling function}
{title2=$\Lambda_{\rm H,He}(T)$}

The <primordial atomic cooling curve> describes hydrogen-helium gas without heavy elements. Near the <hydrogen> excitation threshold it rises sharply, then shows <hydrogen> and <helium> excitation/<ionization> features. Bound-state losses weaken when both elements become highly ionized. At sufficiently high temperatures <thermal bremsstrahlung> gives a slowly rising tail. Composition, density convention and <ionization> assumptions fix the numerical normalization.

= Atomic line cooling
{parent=Astrophysical cooling function}
{title2=$\mathcal C_{\rm line}=\sum n_en_iq_{ij}(T)\,E_{ij}$}

<Atomic line cooling> removes gas kinetic energy by <collisional excitation> followed by photon escape. It is strongest where atoms or ions have bound <electrons> and thermally accessible transitions. If photons are trapped, or if the relevant atoms are fully ionized, the simple optically thin line-cooling rate no longer gives the same loss.

= Metal-line cooling
{parent=Atomic line cooling}
{title2=$\Lambda(T,Z)$}

Heavy elements provide many ionic transitions at temperatures where <hydrogen> and <helium> have lost their most effective line emitters. <Metal-line cooling> can therefore increase the plasma cooling function substantially and broaden its temperature features. Low-energy fine-structure transitions also permit cooling below the <hydrogen> atomic threshold when the relevant ions and <electrons> are present.

= Heat flux density
{title2=$\mathbf q$}
{parent=Heat}

= Heat flux
{synonym}

<Heat flux density> is a <vector> $\mathbf q$ describing the rate of <heat transfer> per unit <area>, measured in $\mathrm{W\,m^{-2}}$. Through a planar face of <area> $A$ with uniform normal component $q$, the signed rate is $\dot Q=Aq$ in $\mathrm W$; positive $q$ means <heat> travels in the chosen positive normal direction. <Fourier's law> determines the conductive contribution. In a bar with a constant cross-sectional <area>, steady <thermal conduction> without internal sources or side losses makes both $q$ and $\dot Q$ constant along the bar. Constant $\dot Q$ need not mean constant $q$ when the cross-sectional <area> varies.

= Thermal conduction
{parent=Heat}
{wiki}

= Heat conduction
{synonym}

<Thermal conduction> transfers <heat> through microscopic interactions without bulk transport of matter. To predict the heating load through a bar, combine <Fourier's law> with <conservation of energy> and the imposed end <temperatures>. In the <isotropic> model with positive <thermal conductivity> $k$, the conductive <heat flux> points from high to low <temperature>, opposite to the <temperature gradient>. A spoon warming along its handle and <heat> escaping through a wall are familiar examples.

Consider a bar of <length> $L=0.10\,\mathrm m$, constant cross-sectional <area> $A=0.010\,\mathrm{m^2}$ and constant <thermal conductivity> $k=2\,\mathrm{W\,m^{-1}\,K^{-1}}$. Maintain its ends at $T_h=40\,{}^\circ\mathrm C$ and $T_c=20\,{}^\circ\mathrm C$, with $x$ increasing toward the colder end. Assume a <steady state>, one-dimensional <thermal conduction>, no internal sources and no <heat transfer> through the sides. The <temperature> falls by $20\,\mathrm K$ over $0.10\,\mathrm m$, giving
$$
\frac{dT}{dx}=-200\,\mathrm{K\,m^{-1}},\qquad
q=-k\frac{dT}{dx}=400\,\mathrm{W\,m^{-2}},\qquad
\dot Q=Aq=4\,\mathrm W.
$$
The difference between the two readings in <degree Celsius> units is the same $20$-<kelvin> difference. The positive <heat flux> carries <heat> toward increasing $x$.

\Image[fourier-conduction-steady-bar.png]
{title=Steady <thermal conduction> through a bar: declining <temperature>, constant <heat flux>}
{description=An <isotropic> bar with stipulated constant <thermal conductivity> $k=2\,\mathrm{W\,m^{-1}\,K^{-1}}$, <length> $0.10\,\mathrm m$ and cross-sectional <area> $0.010\,\mathrm{m^2}$. Its ends remain at $40$ and $20\,{}^\circ\mathrm C$, and its sides exchange no <heat>. The lower panel distinguishes $q=400\,\mathrm{W\,m^{-2}}$ from $\dot Q=4\,\mathrm W$.}

The general solution under these assumptions is
$$
T(x)=T_h-\frac{T_h-T_c}{L}x,\qquad
q=\frac{k(T_h-T_c)}{L},\qquad
\dot Q=\frac{kA(T_h-T_c)}{L}.
$$
A proof outline is enough to explain the straight line: <conservation of energy> in any small slice, with no accumulation, sources or side losses, gives $d\dot Q/dx=0$. Constant cross-sectional <area> gives $dq/dx=0$; constant <thermal conductivity> and <Fourier's law> then give $d^2T/dx^2=0$. Two <integrations> and the end <boundary conditions> determine the displayed <affine function>. This is why a visually simple <temperature> profile can encode a continuous flow of <energy>. \a[https://web.mit.edu/16.unified/www/FALL/thermodynamics/notes/node117.html][E. M. Greitzer, Z. S. Spakovszky and I. A. Waitz, MIT Thermodynamics and Propulsion, section 16.3: steady one-dimensional conduction]{ref}

Doubling the <length> halves the rate, while doubling the cross-sectional <area> doubles it, at fixed end <temperatures> and <thermal conductivity>. This explains why a long narrow support can limit unwanted <heat transfer>, while a short broad path can help remove <heat>. Joining materials leads to <thermal resistance> in series. During initial warming, <internal energy> can accumulate and the <temperature> profile need not be a straight line; the time dependence is described by the <heat equation> and <thermal diffusivity>.

Now join two layers, each of <length> $L_1=L_2=0.10\,\mathrm m$, with constant cross-sectional <area> $A=0.010\,\mathrm{m^2}$ and stipulated constant <thermal conductivities> $k_1=1$ and $k_2=4\,\mathrm{W\,m^{-1}\,K^{-1}}$. Keep the outer ends at $40$ and $20\,{}^\circ\mathrm C$. Assume a <steady state>, one-dimensional <thermal conduction>, no internal sources or side losses, and perfect contact: the <temperature> is continuous at the interface, with no extra contact drop. For each layer, use the area-specific <thermal resistance> $r_i=L_i/k_i$ and $R_i=r_i/A$. The series calculation gives
$$
\begin{aligned}
r_1+r_2=\frac{L_1}{k_1}+\frac{L_2}{k_2}
=0.100+0.025=0.125\,\mathrm{m^2\,K\,W^{-1}},\\
R_1+R_2=\frac{r_1+r_2}{A}=12.5\,\mathrm{K\,W^{-1}},\\
q=\frac{20\,\mathrm K}{0.125\,\mathrm{m^2\,K\,W^{-1}}}
=160\,\mathrm{W\,m^{-2}},\qquad \dot Q=Aq=1.6\,\mathrm W,\\
\Delta T_1=qr_1=16\,\mathrm K,\qquad
\Delta T_2=qr_2=4\,\mathrm K,\qquad
T_{\rm interface}=40\,{}^\circ\mathrm C-16\,\mathrm K=24\,{}^\circ\mathrm C.
\end{aligned}
$$

\Image[layered-thermal-conduction.png]
{title=Two layers in series: continuous <heat flux>, different <temperature gradients>}
{description=Each layer is $0.10\,\mathrm m$ long with the same cross-sectional <area> $0.010\,\mathrm{m^2}$ and perfect contact. Stipulated constant <thermal conductivities> are $1$ and $4\,\mathrm{W\,m^{-1}\,K^{-1}}$. The <temperature> drops by $16\,\mathrm K$ and $4\,\mathrm K$, reaching $24\,{}^\circ\mathrm C$ at the interface, while $q=160\,\mathrm{W\,m^{-2}}$ and $\dot Q=1.6\,\mathrm W$ throughout.}

The <heat flux> is continuous: otherwise <energy> would accumulate at the interface. Yet <Fourier's law> gives different <temperature gradients>, $dT/dx=-q/k_1=-160\,\mathrm{K\,m^{-1}}$ and $dT/dx=-q/k_2=-40\,\mathrm{K\,m^{-1}}$. Lower <thermal conductivity> requires a steeper <temperature gradient> to carry the same <heat flux>; equal <lengths> therefore give drops in the ratio $4:1$. A kink in the <temperature> profile records the change of material while perfect contact keeps the <temperature> itself continuous.

= Thermal resistance
{parent=Thermal conduction}
{title2=$R_{\rm th}=\Delta T/\dot Q$}

<Thermal resistance> measures the <temperature> drop required to drive a given rate of <heat transfer>. For the steady one-dimensional bar model in <thermal conduction>,
$$
R_{\rm th}=\frac{T_h-T_c}{\dot Q}=\frac{L}{kA}
\quad[\mathrm{K\,W^{-1}}],\qquad
r=AR_{\rm th}=\frac{L}{k}
\quad[\mathrm{m^2\,K\,W^{-1}}],\qquad
q=\frac{T_h-T_c}{r}.
$$
Here $r$ relates the <temperature> drop to <heat flux>, while $R_{\rm th}$ relates it to the rate of <heat transfer>. The single bar above has $r=0.050\,\mathrm{m^2\,K\,W^{-1}}$ and $R_{\rm th}=5\,\mathrm{K\,W^{-1}}$, so its $20\,\mathrm K$ drop drives $4\,\mathrm W$.

To see why series <thermal resistances> add, apply <Fourier's law> in each layer to obtain $\Delta T_i=\dot Q R_i$. With no accumulation or side losses the rate of <heat transfer> is the same through both, so adding the drops gives $T_h-T_c=\dot Q(R_1+R_2)$. This lets us size layered walls without solving a new <differential equation> for every layer. The structure matches <Ohm's law>: <temperature> difference plays the role of <electric potential difference>, the rate of <heat transfer> plays the role of <electric current>, and <thermal resistance> plays the role of <electrical resistance>. \a[https://web.mit.edu/16.unified/www/FALL/thermodynamics/notes/node118.html][E. M. Greitzer, Z. S. Spakovszky and I. A. Waitz, MIT Thermodynamics and Propulsion, section 16.4: series thermal resistances and the electrical analogy]{ref}

= Temperature dipole around an insulating sphere
{parent=Thermal conduction}
{title2=$T=T_0+(\mathbf H\cdot\mathbf r)(1+a^3/(2r^3))$}

In the absence of heat advection, an insulating <sphere> of radius $a$ in a uniform remote <temperature> gradient $\mathbf H$ has the displayed exterior <harmonic function> as its temperature field. The reflected dipole term makes $\partial_rT=0$ at $r=a$. The surface temperature is $T_0+(3/2)a\mathbf H\cdot\mathbf n$, so the tangential gradient is amplified by $3/2$. If the sphere has <thermal conductivity> ratio $K$ relative to its surroundings, temperature and heat-flux continuity instead give surface gradient factor $3/(2+K)$.

= Isobaric conductive cooling in mass coordinates
{parent=Thermal conduction}
{title2=$T_\tau+\Lambda(T)/T-\lambda_0\partial_m(T^{\alpha-1}T_m)=0$}

With fixed <pressure> $p$, let $A=\mu p/\mathcal R$, so $\rho=A/T$. A material mass coordinate has $\partial_x=\rho\partial_m$ and the material time derivative becomes $\partial_t|_m$. The energy and continuity equations then give the displayed equation with $\tau=Ct$ and $C=(\gamma-1)(\mu/\mathcal R)^2p/\gamma$. A mass integral from a fixed origin is material only when the <mass flux> there vanishes; otherwise subtract its time-integrated flux to label parcels. Uniform <pressure> is a low-Mach approximation when sound crossing is faster than thermal evolution.

= Error-function isobaric cooling front
{parent=Isobaric conductive cooling in mass coordinates}
{title2=$T=T_0\operatorname{erf}\!\left(m/(2\sqrt{\lambda_0\tau})\right)$}

For conductivity $\lambda=\lambda_0T$, no cooling in the hot phase and a zero-<temperature> boundary at $m=0$, the mass-coordinate equation is linear diffusion. Its similarity profile solves $f''+\xi f'/2=0$, $f(0)=0$, $f(\infty)=1$, giving the displayed <error function>. The thermal layer broadens as the square root of time. Conducted energy enters the cold radiating reservoir at rate per area $L=\lambda_0A T_m(0)=A T_0\sqrt{\lambda_0}/\sqrt{\pi\tau}$, hence $L\propto t^{-1/2}$.

= Temperature gradient
{title2=$\nabla T$}
{parent=Thermal conduction}

The temperature gradient is the <gradient> $\nabla T$ of the <temperature> field. It points in the direction of fastest temperature increase.

= Fourier's law
{title2=$\mathbf q=-k\nabla T$}
{c}
{parent=Thermal conduction}
{wiki}

<Fourier's law> predicts the local conductive <heat flux> from the local <temperature gradient>. In a <continuum approximation> with <isotropic> <thermal conductivity> $k>0$, its <vector> form and one-dimensional form are
$$
\mathbf q=-k\nabla T,\qquad
q=-k\frac{dT}{dx},\qquad
\dot Q=Aq\quad\text{for uniform normal flux over area }A.
$$
The <heat flux> $q$ has units $\mathrm{W\,m^{-2}}$, the <temperature gradient> has units $\mathrm{K\,m^{-1}}$ and the <thermal conductivity> has units $\mathrm{W\,m^{-1}\,K^{-1}}$. The rate of <heat transfer> $\dot Q$ has units $\mathrm W$. These units distinguish a local material response from the total <power> crossing a surface.

The minus sign makes <heat> flow toward lower <temperature>: for $k>0$, $\mathbf q\cdot\nabla T=-k|\nabla T|^2\leq0$. In the worked bar in <thermal conduction>, $dT/dx=-200\,\mathrm{K\,m^{-1}}$ therefore gives $q=+400\,\mathrm{W\,m^{-2}}$. Reversing the end <temperatures> reverses the <heat flux>. A zero <temperature gradient> gives zero conductive <heat flux> in this model.

<Fourier's law> is a constitutive relation expressing measured material behavior; it is not a consequence of <conservation of energy> alone. It applies locally and does not require a <steady state>. The straight-line bar solution additionally assumes constant <thermal conductivity>, constant cross-sectional <area>, no sources or side losses, and fixed end <temperatures>. <Anisotropic> materials require the directional response described by the <thermal conductivity tensor>. The <scalar> $k$ used in the examples expresses the stated <isotropy> assumption. \a[https://web.mit.edu/16.unified/www/FALL/thermodynamics/notes/node116.html][E. M. Greitzer, Z. S. Spakovszky and I. A. Waitz, MIT Thermodynamics and Propulsion, section 16.2: Fourier's law and thermal conductivity]{ref}

Historically, <Joseph Fourier>'s \i[Théorie analytique de la chaleur] was published in 1822. In Chapter II, article 112, he writes the <heat> crossing a spherical surface as the product of <thermal conductivity>, elapsed time, surface <area> and the negative radial <temperature gradient>; article 113 balances the incoming and outgoing <heat> to obtain the <heat equation> for a <sphere>. The enduring idea is this connection between a local flow rule and a global prediction of changing <temperature>. \a[https://fr.wikisource.org/wiki/Théorie_analytique_de_la_chaleur][Joseph Fourier, Théorie analytique de la chaleur, Firmin Didot, 1822]{ref} \a[https://fr.wikisource.org/wiki/Théorie_analytique_de_la_chaleur/Chapitre_2][Fourier, 1822, Chapter II, articles 112–113]{ref}

= Thermal conductivity
{title2=$k$}
{parent=Fourier's law}
{wiki}

<Thermal conductivity> is the material response coefficient in <Fourier's law>. For an <isotropic> material it is a <scalar> $k$, with units $\mathrm{W\,m^{-1}\,K^{-1}}$; larger positive $k$ means a larger conductive <heat flux> for the same <temperature gradient>. It can depend on <temperature> and composition. Direction-dependent response in an <anisotropic> material is described by a <thermal conductivity tensor>. The two worked examples use stipulated positive constants, rather than measured values for named materials.

<Thermal conductivity> describes material response, whereas <thermal resistance> also includes geometry: for the steady bar, $R_{\rm th}=L/(kA)$. Doubling $k$ at fixed <length>, cross-sectional <area> and end <temperatures> halves <thermal resistance> and doubles the rate of <heat transfer>. Doubling <length> changes <thermal resistance> without changing the material's <thermal conductivity>. This distinction lets a designer improve cooling either by choosing a higher <thermal conductivity> or by shortening and widening the path. It also explains why the less conductive layer in <thermal resistance> takes the greater <temperature> drop.

<Thermal diffusivity> answers a different question: how rapidly a <temperature> disturbance spreads. Its usual model $\kappa=k/(\rho c_p)$ also involves <mass density> $\rho$ and <specific heat capacity> $c_p$, so equal <thermal conductivities> need not imply equal rates of warming.

= Thermal conductivity tensor
{parent=Thermal conductivity}
{title2=$\mathbf q=-a\nabla T$}

The thermal conductivity tensor maps a temperature gradient to the negative heat-flux density. In passive reciprocal conduction it is symmetric positive definite. This permits direction-dependent transport while ensuring positive dissipated energy.

= Thermal diffusivity
{title2=$\kappa$}
{parent=Thermal conductivity}
{wiki}

Thermal diffusivity is $\kappa=k/(\rho c_p)$, the ratio of <thermal conductivity> to volumetric <heat capacity>. It sets the diffusion length $\sqrt{\kappa t}$ over time $t$.

= Prandtl number
{c}
{parent=Thermal diffusivity}
{title2=$\operatorname{Pr}=\nu/\kappa_T$}
{wiki}

The <Prandtl number> is the ratio of <kinematic viscosity> to <thermal diffusivity>. It compares the rates at which momentum and <temperature> variations diffuse. In thermal-diffusion units the <Boussinesq equations> have momentum inertia multiplied by $\operatorname{Pr}^{-1}$; large, rather than small, <Prandtl number> supplies the usual instantaneous <Stokes flow> limit.

= Thermal diffusion time
{title2=$L^2/\kappa$}
{parent=Thermal diffusivity}

The thermal diffusion time across a distance $L$ is $L^2/\kappa$, where $\kappa$ is the <thermal diffusivity>. It is the time required for a temperature disturbance to spread over that distance by <thermal conduction>.

= Thermal diffusion rate of a Fourier mode
{parent=Thermal diffusion time}
{title2=$\beta=\xi k^2$}

With <thermal diffusivity> $\xi$, a <Fourier mode> of <wavenumber> $k$ decays by diffusion at this rate.

= Stefan–Boltzmann law
{title2=$F=\epsilon\sigma T^4$}
{c}
{parent=Heat}
{wiki}

The Stefan–Boltzmann law gives the thermal-radiation flux emitted by a surface as $F=\epsilon\sigma T^4$, where $\epsilon$ is its <emissivity> and $\sigma$ is the Stefan–Boltzmann constant.

= Stefan-Boltzmann constant
{parent=Stefan–Boltzmann law}
{c}
{title2=$\sigma$}

The <Stefan-Boltzmann constant> $\sigma$ is the proportionality constant in the blackbody flux $F=\sigma T^4$. The blackbody <radiation pressure> is $p_{\rm rad}=4\sigma T^4/(3c)$.

= Emissivity
{title2=$\epsilon$}
{parent=Stefan–Boltzmann law}
{wiki}

Emissivity is the ratio of the thermal radiation emitted by a surface to that emitted by an ideal black body at the same <temperature>.

= Radiative equilibrium
{parent=Stefan–Boltzmann law}
{wiki}

A body is in radiative equilibrium when absorbed radiative power equals emitted radiative power. A surface absorbing flux $F$ and emitting as a grey body has equilibrium temperature $T=(F/(\epsilon\sigma))^{1/4}$.

= Heat capacity
{parent=Heat}
{title2=$C_X=(\delta Q/dT)_X$}
{wiki}

Heat capacity measures the <heat> supplied per small <temperature> increase along a specified process:
$$
C_X=\left(\frac{\delta Q}{dT}\right)_X.
$$
The subscript $X$ records the constraint, such as fixed <volume (thermodynamics)> or fixed <pressure>. The units are $\mathrm{J\,K^{-1}}$. The symbol $\delta Q$ emphasizes that <heat> is a transfer along a path, rather than a <state function> with a differential $dQ$. Consequently, an unspecified <heat capacity> does not determine the required <heat>.

For a <closed system> of fixed composition undergoing a <quasistatic process>, assume that only <pressure>-<volume (thermodynamics)> <work> occurs, with no <friction> and no change in bulk <kinetic energy> or <potential energy>. The <first law of thermodynamics> and $H=U+pV$, where $U$ is <internal energy> and $H$ is <enthalpy>, then give
$$
\delta Q=dU+p\,dV=dH-V\,dp,\qquad
C_V=\left(\frac{\partial U}{\partial T}\right)_V,\qquad
C_p=\left(\frac{\partial H}{\partial T}\right)_p.
$$
These <partial derivatives> define <heat capacity at constant volume> and <heat capacity at constant pressure>, with the amount and composition also held fixed. Integrating along the selected constraint gives $Q=\int_{T_1}^{T_2}C_X(T)\,dT$ within a single <thermodynamic phase>; $Q=C_X\Delta T$ requires constant $C_X$. \a[https://davidtong.org/teaching/statistical-physics/statmechhtml/S4][Tong, Statistical Physics, first law and heat capacities]{ref}

For a computed example, take a <monatomic gas> in the classical <ideal gas> regime, with <amount of substance> $n=1\,\mathrm{mol}$ and stipulated <molar gas constant> $R=8.314\,\mathrm{J\,mol^{-1}\,K^{-1}}$. The <equipartition theorem> gives $U=3nRT/2$, and the <ideal gas law> gives $pV=nRT$. Thus
$$
C_V=\frac32nR=12.471\,\mathrm{J\,K^{-1}},\qquad
C_p=\frac52nR=20.785\,\mathrm{J\,K^{-1}}.
$$
For the same <temperature> increase $\Delta T=10\,\mathrm K$, the two processes require
$$
Q_V=124.71\,\mathrm J,\qquad Q_p=207.85\,\mathrm J,\qquad
\Delta U_V=\Delta U_p=124.71\,\mathrm J,\qquad
W_V=0,\quad W_p=p\Delta V=nR\Delta T=83.14\,\mathrm J.
$$
Here <work> done by the <gas> is positive. At fixed <pressure>, the extra <heat> supplies expansion <work>; the <internal energy> increase is the same because <internal energy of an ideal gas> depends only on <temperature>. Their final <volume (thermodynamics)> values differ. \a[https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-2/pages/3-5-heat-capacities-of-an-ideal-gas][OpenStax, Heat capacities of an ideal gas]{ref}

\Image[heat-capacity-constraints.png]
{title=Required <heat> depends on the constraint and the amount of material}
{description=Left: one <mole> of classical <monatomic gas> with the stipulated <molar gas constant> above; the shaded difference is expansion <work> at fixed <pressure>. Right: the illustrative fixed-<volume (thermodynamics)> body from <specific heat capacity>, with <mass> $2\,\mathrm{kg}$ and $c_V=400\,\mathrm{J\,kg^{-1}\,K^{-1}}$. Each curve starts at zero accumulated <heat>, rather than zero absolute <internal energy>.}

The practical value of <heat capacity> is that a measured <heat> input and <temperature> response can determine it. Once its constraint and <temperature> dependence are known, it predicts heating requirements and supplies <entropy> changes through <entropy from heat capacity>. \a[https://davidtong.org/teaching/statistical-physics/statmechhtml/S1][Tong, Statistical Physics, heat capacity as a measurable response]{ref}

= Molar heat capacity
{parent=Heat capacity}
{title2=$C_{\mathrm m}=C/n$}

Molar heat capacity is <heat capacity> divided by <amount of substance> $n$, with units $\mathrm{J\,mol^{-1}\,K^{-1}}$. Its constraint must still be specified: $C_{V,\mathrm m}=C_V/n$ and $C_{p,\mathrm m}=C_p/n$. For the classical <monatomic gas> example in <heat capacity>, these are $3R/2$ and $5R/2$, where $R$ is the <molar gas constant>. Some textbooks use $C_V$ and $C_p$ for these per-<mole> quantities; the convention here reserves them for the whole sample.

= Heating curve
{parent=Heat capacity}

A heating curve records <temperature> against accumulated supplied <heat> under a specified constraint. Within one <thermodynamic phase>, its slope is $dT/dQ=1/C_X$, where $C_X$ is the constrained <heat capacity>. A <latent heat> plateau instead records changing <phase fraction> at the <phase coexistence> <temperature>. The <sensible heat> example combines two sloping intervals and a <melting> plateau in one heating budget.

= Entropy from heat capacity
{parent=Heat capacity}

Along a reversible path at fixed volume, $dS=C_V(T)dT/T$. Hence entropy differences can be measured macroscopically from
$$
S(T_2)-S(T_1)=\int_{T_1}^{T_2}\frac{C_V(T)}T\,dT,
$$
with an additional $L/T_c$ across a first-order transition of <latent heat> $L$ at $T_c$.

= Entropy concavity and positive heat capacity
{parent=Heat capacity}

For a thermodynamically stable system, the <entropy> is concave in energy, so
$$
\frac{\partial^2S}{\partial E^2}
=-\frac1{T^2}\frac{\partial T}{\partial E}<0.
$$
Consequently $\partial E/\partial T>0$: energy increases with <temperature>, and the corresponding <heat capacity> is positive.

= Specific heat capacity
{title2=$c=C/m$}
{parent=Heat capacity}
{wiki}

Specific heat capacity is <heat capacity> per unit <mass>:
$$
c=\frac Cm,\qquad c_V=\frac{C_V}{m},\qquad c_p=\frac{C_p}{m}.
$$
Its units are $\mathrm{J\,kg^{-1}\,K^{-1}}$, and the constraint remains part of the definition. In particular, <specific heat capacity at constant volume> and <specific heat capacity at constant pressure> are different responses. For homogeneous material, doubling the <mass> doubles its <heat capacity> while leaving its <specific heat capacity> unchanged. \a[https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-2/pages/1-4-heat-transfer-specific-heat-and-calorimetry][OpenStax, Specific heat and heating calculations]{ref}

As an illustrative material model, give a fixed-<volume (thermodynamics)> body <mass> $m=2\,\mathrm{kg}$ and constant <specific heat capacity at constant volume> $c_V=400\,\mathrm{J\,kg^{-1}\,K^{-1}}$. With no <phase transition> and no other <work>, increasing its uniform <temperature> by $10\,\mathrm K$ requires
$$
C_V=mc_V=800\,\mathrm{J\,K^{-1}},\qquad
Q=mc_V\Delta T=8000\,\mathrm J=\Delta U.
$$
These stipulated values describe no measured substance. The right panel of the diagram in <heat capacity> shows the straight line $Q=mc_V\Delta T$: a <temperature> increase of $5\,\mathrm K$ takes $4000\,\mathrm J$ of <heat>. If the <specific heat capacity at constant volume> depends on <temperature>, use $Q=m\int_{T_1}^{T_2}c_V(T)\,dT$ instead. The corresponding fixed-<pressure> calculation uses <specific heat capacity at constant pressure> and gives $Q=\Delta H$ under the assumptions in <heat capacity>.

Choosing between <specific heat capacity>, <molar heat capacity> and <volumetric heat capacity> is choosing whether to compare equal <mass>, equal <amount of substance> or equal <volume (thermodynamics)>. A small sample with large <specific heat capacity> can still have less total <heat capacity> than a much larger sample.

= Volumetric heat capacity
{parent=Specific heat capacity}
{title2=$C_{\mathrm{vol}}=C/V=\rho c$}
{wiki}

Volumetric heat capacity is <heat capacity> per unit <volume (thermodynamics)>, with units $\mathrm{J\,m^{-3}\,K^{-1}}$. For homogeneous material of <mass density> $\rho=m/V$, it equals $\rho c$, using <specific heat capacity> $c$ for the same constraint. It measures the <heat> required per small <temperature> increase for a chosen occupied <volume (thermodynamics)>. In a material model using <specific heat capacity at constant pressure>, <thermal diffusivity> is $\alpha=k/(\rho c_p)$, where $k$ is <thermal conductivity>.

= Specific heat capacity at constant volume
{parent=Specific heat capacity}
{title2=$c_V=(\partial u/\partial T)_\rho$}

The specific heat capacity at constant volume is the temperature derivative of <specific internal energy> at fixed density and composition: $c_V=(\partial u/\partial T)_\rho$. It is the corresponding extensive <heat capacity at constant volume> divided by material mass.

= Specific heat capacity at constant pressure
{title2=$c_P$}
{parent=Specific heat capacity}

The <specific heat capacity> at fixed <pressure> and composition is $c_P=(\partial h/\partial T)_P$, where $h$ is <specific enthalpy>. Equivalently, it is <heat capacity at constant pressure> divided by <mass>. Under the process assumptions in <heat capacity>, it includes expansion <work> as well as the <internal energy> change. The <sensible heat> example uses this response separately for <ice> and <water>.

= Pure-radiation constant-pressure heat-capacity singularity
{parent=Specific heat capacity at constant pressure}

For pure <blackbody radiation>, $P=aT^4/3$ depends only on <temperature>. Constant pressure therefore fixes temperature, so a usual finite constant-pressure temperature derivative is unavailable. In the material gas-radiation limit the <specific-heat ratio> diverges, although all three <stellar adiabatic exponents> approach $4/3$. The radiation index $4/3$ is an adiabatic pressure-volume response, not a finite heat-capacity ratio.

= Heat capacity at constant volume
{title2=$C_V$}
{parent=Heat capacity}

= Constant-volume heat capacity
{synonym}

For a fixed-composition <closed system>, the <heat capacity> at fixed <volume (thermodynamics)> is $C_V=(\partial U/\partial T)_V$, with <internal energy> $U$. Under the <quasistatic process> assumptions in <heat capacity>, only <pressure>-<volume (thermodynamics)> <work> is permitted, so $dV=0$ gives $\delta Q=dU=C_V\,dT$. A rigid vessel therefore needs $Q=\Delta U$, or $Q=C_V\Delta T$ when $C_V$ is constant. Its per-unit-<mass> counterpart is <specific heat capacity at constant volume>.

= Dulong-Petit law
{c}
{parent=Heat capacity at constant volume}
{wiki=Dulong–Petit_law}

In the classical high-<temperature> limit, each independent <harmonic oscillator> contributes $k_B$ to the <heat capacity at constant volume>, where $k_B$ is the <Boltzmann constant>. A three-dimensional crystalline <solid> with $N$ <chemical atoms> has $3N$ vibrational <normal modes> in the bulk harmonic approximation. Its vibrational <internal energy> and <heat capacity at constant volume> therefore obey
$$
U_{\rm vib}=U_0+3Nk_BT,\qquad
C_V=\left(\frac{\partial U_{\rm vib}}{\partial T}\right)_{V,N}=3Nk_B.
$$
Here $U_0$ is independent of <temperature> at fixed <volume (thermodynamics)>. Thus the <Dulong-Petit law> predicts $C_V\simeq3Nk_B$ for a real <solid> in this regime. Per <mole> of <chemical atoms>, rather than per <mole> of a compound's formula units,
$$
C_{V,\rm mol}\longrightarrow3R\simeq24.9434\,\mathrm{J\,mol^{-1}\,K^{-1}},\qquad R=N_Ak_B,
$$
with <molar gas constant> $R$ and <Avogadro constant> $N_A$. A formula unit containing $r$ <chemical atoms> consequently has a classical vibrational prediction of $3rR$ per <mole> of formula units.

For example, in this ideal classical model, one <mole> of <chemical atoms> heated at fixed <volume (thermodynamics)> from $300$ to $600\,\mathrm K$ gains vibrational <internal energy>
$$
\Delta U_{\rm vib}=3R(600-300)\,\mathrm K\simeq7483.02\,\mathrm J.
$$
The <heat capacity at constant volume> stays the same even though the thermal part of the <internal energy> doubles. These <temperatures> illustrate the stipulated classical model; they do not assert that every real <solid> is classical at $300\,\mathrm K$.

\Image[dulong-petit-classical-energy.png]
{title=Classical <equipartition theorem>: one <harmonic oscillator> stores $k_BT$, one <chemical atom> stores $3k_BT$}
{description=Mean <kinetic energy> and <potential energy> at $300$ and $600\,\mathrm K$, in units of $k_B(300\,\mathrm K)$. Each independent quadratic term contributes $k_BT/2$. The three-direction model has three kinetic and three potential terms per <chemical atom>; doubling <temperature> doubles both contributions.}

The proof outline is the <equipartition theorem>: one classical <harmonic oscillator> has a quadratic <kinetic energy> and a quadratic <potential energy>, each with mean $k_BT/2$. Their sum is $k_BT$, whose <derivative> with respect to <temperature> is $k_B$. Summing over $3N$ <normal modes> gives $3Nk_B$. The classical result does not require equal <angular frequencies>: each harmonic <normal mode> contributes the same amount once it is classical. Overall rigid motions give only a finite-size correction to the bulk count. This independence from the individual <angular frequencies> makes the <Dulong-Petit law> useful as a quick estimate of a <solid>'s heating requirement.

Experiments on freely expanding <solids> often measure the <heat capacity at constant pressure> $C_P$, whereas the harmonic prediction is for the <heat capacity at constant volume> $C_V$. In a smooth single <thermodynamic phase> of a simple compressible material, the <difference between constant-pressure and constant-volume heat capacities> is
$$
C_P-C_V=\frac{TV\alpha^2}{\kappa_T},
$$
where $\alpha=V^{-1}(\partial V/\partial T)_P$ is the volumetric <coefficient of thermal expansion> and $\kappa_T=-V^{-1}(\partial V/\partial P)_T$ is the <isothermal compressibility>. Using measured $C_P$ as an approximation to $C_V$ requires this correction to be small compared with $C_V$. \a[https://davidtong.org/teaching/statistical-physics/statmechhtml/S4#S4.SS4.SSS2][David Tong, Statistical Physics, section 4.4.2: relation between the two heat capacities]{ref}

High <temperature> here means $k_BT\gg\hbar\omega$ for the relevant vibrational <normal modes>, with reduced <Planck constant> $\hbar$ and mode <angular frequency> $\omega$. At low <temperature>, excitation across a quantum <energy> spacing is suppressed: modes with $\hbar\omega\gg k_BT$ freeze out thermally, and the classical <equipartition theorem> fails. The <Einstein model of a solid> makes this loss of vibrational <heat capacity> explicit. The <Dulong-Petit law> is therefore an ideal high-<temperature> vibrational limit, not a statement that all <solids> have the same <heat capacity> at every <temperature>. Other contributions and departures from the harmonic approximation can matter even at high <temperature>. \a[https://davidtong.org/teaching/statistical-physics/statmechhtml/S3#S3.SS3][David Tong, Statistical Physics, section 3.3: solid vibrations, the classical limit and quantum freeze-out]{ref}

Historically, Petit and Dulong's 1819 experiments compared the <specific heat capacities> of several elemental <solids> and found an approximately constant product with their relative atomic weights. Their original empirical regularity came before the classical harmonic explanation; it suggested that counting <chemical atoms> reveals a simpler pattern than comparing equal <masses>. \a[https://webserver.lemoyne.edu/giunta/PETIT.html][Petit and Dulong, Recherches sur quelques points importants de la Théorie de la Chaleur, Annales de Chimie et de Physique 10, 395–413 (1819), contemporary English translation hosted by Carmen Giunta]{ref}

= Einstein solid
{c}
{parent=Dulong-Petit law}
{title2=$C_V/(3Nk_B)=x^2e^x/(e^x-1)^2$}
{wiki}

= Einstein model of a solid
{c}
{synonym}

The <Einstein model of a solid> represents the vibrations of $N$ <chemical atoms> by $3N$ independent one-dimensional <quantum harmonic oscillators>, all with the same positive <angular frequency> $\omega$. Equivalently, each <chemical atom> has three independent oscillator directions. The common <angular frequency> is a simplifying model assumption, not a claim about every vibration of a real <solid>. Hold the <volume (thermodynamics)>, $N$ and $\omega$ fixed. Define the characteristic <temperature> $\theta_E=\hbar\omega/k_B$ and the dimensionless ratio $x=\theta_E/T$. The vibrational <internal energy> is
$$
U=3N\hbar\omega\left[\frac12+\frac{1}{e^x-1}\right],\qquad
x=\frac{\hbar\omega}{k_BT}=\frac{\theta_E}{T}.
$$
The first term is the <zero-point energy> $3N\hbar\omega/2$. It is constant at fixed $\omega$, so it contributes no <heat capacity at constant volume>. The <temperature>-dependent term gives
$$
\frac{C_V}{3Nk_B}=\frac{x^2e^x}{(e^x-1)^2}
=\frac{x^2e^{-x}}{(1-e^{-x})^2}.
$$
This is the vibrational <heat capacity at constant volume> of the model, with no expansion or other contributions included. \a[https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/3-012-fundamentals-of-materials-science-fall-2005/d3fa883afa8df7cf7aa0432fcaadde8f_lec22t.pdf][MIT 3.012, Lecture 22, pages 9–13: Einstein-solid partition function, energy and heat capacity]{ref}

For a worked comparison, choose the model parameter $\theta_E=300\,\mathrm K$. The normalized <heat capacity at constant volume> depends only on $T/\theta_E$:

|| $T/\theta_E$
|| <Temperature> $T$ in $\mathrm K$
|| $C_V/(3Nk_B)$

| $0.1$
| $30$
| $0.00454$

| $0.5$
| $150$
| $0.72406$

| $1$
| $300$
| $0.92067$

| $2$
| $600$
| $0.97942$

At $T=\theta_E$, one <mole> of <chemical atoms> therefore has vibrational <heat capacity at constant volume> $3R(0.92067)\simeq22.965\,\mathrm{J\,mol^{-1}\,K^{-1}}$, using the unrounded model value. Even at twice the characteristic <temperature>, the model remains about $2.06\%$ below the <Dulong-Petit law> limit. These are calculations for the ideal <Einstein solid>, not material measurements.

\Image[einstein-solid-heat-capacity.png]
{title=<Einstein solid> vibrational <heat capacity> approaches the <Dulong-Petit law> limit}
{description=The model ratio $C_V/(3Nk_B)$ over $0.02\leq T/\theta_E\leq3$, with the four computed cases marked. The dashed classical line is $1$; quantum freeze-out suppresses the low-<temperature> response. All <quantum harmonic oscillators> have the same stipulated <angular frequency>.}

A short derivation uses the <thermal partition function of a quantum harmonic oscillator>. The <energy eigenvalues> $E_n=\hbar\omega(n+1/2)$ and their <Boltzmann factors> form a <geometric series>, giving the one-oscillator <canonical partition function> $z=e^{-x/2}/(1-e^{-x})$. The mean occupation is $1/(e^x-1)$, hence the displayed $U$ after summing over $3N$ independent <quantum harmonic oscillators>. Taking its <derivative> with $dx/dT=-x/T$ gives the displayed <heat capacity at constant volume>. \a[https://davidtong.org/pdfs/teaching/statistical-physics/omg1.pdf][David Tong, Statistical Physics, Example Sheet 1 (January 2012), problem 5: oscillator energy, the Einstein model and its limits]{ref}

The two limits explain the shape. At high <temperature>, a <Taylor series> in small $x$ gives
$$
\frac{C_V}{3Nk_B}=1-\frac{x^2}{12}+O(x^4)\longrightarrow1
\qquad(T\gg\theta_E).
$$
At low <temperature>, $1-e^{-x}\to1$, so
$$
\frac{C_V}{3Nk_B}\sim x^2e^{-x}\longrightarrow0
\qquad(T\ll\theta_E).
$$
The <zero-point energy> remains even when the thermal response is tiny: freeze-out means that changing <temperature> scarcely changes the occupations, not that the <chemical atoms> have zero <energy>. This is the useful lesson of the <Einstein model of a solid>: the classical counting rule reappears when many levels are accessible, while discrete excitation spacings explain its failure upon cooling.

The exponential low-<temperature> decay is also the model's limitation. Real three-dimensional crystalline <solids> have <acoustic phonon branches> with arbitrarily small <angular frequencies> in the bulk limit. The <Debye model> includes these collective <normal modes> and predicts a low-<temperature> vibrational contribution $C_V\propto T^3$, which the single-frequency <Einstein solid> cannot reproduce. This qualification concerns the vibrational contribution; additional excitations can change a material's total <heat capacity>. \a[https://davidtong.org/teaching/statistical-physics/statmechhtml/S3#S3.SS3][David Tong, Statistical Physics, section 3.3: acoustic phonons and the Debye low-temperature law]{ref}

Einstein introduced the quantum treatment of <solid> vibrations in his 1907 paper \i[Die Plancksche Theorie der Strahlung und die Theorie der spezifischen Wärme]. Its historical importance is that quantum excitation energies explained why the classical <Dulong-Petit law> could fail; the modern <quantum harmonic oscillator> formulation above includes the <zero-point energy> explicitly. \a[https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/10.1002/andp.19063270110][A. Einstein, Annalen der Physik 327, 180–190 (1907), original-paper publication metadata]{ref}

= Heat capacity at constant area
{title2=$C_A$}
{parent=Heat capacity}

For a two-dimensional system of fixed area $A$, the heat capacity at constant area is $C_A=(\partial E/\partial T)_{N,A}$ when the particle number and other conserved quantities are also fixed. It is the two-dimensional analogue of <heat capacity at constant volume>.

= Third law of thermodynamics
{c}
{parent=Thermodynamics}
{wiki}

The third law implies that the entropy of a system with a nondegenerate ground state approaches zero as its temperature approaches absolute zero. In particular, ordinary equilibrium heat capacities approach zero.

= Pressure
{parent=Thermodynamics}
{title2=$P=F_\perp/A$}
{wiki}

Pressure measures the compressive <normal force> per unit <surface area>. For a uniformly loaded surface of <surface area> $A$, the magnitude of the perpendicular <force> is $F_\perp=PA$; locally, $P=dF_\perp/dA$. Its <SI> unit is the pascal, $1\,\mathrm{Pa}=1\,\mathrm{N\,m^{-2}}$. A <force> parallel to the surface contributes shear <stress>, rather than <pressure>. In a <gas>, the steady macroscopic <force> comes from many microscopic transfers of <momentum>; <ideal gas pressure from wall collisions> makes this connection explicit. \a[https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-2/pages/2-2-pressure-temperature-and-rms-speed][OpenStax University Physics Volume 2, section 2.2: microscopic origin of pressure]{ref}

For a computed example, a uniform <pressure> of $100\,\mathrm{kPa}$ on a piston of <surface area> $10\,\mathrm{cm^2}=10^{-3}\,\mathrm{m^2}$ produces a perpendicular <force> of $100\,\mathrm N$. If the opposite face is exposed to $80\,\mathrm{kPa}$, the net <force> due to the two <pressures> is $(100-80)\times10^3\times10^{-3}=20\,\mathrm N$, directed toward the lower-<pressure> side. The <pressure> on either face and their difference answer different questions; <gauge pressure> records a difference from a specified reference, often <atmospheric pressure>.

A scalar <pressure> describes a <gas> in local <thermodynamic equilibrium> with <isotropic> random particle <velocities>: every wall orientation has the same normal <force> per unit <surface area>. Without this <isotropy>, normal <stresses> can depend on direction and a <pressure tensor> is needed. Thus random motion alone is insufficient; a preferred direction matters.

In <thermodynamics>, <pressure> and <volume (thermodynamics)> are <thermodynamic conjugate variables>. For a <simple compressible system>, reversible expansion delivers <work> $\delta W=P\,dV$, and at fixed <entropy> $S$ and particle number $N$,
$$
P=-\left(\frac{\partial U}{\partial V}\right)_{S,N},
$$
where $U$ is <internal energy>. A large <pressure> therefore means both a large wall <force> per unit <surface area> and a large amount of expansion <work> per small increase in <volume (thermodynamics)>.

= Atmospheric pressure
{parent=Pressure}
{wiki}
{title2=$p_{\rm atm}$}

<Atmospheric pressure> often provides the reference for a <gauge pressure> measurement: $p_g=p_{\rm abs}-p_{\rm atm}$. The absolute <pressure> is measured relative to an ideal <vacuum>, whereas zero <gauge pressure> means equality to the chosen reference. The actual <atmospheric pressure> can vary; it must be specified when converting between these descriptions. \a[https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-1/pages/14-2-measuring-pressure][OpenStax: atmospheric, gauge and absolute pressure]{ref}.

= Kinematic pressure
{parent=Pressure}
{title2=$p/\rho_0$}

Dividing a <pressure> disturbance by the constant reference <mass density> $\rho_0$ gives a quantity whose <gradient> enters the <Boussinesq equations> directly as an <acceleration>. Its units are squared <velocity>. Distinguish this convention from physical <pressure> when calculating <energy flux> or comparing <wave polarization> formulae.

= Pressure tensor
{parent=Pressure}
{title2=$P_{ij}$}

In the local rest frame of a gas, the <pressure tensor> is the directional <momentum flux> $P_{ij}=\int p_iv_jf(\mathbf p)\,d^3p$, where $f$ is the <phase-space distribution function> per unit <momentum> volume and $\mathbf v$ is particle velocity. For an isotropic distribution, $P_{ij}=P\delta_{ij}$, recovering the <kinetic pressure of an isotropic gas>. An anisotropic distribution can exert different normal <pressures> along different directions.

= Gas pressure
{title2=$p_{\rm gas}$}
{parent=Pressure}

Gas pressure is the mechanical pressure contributed by material particles. For a classical ideal gas, $p_{\rm gas}=nk_BT$.

= Radiation pressure
{title2=$p_{\rm rad}$}
{parent=Pressure}
{wiki}

Radiation pressure is momentum flux carried by electromagnetic radiation. Isotropic blackbody radiation has $p_{\rm rad}=a_{\rm rad}T^4/3$.

= Equation of state
{parent=Pressure}
{wiki}

= Equations of state
{synonym}

An equation of state is a relation among thermodynamic state variables such as pressure, volume, temperature, energy, and particle number.

= Barotropic stellar equation of state
{parent=Equation of state}
{title2=$p=p(\rho)$}

= Stellar barotropic equation of state
{synonym}

A <perfect fluid in general relativity> stellar model in which pressure depends only on energy density, $p=p(\rho)$. A positive derivative makes this relation locally invertible and turns outward-decreasing pressure into outward-decreasing density. This general relation need not have the constant ratio $p/\rho$ used for a constant cosmological fluid parameter.

= Clausius gas
{parent=Equation of state}
{c}

A Clausius gas with covolume parameter $b$ obeys $P(V-Nb)=Nk_BT$. It is the repulsive-volume part of a <Van der Waals equation> without the attractive term.

= Van der Waals equation
{c}
{parent=Equation of state}
{wiki}

The van der Waals equation $(P+aN^2/V^2)(V-Nb)=Nk_BT$ augments excluded volume $b$ with a mean attractive interaction $a$.

= Van der Waals law of corresponding states
{c}
{parent=Van der Waals equation}

The <Van der Waals equation> becomes independent of its material parameters when <pressure>, <temperature> and molecular <volume> are divided by their critical values: $\bar p=8\bar T/(3\bar v-1)-3/\bar v^2$. This explains the common reduced isotherms within this model, without claiming exact agreement for all real fluids.

= Equation of state of an ideal ultrarelativistic gas
{parent=Equation of state}

An isotropic ideal gas whose particles obey the ultrarelativistic dispersion $\varepsilon=pc$ satisfies
$$
pV=\frac E3.
$$

= Adiabatic equation of state
{parent=Equation of state}

An adiabatic equation of state relates pressure and volume along a process with no heat transfer. It commonly has the form $pV^\gamma=\text{constant}$.

= Volume
{disambiguate=thermodynamics}
{parent=Thermodynamics}
{wiki}

Thermodynamic volume is the <volume> occupied by a thermodynamic system, treated as an <extensive variable>.

= Internal energy
{parent=Thermodynamics}
{wiki}

Internal energy is the energy stored in the microscopic degrees of freedom of a system.

= Specific internal energy
{parent=Internal energy}
{title2=$e$}

Specific <internal energy> is <internal energy> per unit mass. For a <perfect gas> with constant <specific-heat ratio>, $e=p/[(\gamma-1)\rho]$. Its material balance is $\rho De/Dt=-p\nabla\cdot\mathbf u+Q$, where $Q$ is <heat> supplied per unit volume per unit time.

= Thermodynamic potential
{parent=Thermodynamics}
{wiki}

A <state function> used to describe a <thermodynamic system> with a chosen set of natural variables. <Internal energy>, <enthalpy>, <Helmholtz free energy>, and <Gibbs free energy> use different variables and serve different environmental constraints.

= Thermodynamic free energy
{parent=Thermodynamic potential}
{wiki}

= Free energy
{synonym}

A <free energy> is a <thermodynamic potential> that compares possible states under specified environmental constraints. For a <closed system> of fixed composition, treated as a <simple compressible system> with only <pressure>-<volume (thermodynamics)> <work>, stable <thermodynamic equilibrium> minimizes <Helmholtz free energy> at fixed <temperature> and <volume (thermodynamics)>, or <Gibbs free energy> at fixed <temperature> and <pressure>. The choice follows the experiment: a rigid sealed vessel and a sealed cylinder with a freely moving loaded piston impose different constraints. Both may exchange <heat> with a <thermal reservoir>. See \a[https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/3-00-thermodynamics-of-materials-fall-2002/14c95abd6fac023d84bd33c5d74d787d_lecture_02_oneside.PDF][W. C. Carter, MIT thermodynamics notes, sections 2-1.14 and 2-1.19]{ref}.

\Image[thermodynamic-potential-constraints.png]
{title=Choose <Helmholtz free energy> for fixed <temperature> and <volume (thermodynamics)>, or <Gibbs free energy> for fixed <temperature> and <pressure>}
{description=Original schematic of two closed vessels in contact with a <thermal reservoir>. Composition is fixed, and only <pressure>-<volume (thermodynamics)> <work> is allowed. The right piston permits the <volume (thermodynamics)> to change.}

The useful insight is that <internal energy> alone cannot rank these alternatives: exchanging <heat> also changes the surroundings' <entropy>. Subtracting $TS$ packages that accounting into a property of the chosen system. Adding $pV$ additionally accounts for displacing the surroundings at fixed <pressure>. A <free energy> is a <state function>; supplied <heat> describes a transfer during a process. Under the stated constraints, supplied <heat> is $Q=\Delta U$ at fixed <volume (thermodynamics)> or $Q=\Delta H$ at fixed <pressure>, whereas neither $\Delta F$ nor $\Delta G$ generally equals $Q$.

A lower <free energy> identifies a thermodynamic preference, without specifying a timescale. A barrier can trap a system in a <local minimum>, producing <metastability> although a lower <global minimum> exists. The <enzyme> example illustrates the corresponding distinction for <chemical reactions>: changing the route can change the <reaction rate> while preserving the endpoint <Gibbs free energy> difference and <chemical equilibrium>.

= Free-energy functional
{parent=Thermodynamic free energy}
{title2=$\mathcal A[m]$}

A <free-energy functional> assigns a <free energy> to an entire spatial <order parameter> configuration rather than only to one uniform value. In <Landau-Ginzburg theory>, a <local derivative expansion> gives $\mathcal A[m]=\int d^Dx\{\kappa(\nabla m)^2/2+V(m)\}$, with appropriate boundary terms and regular backgrounds. The <Landau approximation> minimizes this functional; a fluctuating <statistical field theory> instead integrates its <Boltzmann weight> over configurations. These procedures need not give identical <critical exponents>.

= Helmholtz free energy
{title2=$F$}
{parent=Thermodynamic free energy}
{c}
{wiki}

= Helmholtz energy
{c}
{synonym}

The <Helmholtz free energy> is
$$
F=U-TS,
$$
where $U$ is <internal energy>, also denoted $E$ elsewhere here, $T$ is absolute <temperature>, and $S$ is <entropy>. For the <closed system> of fixed composition and only <pressure>-<volume (thermodynamics)> <work> described above, spontaneous relaxation at fixed <temperature> and <volume (thermodynamics)> cannot increase $F$. Stable <thermodynamic equilibrium> is a <global minimum> over the permitted alternatives, including different <thermodynamic phases>. A rigid vessel fixes the total <volume (thermodynamics)>; it does not require each coexisting <thermodynamic phase> to retain its own <volume (thermodynamics)>.

For a constructed comparison at $300\,\mathrm K$, suppose one candidate has $1.5\,\mathrm{kJ}$ more <internal energy> but $6\,\mathrm{J\,K^{-1}}$ more <entropy> than another at the same <temperature> and <volume (thermodynamics)>. Its difference is
$$
\Delta F=1500-300(6)=-300\,\mathrm J.
$$
Thus the candidate with higher <internal energy> has lower <Helmholtz free energy>. This is the practical reason for retaining the <entropy> term when selecting a state.

For equilibrium states within a smooth branch of fixed composition, the <first law of thermodynamics> gives $dU=T\,dS-p\,dV$, hence
$$
dF=-S\,dT-p\,dV,\qquad
S=-\left(\frac{\partial F}{\partial T}\right)_V,\qquad
p=-\left(\frac{\partial F}{\partial V}\right)_T.
$$
These <derivatives> extract <entropy> and <pressure>; the differential is not an evolution equation for relaxation. If <chemical species> amounts $n_i$ vary, add $\sum_i\mu_i\,dn_i$, with molar <chemical potentials> $\mu_i$. A <closed system> can still change <chemical species> amounts through <chemical reactions>; the fixed-composition simplification then needs the conserved-inventory constraints of <chemical equilibrium>. See \a[https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/5-60-thermodynamics-kinetics-spring-2008/e763a06aa3b5de09cd6bfea6280f219c_5_60_lecture14.pdf][MIT 5.60, Lecture 14, fundamental equations and chemical potentials]{ref}.

The minimum principle has a short explanation. At fixed <volume (thermodynamics)>, no boundary <work> occurs, so $Q=\Delta U$. A <thermal reservoir> at positive <temperature> $T$ loses <entropy> $Q/T$. Consequently
$$
\Delta S_{\rm total}=\Delta S-\frac{\Delta U}{T}=-\frac{\Delta F}{T}\geq0,
$$
by the <second law of thermodynamics>. Here both endpoint states share the imposed <temperature>. See \a[https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/5-60-thermodynamics-kinetics-spring-2008/64efc6f359b20c43dab61fc77791d744_5_60_lecture12.pdf][MIT 5.60, Lecture 12, criteria for spontaneous change]{ref}.

The microscopic counterpart is $F=-k_BT\ln Z$ in the <canonical ensemble>, with <Boltzmann constant> $k_B$ and <partition function> $Z$. The <partition function> examples compute the balance between <internal energy> and <entropy>; the <chemical equilibrium> example already displays an ideal-mixture <Helmholtz free energy> minimum.

= Gibbs free energy
{parent=Thermodynamic free energy}
{c}
{title2=$G$}
{wiki}

= Gibbs energy
{c}
{synonym}

The <Gibbs free energy> is
$$
G=U-TS+pV=H-TS,\qquad H=U+pV,
$$
where $H$ is <enthalpy>. For a <closed system> of fixed composition, with only <pressure>-<volume (thermodynamics)> <work>, spontaneous relaxation at fixed <temperature> and <pressure> cannot increase $G$; stable <thermodynamic equilibrium> minimizes it over the allowed states. A loaded piston can move while maintaining the imposed <pressure>, so the <enthalpy> term includes the displacement cost. Along a smooth equilibrium branch of fixed composition,
$$
dG=-S\,dT+V\,dp.
$$
Thus its <temperature> slope at fixed <pressure> is $-S$, while its <pressure> slope at fixed <temperature> is $V$. See \a[https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/5-60-thermodynamics-kinetics-spring-2008/7bc33304ab1db561a64b4a6b12e25476_5_60_lecture13.pdf][MIT 5.60, Lecture 13, Gibbs free energy and its derivatives]{ref}.

Consider two illustrative <thermodynamic phases>, A and B, of the same substance at one fixed <pressure>. Write B minus A, using quantities per <mole>:
$$
\Delta H=12\,\mathrm{kJ\,mol^{-1}},\qquad
\Delta S=40\,\mathrm{J\,mol^{-1}\,K^{-1}}=0.040\,\mathrm{kJ\,mol^{-1}\,K^{-1}}.
$$
Treat these differences as approximately constant over a limited <temperature> window. Then
$$
\frac{\Delta G(T)}{\mathrm{kJ\,mol^{-1}}}=12-0.040\frac{T}{\mathrm K},\qquad
T_* =\frac{\Delta H}{\Delta S}=300\,\mathrm K.
$$
At $280\,\mathrm K$, $\Delta G=+0.8\,\mathrm{kJ\,mol^{-1}}$, so A is preferred. At $320\,\mathrm K$, $\Delta G=-0.8\,\mathrm{kJ\,mol^{-1}}$, so B is preferred. At $300\,\mathrm K$, both have equal molar <Gibbs free energy>, permitting <phase coexistence> in this bulk model. B's greater <entropy> outweighs its greater <enthalpy> above the crossing.

\Image[free-energy-temperature-crossing.png]
{title=Illustrative molar <Gibbs free energy> difference crosses zero at $300\,\mathrm K$}
{description=Constructed B-minus-A values: $+0.8\,\mathrm{kJ\,mol^{-1}}$ at $280\,\mathrm K$ and $-0.8\,\mathrm{kJ\,mol^{-1}}$ at $320\,\mathrm K$. The constants approximate a limited <temperature> window and represent no measured substance.}

Each plotted point compares alternatives at the same <temperature>; moving along the curve changes the imposed <temperature>. The model predicts preference, not instantaneous conversion: <nucleation> barriers can sustain <metastability>. Real <enthalpy> and <entropy> differences generally vary with <temperature> through <heat capacities>, bending this line outside the approximation.

At fixed <pressure>, the <first law of thermodynamics> gives $Q=\Delta H$ under the stated <work> restriction, so the same <thermal reservoir> accounting yields $\Delta S_{\rm total}=-\Delta G/T\geq0$. Variable composition restores $\sum_i\mu_i\,dn_i$ to $dG$; <constrained Gibbs minimization for chemical equilibrium> handles those permitted changes.

The historical foundation is Gibbs's \a[https://en.wikisource.org/wiki/Scientific_Papers_of_Josiah_Willard_Gibbs,_Volume_1/Chapter_IIIa][On the Equilibrium of Heterogeneous Substances, first part completed in 1876, continued in 1878]{ref}. Its equations (87) and (91) introduce the combinations now called <Helmholtz free energy> and <Gibbs free energy>, connecting <entropy> criteria, <chemical potentials> and <phase coexistence> within one framework.

= Gibbs free energy of an ideal-gas mixture
{c}
{parent=Gibbs free energy}

For a single-phase <ideal gas> mixture with molar amounts $N_i$, common <temperature> $T$, and <pressure> $P$, extensivity and ideal mixing give
$$
G=\sum_iN_i\mu_i=N\sum_i x_i\left[\mu_i^\circ(T)+\mathcal RT\log\left(\frac{x_iP}{P^\circ}\right)\right].
$$
Here $N=\sum_iN_i$, $x_i$ is the <mole fraction>, $\mathcal R$ is the molar gas constant, and $P^\circ$ is the standard <pressure>. Integrating $\partial\mu_i/\partial P=\mathcal RT/P$ and including the ideal mixing <entropy> proves $\mu_i=\mu_i^\circ+\mathcal RT\log(x_iP/P^\circ)$. Reactions can change $N$, so fractions alone do not set the extensive <Gibbs free energy>. Its composition Hessian is $\mathcal RT(\delta_{ij}/N_i-1/N)$; it is positive semidefinite by the weighted <Cauchy-Schwarz inequality>.

= Gibbs free-energy Maxwell relation
{c}
{parent=Gibbs free energy}

Equality of mixed derivatives of $dG=-S\,dT+V\,dP$ gives
$$
\left(\frac{\partial S}{\partial P}\right)_T
=-\left(\frac{\partial V}{\partial T}\right)_P.
$$

= Chemical-potential balance for a reaction
{parent=Gibbs free energy}

For a reaction with stoichiometric changes $\nu_i$, varying the reaction extent at fixed temperature and pressure gives
$$
dG=\left(\sum_i\nu_i\mu_i\right)d\xi.
$$
Equilibrium requires $\sum_i\nu_i\mu_i=0$.

= Thermochemical equilibrium
{parent=Chemical-potential balance for a reaction}

Thermochemical equilibrium requires every chemical reaction to have zero Gibbs free-energy change. For ideal reactants, the equilibrium constant is $K=\exp[-\Delta_rG^\circ/(RT)]$ after standard-state activity factors are included.

= Constrained Gibbs minimization for chemical equilibrium
{parent=Thermochemical equilibrium}

At fixed <temperature> and <pressure>, a closed reacting system reaches <thermochemical equilibrium> by minimizing <Gibbs free energy> over nonnegative species amounts subject to conserved elemental abundances. For an allowed <stoichiometric vector> $\nu$, an interior minimum has $\sum_i\nu_i\mu_i=0$. More generally the <chemical potential> vector lies in the row span of the elemental conservation matrix at positive concentrations, with the appropriate one-sided inequalities for absent species. The <first law of thermodynamics> gives the thermodynamic differential, while the <second law of thermodynamics> supplies the minimization direction.

= Reverse reaction rate from equilibrium thermodynamics
{parent=Thermochemical equilibrium}

For an elementary reversible reaction, detailed balance gives $k_f\prod_r n_r^{\nu_r}=k_r\prod_p n_p^{\nu_p}$ at equilibrium. Hence the reverse rate coefficient is the forward coefficient divided by the equilibrium constant, with units fixed by the reaction stoichiometry.

= Phase coexistence curve
{parent=Gibbs free energy}

Two phases coexist where their molar Gibbs free energies agree. Differentiating that equality along the coexistence curve relates its slope to the entropy and volume jumps.

= Maxwell construction
{c}
{parent=Phase coexistence curve}

= Maxwell equal-area construction
{c}
{synonym}

At fixed <temperature>, choose a coexistence <pressure> $p_*$ such that the gas and liquid have equal <chemical potentials>. Because $d\mu=v\,dp$, this gives $\int_{v_l}^{v_g}v\,dp=0$, or equivalently $\int_{v_l}^{v_g}[p(v,T)-p_*]\,dv=0$. The equal areas replace the unstable part of an isotherm by a horizontal coexistence segment.

= Phase diagram
{parent=Phase coexistence curve}
{wiki}

A phase diagram partitions a space of thermodynamic variables into regions occupied by different equilibrium phases and records the coexistence curves between them.

= Solidus
{parent=Phase diagram}
{title2=$T_S(C)$}

The solidus bounds the entirely solid equilibrium region of a <phase diagram>. For a linear <liquidus> $T_L(C_l)=-mC_l$ and a constant <segregation coefficient> $C_s=k_D C_l$, the corresponding solidus is $T_S(C_s)=-mC_s/k_D$. The <concentration> argument is the solid <concentration>, not the neighboring liquid <concentration>.

= Lever rule
{parent=Phase diagram}
{title2=$\phi=(\overline C-C_s)/(C_l-C_s)$}
{wiki}

The lever rule finds phase fractions from a horizontal tie line on a <phase diagram>. For solid and liquid <concentrations> $C_s,C_l$ and bulk <concentration> $\overline C$, conservation gives $\overline C=(1-\phi)C_s+\phi C_l$. Thus $\phi=(\overline C-C_s)/(C_l-C_s)$ is the <liquid fraction>, provided $C_s\le\overline C\le C_l$. For pure solid, $\phi=\overline C/C_l$.

= Bicritical point
{parent=Phase diagram}

A bicritical point is where two continuous transition lines and a first-order line meet, typically separating a disordered phase from two competing ordered phases.

= Liquidus
{parent=Phase diagram}
{wiki}

The liquidus is the locus in a <phase diagram> above which the equilibrium state is entirely liquid. In a dilute binary solution it is often approximated locally by a linear relation such as $T_L=T_0-mC$.

= Freezing-point depression
{parent=Liquidus}
{wiki}

Freezing-point depression is the lowering of a solvent's equilibrium freezing temperature by dissolved solute. In the linear dilute approximation, $T_L=T_0-mC$ with $m>0$.

= Eutectic system
{parent=Phase diagram}
{wiki}

An eutectic system has a composition and temperature at which a liquid is in equilibrium with two distinct solid phases. Cooling liquid of that composition through the eutectic temperature produces both solid phases together.

= Eutectic composition
{parent=Eutectic system}
{title2=$C_E$}

The eutectic composition is the liquid <concentration> at the <eutectic temperature>, where liquid and two solid phases coexist. It is a composition coordinate on a <phase diagram>, not the overall <concentration> of every sample in an <eutectic system>.

= Eutectic temperature
{title2=$T_E$}
{parent=Eutectic system}

The eutectic temperature is the temperature of simultaneous liquid–two-solid <phase coexistence> in an <eutectic system>. In directional freezing of a solution away from the eutectic composition, a <mushy layer> can end at this temperature, where its remaining liquid solidifies.

= First-order phase transition
{parent=Phase coexistence curve}

At a first-order phase transition, the Gibbs free energy is continuous while at least one first derivative, such as entropy or volume, jumps. A nonzero entropy jump produces latent heat $L=T\Delta S$.

= Two-phase scalar coexistence endpoint conditions
{parent=First-order phase transition}
{title2=$V'=V''=V^{(3)}=0,\quad V^{(4)}>0$}

A smooth scalar <Landau free energy> can end a two-phase <phase coexistence curve> by merging two minima and their intervening maximum. At a generic stable endpoint, its first three derivatives vanish and its fourth derivative is positive. A field shift and two mixed control parameters reduce the local form to a quartic <Landau free energy>; the order-parameter jump then vanishes and the <magnetic susceptibility> diverges. This conclusion assumes there is no competing third phase and that the endpoint lies inside the control-parameter domain. It is not a general theorem about every first-order line: a sextic potential with fixed negative quartic coefficient has a symmetry-related ordered coexistence line that ends at a triple point with a finite jump.

= Spin-flop transition
{parent=First-order phase transition}

= Classical nucleation theory
{parent=First-order phase transition}
{wiki}

Classical nucleation theory balances the positive surface free energy of a droplet against its negative bulk free-energy gain. In three dimensions,
$$
F(R)=4\pi\sigma R^2-\frac{4\pi}{3}\Delta R^3
$$
has critical radius $R^*=2\sigma/\Delta$ and barrier $F^*=16\pi\sigma^3/(3\Delta^2)$.

= Critical nucleus
{parent=Classical nucleation theory}

A critical nucleus is the unstable droplet at the maximum of the nucleation free energy. Smaller droplets shrink, while larger droplets lower their free energy by growing.

= Arrhenius nucleation time
{parent=Classical nucleation theory}

Thermal crossing of a nucleation barrier $F^*$ has rate proportional to $e^{-F^*/(k_BT)}$ and mean waiting time proportional to $e^{F^*/(k_BT)}$, up to a kinetic prefactor.

= Latent heat
{parent=Phase coexistence curve}
{title2=$L=T\Delta S=\Delta H$}
{wiki}

Latent heat is the <heat> absorbed or released in a <first-order phase transition> through its <entropy> jump. For an equilibrium conversion from <thermodynamic phase> $\alpha$ to <thermodynamic phase> $\beta$ at fixed <pressure>, evaluate both <thermodynamic phases> at the same <temperature> and <pressure> on their <phase coexistence curve>:
$$
L=T_{\rm tr}(S_\beta-S_\alpha)=H_\beta-H_\alpha.
$$
Use one normalization throughout: $L$, $S$ and $H$ may all be per <mole>, giving $L$ in $\mathrm{J\,mol^{-1}}$, or all per unit <mass>, giving $L$ in $\mathrm{J\,kg^{-1}}$. Here $S$ is <entropy> and $H$ is <enthalpy> on that basis. Equal <Gibbs free energies> of the coexisting <thermodynamic phases> give $\Delta H=T_{\rm tr}\Delta S$. Identifying this <enthalpy> difference with supplied <heat> assumes a <closed system> and an equilibrium conversion with only <pressure>-<volume (thermodynamics)> <work>. \a[https://davidtong.org/teaching/statistical-physics/statmechhtml/S5][Tong, Statistical Physics, phase coexistence and latent heat]{ref}

For a pure substance with positive <latent heat> per unit <mass> $L$, let $x$ be the <phase fraction> of the higher-<enthalpy> <thermodynamic phase>, measured by <mass>. While both <thermodynamic phases> coexist at the fixed <pressure>,
$$
Q=mL(x_2-x_1),\qquad T=T_{\rm tr}.
$$
Adding <heat> changes $x$ rather than the <temperature>; this creates the horizontal portion of a <heating curve>. Once $x=1$, additional <heat> raises the <temperature> according to <specific heat capacity at constant pressure>. Reversing the conversion releases <heat>. The plateau represents changing <enthalpy>, rather than a finite single-<thermodynamic phase> <heat capacity> satisfying $Q=C_p\Delta T$. \a[https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-2/pages/1-5-phase-changes][OpenStax, Phase changes and heating curves]{ref}

For a constructed fixed-<pressure> model, take $m=1\,\mathrm{kg}$, initial <temperature> $280\,\mathrm K$, transition <temperature> $T_{\rm tr}=300\,\mathrm K$, <solid> <specific heat capacity at constant pressure> $c_s=2\,\mathrm{kJ\,kg^{-1}\,K^{-1}}$, <latent heat> $L=100\,\mathrm{kJ\,kg^{-1}}$, and <liquid> <specific heat capacity at constant pressure> $c_l=3\,\mathrm{kJ\,kg^{-1}\,K^{-1}}$. These are stipulated model parameters, not empirical properties of a named material. Heating the <solid> to $300\,\mathrm K$ takes $40\,\mathrm{kJ}$ of <sensible heat>; converting it fully to <liquid> takes another $100\,\mathrm{kJ}$ of <heat>; heating the <liquid> to $320\,\mathrm K$ takes $60\,\mathrm{kJ}$. The total is $200\,\mathrm{kJ}$. After a total <heat> input of $90\,\mathrm{kJ}$, $x=(90-40)/100=1/2$ and the <temperature> is still $300\,\mathrm K$. The corresponding per-unit-<mass> <entropy> jump is $L/T_{\rm tr}=\frac13\,\mathrm{kJ\,kg^{-1}\,K^{-1}}$.

\Image[sensible-and-latent-heat.png]
{title=<Sensible heat> raises <temperature>; <latent heat> changes <phase fraction>}
{description=Constructed $1\,\mathrm{kg}$ fixed-<pressure> model with total supplied <heat> starting at zero at $280\,\mathrm K$. The plateau runs from $40$ to $140\,\mathrm{kJ}$, while the <liquid> <phase fraction> by <mass> rises from zero to one. The horizontal axis measures $Q=\Delta H$, rather than absolute <internal energy>.}

A <continuous phase transition> has no <latent heat>. Its <heat capacity> can have a jump or a singularity without a finite interval in which added <heat> converts coexisting <thermodynamic phases> at a constant <temperature>. A heating plateau therefore requires the stated <phase coexistence> conditions; it is not a feature of every <phase transition>. \a[https://davidtong.org/teaching/statistical-physics/statmechhtml/S5][Tong, Statistical Physics, first-order and continuous transitions]{ref}

= Stefan number
{c}
{title2=$\mathrm{Ste}=c_p\Delta T/L$}
{parent=Latent heat}
{wiki}

The Stefan number compares sensible heat $c_p\Delta T$ with <latent heat> $L$ per unit mass. Some solidification literature uses its reciprocal as the Stefan parameter, so the defining formula must accompany the convention.

= Latent-to-sensible heat ratio
{title2=$S=L/(c_p\Delta T)$}
{parent=Stefan number}

= Inverse Stefan number
{synonym}

The latent-to-sensible heat ratio is the reciprocal of the <Stefan number> defined by $\mathrm{Ste}=c_p\Delta T/L$. Large $S$ means that phase change uses much more energy than changing temperature by $\Delta T$.

= Clausius-Clapeyron relation
{parent=Latent heat}
{c}
{wiki=Clausius–Clapeyron_relation}

Along a first-order coexistence curve,
$$
\frac{dp}{dT}
=\frac{S_\beta-S_\alpha}{V_\beta-V_\alpha}
=\frac{L}{T(V_\beta-V_\alpha)}.
$$
At a critical point the two phases merge, their entropy jump vanishes, and the latent heat tends to zero.

= Common-pressure melting-point slope
{parent=Clausius-Clapeyron relation}
{title2=$dT_m/dp=-T_m(1/\rho_s-1/\rho_l)/L$}

When the solid and liquid have the same <pressure>, equality of <chemical potentials> implies $-(s_l-s_s)dT+(1/\rho_l-1/\rho_s)dp=0$. With <latent heat of fusion> $L=T_m(s_l-s_s)$, this gives the displayed slope. If solid is less dense than liquid, raising the common pressure depresses the <melting> temperature. $T_m$ is absolute <temperature>. This coexistence-curve derivative differs from a thermomolecular pressure difference between equal-density phases.

= Chemical potential
{parent=Thermodynamics}
{wiki}
{title2=$\mu$}

The <chemical potential> measures how a <thermodynamic potential> changes when matter is added under specified constraints. It plays for matter exchange the role that <temperature> plays for <heat> exchange: a difference supplies a direction, and equality characterizes <thermodynamic equilibrium> when the corresponding exchange is allowed. This connects <diffusion>, <phase coexistence> and <chemical equilibrium> through one quantity.

For a single component, the particle convention is
$$
\mu_{\rm particle}=\left(\frac{\partial U}{\partial N}\right)_{S,V},
$$
where $U$ is <internal energy>, $N$ counts particles, $S$ is <entropy> and $V$ is <volume (thermodynamics)>. This <partial derivative> holds <entropy> and <volume (thermodynamics)> fixed; it measures an incremental change rather than the average <energy> per particle. See \a[https://davidtong.org/pdfs/teaching/statistical-physics/statphys1.pdf][David Tong, Statistical Physics, section 1.4]{ref}.

In <chemistry> the molar convention instead uses <amount of substance> $n$, measured in <moles>:
$$
\mu_{\rm m}=\left(\frac{\partial G}{\partial n}\right)_{T,p},
\qquad n=\frac{N}{N_{\rm A}},
\qquad \mu_{\rm m}=N_{\rm A}\mu_{\rm particle}.
$$
Here $G$ is <Gibbs free energy>, $T$ is <temperature>, $p$ is <pressure> and $N_{\rm A}$ is the <Avogadro constant>. The units are $\mathrm J$ per particle versus $\mathrm{J\,mol^{-1}}$. For several <chemical species>, define each <chemical potential> by varying only its own particle count or <amount of substance>, with all other components' counts or amounts fixed. Many texts use $\mu$ for either convention, so the units and the variable being differentiated must identify it.

For a <simple compressible system> with variable component inventories, the fundamental differentials are
$$
dU=T\,dS-p\,dV+\sum_i\mu_{{\rm particle},i}\,dN_i,
\qquad
dG=-S\,dT+V\,dp+\sum_i\mu_{{\rm m},i}\,dn_i.
$$
They describe changes between states in <thermodynamic equilibrium> with only <pressure>-<volume (thermodynamics)> <work>. If every component inventory is fixed, the sums vanish. The <Gibbs free energy> form follows by differentiating $G=U-TS+pV$ and using $dN_i=N_{\rm A}\,dn_i$; this is why the two definitions describe the same <chemical potential> in different units.

A <chemical potential> can be negative. Holding <entropy> fixed while adding matter need not increase <internal energy>; Tong explains this explicitly for a classical <ideal gas> in \a[https://davidtong.org/pdfs/teaching/statistical-physics/statphys2.pdf][Statistical Physics, section 2.2.4]{ref}. Its absolute sign also depends on the reference <energy>: replacing $U$ by $U+\epsilon_0N$ shifts $\mu_{\rm particle}$ by $\epsilon_0$. A common reference shift cancels from differences for the same <chemical species>, which are what determine exchange. Thus “energy cost” means a signed increment, not an always positive amount that must be supplied.

= Chemical potential equality for particle exchange
{parent=Chemical potential}
{title2=$\mu_{{\rm m},A}=\mu_{{\rm m},B}$}

Consider two internally equilibrated reservoirs A and B at the same imposed <temperature> and <pressure>. They may have different compositions or represent different phases. Transfer a small positive <amount of substance> $\delta n$ of the same <chemical species> from A to B, holding the other inventories fixed. To first order, their total <Gibbs free energy> changes by
$$
\delta G_{\rm total}
=-\mu_{{\rm m},A}\delta n+\mu_{{\rm m},B}\delta n
=(\mu_{{\rm m},B}-\mu_{{\rm m},A})\delta n.
$$
Hence exchange toward lower <chemical potential> lowers <Gibbs free energy>. If transfer is allowed in both directions and the two reservoirs contain the exchangeable component, <thermodynamic equilibrium> requires equal <chemical potentials> for that component. See \a[https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/5-60-thermodynamics-kinetics-spring-2008/e763a06aa3b5de09cd6bfea6280f219c_5_60_lecture14.pdf][MIT 5.60, Lecture 14, pages 1–2]{ref}.

An impermeable partition can maintain unequal <chemical potentials>; equality is a condition on an allowed exchange. Two pure samples of the same <ideal gas> already have equal <chemical potentials> at the same <temperature> and <pressure>. Different compositions at a common <pressure> can instead give different <chemical potentials> for one component through its <thermodynamic activity>.

Equality concerns the same exchangeable component in different places, not all different <chemical species>. For a permitted <chemical reaction>, the corresponding interior <chemical equilibrium> criterion is $\sum_i\nu_i\mu_{{\rm m},i}=0$, with signed <stoichiometric vector> entries $\nu_i$. The existing <chemical equilibrium> and <constrained Gibbs minimization for chemical equilibrium> sections develop those composition constraints.

= Chemical potential and Helmholtz free energy
{parent=Chemical potential equality for particle exchange}
{title2=$\mu_{\rm m}=(\partial F/\partial n)_{T,V}$}

Rigid compartments impose a different constraint: fixed <volume (thermodynamics)> rather than fixed <pressure>. With <Helmholtz free energy> $F=U-TS$,
$$
dF=-S\,dT-p\,dV+\sum_i\mu_{{\rm m},i}\,dn_i,
\qquad
\mu_{{\rm m},i}=\left(\frac{\partial F}{\partial n_i}\right)_{T,V,n_{j\ne i}}.
$$
This follows immediately by differentiating $F=U-TS$ in the <chemical potential> differential above. It expresses the same <chemical potential> through the <thermodynamic potential> appropriate to fixed <temperature> and <volume (thermodynamics)>.

Consider a <closed system> consisting of two rigid compartments of equal <volume (thermodynamics)> $V$, maintained at a common fixed <temperature> $T$. A valve permits exchange of the same <ideal gas>; the partition stays in place. Initially $n_A=9\,\mathrm{mol}$ and $n_B=3\,\mathrm{mol}$, with conserved total <amount of substance> $n_{\rm tot}=12\,\mathrm{mol}$. These are macroscopic <moles>, modeled continuously, not twelve individual particles. By the <ideal gas law>, $p_B/p_A=n_B/n_A=1/3$, so initially
$$
\mu_{{\rm m},B}-\mu_{{\rm m},A}=-RT\ln3.
$$
At $300\,\mathrm K$ with the stipulated $R$, this is approximately $-2740.311\,\mathrm{J\,mol^{-1}}$, favoring transfer from A to B.

At fixed $T$ and equal $V$, integrating the ideal-gas <chemical potential> gives the total <Helmholtz free energy>, up to a term independent of the allocation,
$$
F_{\rm total}
=RT\left[n_A\ln\frac{n_A}{n_{\rm ref}}+n_B\ln\frac{n_B}{n_{\rm ref}}-n_{\rm tot}\right]+C.
$$
Here $n_{\rm ref}$ is a positive reference <amount of substance>; any omitted common term linear in $n_A+n_B$ is constant along this exchange. Let $x=n_A/n_{\rm tot}$ be the <dimensionless> fraction in A. The relative <Helmholtz free energy> then takes the particularly simple form
$$
\frac{F_{\rm total}(x)-F_{\rm total}(1/2)}{n_{\rm tot}RT}
=x\ln x+(1-x)\ln(1-x)+\ln2.
$$
Its <derivative> along the allowed transfer and its second <derivative> are
$$
\frac{dF_{\rm total}}{dn_A}
=\mu_{{\rm m},A}-\mu_{{\rm m},B}
=RT\ln\frac{x}{1-x},
\qquad
\frac{d^2F_{\rm total}}{dn_A^2}
=RT\left(\frac{1}{n_A}+\frac{1}{n_B}\right)>0.
$$
Thus the <Helmholtz free energy> is a strictly <convex function> of the allocation for positive amounts. Its unique minimum has $x=1/2$, or $n_A=n_B=6\,\mathrm{mol}$, and equal <chemical potentials>. Initially its excess above this minimum is $n_{\rm tot}RT[\tfrac34\ln(3/2)+\tfrac14\ln(1/2)]\simeq3915.474\,\mathrm J$ at $300\,\mathrm K$. Relaxation decreases the total <Helmholtz free energy> by this amount in the model; the final <pressures> are also equal because both compartments have the same <volume (thermodynamics)>.

\Image[chemical-potential-particle-transfer.png]
{title=<Chemical potential> differences are slopes of the total <Helmholtz free energy>}
{description=Two equal rigid compartments at fixed <temperature> start with $9\,\mathrm{mol}$ and $3\,\mathrm{mol}$ of the same <ideal gas>, and reach $6\,\mathrm{mol}$ each after exchange. Lower left: the <dimensionless> excess <Helmholtz free energy>. Lower right: its <derivative> with respect to the allocation, divided by $RT$, equals the <chemical potential> difference divided by $RT$. The horizontal variable is $x=n_A/n_{\rm tot}$.}

The beautiful connection is that the direction of matter exchange and the slope of a <free energy> curve are the same calculation. The <entropy> gained by redistributing the <ideal gas> makes equal allocation favorable here. As with <chemical equilibrium>, a <free energy> minimum identifies the favored state, while transport properties determine how quickly it is reached. The fixed-<temperature>, fixed-<pressure> <Gibbs free energy> criterion is summarized in \a[https://openstax.org/books/chemistry-2e/pages/16-4-free-energy][OpenStax Chemistry 2e, section 16.4]{ref}; the rigid experiment instead uses <Helmholtz free energy>.

= Chemical potential of an ideal gas
{parent=Chemical potential}
{title2=$\mu_{\rm m}=\mu_{\rm m}^\circ+RT\ln(p/p^\circ)$}

For a pure <ideal gas> at <temperature> $T$ and <pressure> $p$,
$$
\mu_{\rm m}(T,p)=\mu_{\rm m}^\circ(T)+RT\ln\frac{p}{p^\circ},
$$
where $R$ is the <molar gas constant> and $p^\circ$ specifies the reference <pressure> of the <standard state>. The ratio inside the <natural logarithm> is <dimensionless>. The <chemical potential> difference therefore depends on a ratio of <pressures>, not an absolute difference. See \a[https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/5-60-thermodynamics-kinetics-spring-2008/e763a06aa3b5de09cd6bfea6280f219c_5_60_lecture14.pdf][MIT 5.60, Lecture 14, page 4]{ref}.

For a <chemical species> in an ideal <gas> <mixture>, replace $p$ by its <partial pressure>. More generally write $\mu_{{\rm m},i}=\mu_{{\rm m},i}^\circ+RT\ln a_i$ using its <thermodynamic activity> $a_i$ and a consistent <standard state>. For an ideal dilute <chemical solution>, $a_i=c_i/c^\circ$ with reference <concentration> $c^\circ$; the <thermodynamic activity> section describes departures from this idealization.

As a computed comparison, take identical <chemical species> at $T=300\,\mathrm K$, with $p_A=2p^\circ$ and $p_B=p^\circ$. Stipulate $R=8.314462618\,\mathrm{J\,mol^{-1}\,K^{-1}}$. Then
$$
\mu_{{\rm m},B}-\mu_{{\rm m},A}
=RT\ln\frac{p_B}{p_A}
=-RT\ln2
\simeq-1728.944\,\mathrm{J\,mol^{-1}}.
$$
Halving the <pressure> gives the same decrement whether starting from $2p^\circ$ or $20p^\circ$, provided the <ideal gas> approximation remains valid. This comparison uses different <pressures>; it is not the common-<pressure> reservoir setup of <chemical potential equality for particle exchange>. The fixed-<volume (thermodynamics)> example in <chemical potential and Helmholtz free energy> supplies an exchange experiment with unequal initial <pressures>.

\Image[chemical-potential-and-pressure.png]
{title=<Ideal gas> <chemical potential> difference versus <pressure> ratio}
{description=At a common <temperature>, the <chemical potential> difference is $RT\ln(p_B/p_A)$. The arrows give the initial transfer direction through an open route between rigid compartments; the decreasing <thermodynamic potential> there is <Helmholtz free energy>. The marked comparison uses $300\,\mathrm K$ and the stipulated <molar gas constant>.}

= Electrochemical potential
{parent=Chemical potential}
{wiki}
{title2=$\widetilde\mu_{\rm m}=\mu_{\rm m}+zF_{\rm Far}\psi$}

The molar <electrochemical potential> combines the molar <chemical potential> $\mu_{\rm m}$ with the electrical contribution $zF_{\rm Far}\psi$, where $z$ is the signed charge number of an <ion>, $F_{\rm Far}=N_{\rm A}e$ is the <Faraday constant> and $\psi$ is <electric potential>. Both terms have units $\mathrm{J\,mol^{-1}}$. In the particle convention the corresponding expression is $\widetilde\mu_{\rm particle}=\mu_{\rm particle}+ze\psi$, with <elementary charge> $e$; multiplying it by the <Avogadro constant> gives the molar form. Transfer between locations at the same <temperature> and <pressure> is driven by differences in <electrochemical potential>. The <membrane transport> examples use it to distinguish neutral <solute> transfer from <ion> transfer.

= Electrochemical gradient
{parent=Electrochemical potential}
{wiki}
{title2=$\nabla\widetilde\mu_{\rm m}$}

An <electrochemical gradient> combines spatial changes in <chemical potential> and <electric potential> for a charged species. <Passive transport> proceeds downhill in <electrochemical potential> when an appropriate route is available; a <concentration> difference alone does not determine the direction for an <ion>.

= Chemical potential of a composition field
{parent=Chemical potential}
{title2=$\mu=\delta F/\delta\phi$}

For a composition <free energy> $F=\int[f(\phi)+\kappa|\nabla\phi|^2/2]$, the local chemical potential is the <functional derivative>
$$
\mu=f'(\phi)-\kappa\nabla^2\phi.
$$
At <phase coexistence>, exchanges between the bulk phases require equal <chemical potentials>. Mechanical equilibrium additionally requires equal <pressure> for a flat interface, or a <Young–Laplace equation> pressure jump for a curved interface.

= Chemical equilibrium
{parent=Chemical potential}
{wiki}

At <chemical equilibrium>, a reversible <chemical reaction> has equal forward and reverse <reaction rates>, so its macroscopic composition stays constant. Individual <molecules> can keep changing identity: the reaction has not stopped. This dynamic balance is the definition described by the \a[https://goldbook.iupac.org/terms/view/C01023][IUPAC Gold Book, chemical equilibrium]{ref}. Equal <reaction rates> also need not mean equal <concentrations>. A small population converting rapidly can balance a large population converting slowly.

This distinction gives two useful questions. Which composition is favored at the specified <temperature> and other constraints? <Thermodynamics> answers through <chemical potentials> and an <equilibrium constant>. How quickly does a prepared mixture approach that composition? <Chemical kinetics> answers through <reaction rates>. Knowing the destination does not determine the journey's duration. A composition that barely changes during observation may reflect extremely slow <chemical kinetics>, so constancy alone does not establish <chemical equilibrium>.

Consider the idealized elementary <chemical reaction>
$$
\mathrm A\rightleftharpoons\mathrm B,
\qquad k_f=0.3\,\mathrm{s}^{-1},\qquad k_r=0.1\,\mathrm{s}^{-1}.
$$
Assume a well-mixed <closed system> at fixed <temperature> and <volume>, with first-order <law of mass action> rates in both directions and no competing <chemical reactions>. Write $a=[\mathrm A]$ and $b=[\mathrm B]$ for <concentrations> in $\mathrm M=\mathrm{mol}/\mathrm L$. The total $a+b=C=1\,\mathrm M$ is conserved because each conversion replaces one <molecule> by one <molecule>. This conservation is special to the chosen <stoichiometry>.

The forward and reverse <reaction rates>, measured as conversions per unit <volume> per unit time, are $J_f=k_fa$ and $J_r=k_rb$. Hence
$$
\frac{da}{dt}=-k_fa+k_rb=-(k_f+k_r)a+k_rC.
$$
Setting this <derivative> to zero gives
$$
a_{\rm eq}=\frac{k_rC}{k_f+k_r}=0.25\,\mathrm M,
\qquad b_{\rm eq}=0.75\,\mathrm M.
$$
With $a(0)=1\,\mathrm M$ and $b(0)=0$, solving the <ordinary differential equation> yields
$$
a(t)=\bigl(0.25+0.75e^{-0.4t/\mathrm s}\bigr)\,\mathrm M,
\qquad b(t)=\bigl(0.75-0.75e^{-0.4t/\mathrm s}\bigr)\,\mathrm M.
$$
At $t=5\,\mathrm s$, these are approximately $0.3515\,\mathrm M$ and $0.6485\,\mathrm M$. Initially $J_f=0.3\,\mathrm{M/s}$ and $J_r=0$. At <chemical equilibrium>, both are $0.075\,\mathrm{M/s}$, while the net <reaction rate> $J_f-J_r$ is zero. The persistent exchange is visible in the lower panel even after the upper panel has nearly flattened.

\Image[reversible-reaction-equilibrium.png]
{title=Constant equilibrium composition with continuing molecular conversion}
{description=The idealized <chemical reaction> starts entirely in A. Upper panel: <concentrations>. Lower panel: forward, reverse and net <reaction rates>. The equilibrium rates coincide at a positive value.}

Why does <chemical equilibrium> also involve a <free energy> minimum? For a <closed system> at fixed <temperature> and <pressure>, without imposed non-expansion <work>, stable equilibrium minimizes <Gibbs free energy> over compositions allowed by conserved elemental inventories. See \a[https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/3-00-thermodynamics-of-materials-fall-2002/14c95abd6fac023d84bd33c5d74d787d_lecture_02_oneside.PDF][W. C. Carter, MIT thermodynamics notes, sections 2-1.14 and 2-1.19]{ref}. This is <constrained Gibbs minimization for chemical equilibrium>: freely minimizing over unrelated species amounts would change the inventory. At fixed <temperature> and <volume>, the appropriate potential is <Helmholtz free energy>. In either setting, varying an allowed <extent of reaction> gives the corresponding <free energy> slope $\sum_i\nu_i\mu_i$, with molar <chemical potentials> and signed <stoichiometric vector> entries. An interior equilibrium has zero slope; stability requires a minimum. For A converting to B, this says $\mu_{\mathrm B}=\mu_{\mathrm A}$.

The beauty of this connection is that molecular exchange, a macroscopic composition, and a geometric minimum describe the same balance. In the ideal concentration model, let $x=b/C$ and choose the standard molar <chemical potential> difference consistent with $K=3$. Up to a composition-independent constant, the fixed-volume <Helmholtz free energy> then obeys
$$
\frac{F}{nRT}=(1-x)\ln(1-x)+x\ln x-x\ln3,
$$
where $n$ is the conserved total <amount of substance> and $R$ the <molar gas constant>. Its <derivative> is $\ln[x/(1-x)]-\ln3$, vanishing at $x=3/4$. Its second <derivative> is $1/x+1/(1-x)>0$ inside $0<x<1$, so this balance is the unique minimum. The mixing <entropy> terms explain why the favored composition still contains A.

= Chemical disequilibrium
{parent=Chemical equilibrium}

A chemical system is out of local <chemical equilibrium> when reaction adjustment cannot keep pace with transport, imposed changes or external driving. Comparing the <chemical relaxation time> with transport and irradiation times identifies plausible departures. <Disequilibrium chemistry in an exoplanet atmosphere> combines this principle with mixing, horizontal advection and <atmospheric photochemistry>.

= Zero chemical potential from number-changing reactions
{parent=Chemical equilibrium}
{title2=$\mu_\phi=0$}

For one particle species in <chemical equilibrium> under $2\phi\leftrightarrow3\phi$, chemical balance gives $2\mu_\phi=3\mu_\phi$, hence $\mu_\phi=0$. Elastic $2\phi\leftrightarrow2\phi$ imposes no such constraint. In an expanding universe the number-changing reaction must remain fast compared with the <Hubble parameter>; after chemical freeze-out a nonzero <chemical potential> can develop even if elastic collisions maintain <kinetic equilibrium>.

= Chemical relaxation time
{title2=$\tau_{\rm chem}$}
{parent=Chemical equilibrium}

The chemical relaxation time estimates how rapidly a perturbed abundance returns toward <chemical equilibrium>. Comparing it with the <eddy mixing time> identifies a <chemical quench level>.

For the A-to-B model in <chemical equilibrium>, deviations decay by <exponential decay> with $\tau_{\rm chem}=1/(k_f+k_r)=2.5\,\mathrm s$. After one such interval, $1/e$ of the initial deviation remains; reaching one percent takes $\ln(100)\tau_{\rm chem}\simeq11.51\,\mathrm s$. Multiplying both <rate constants> by two preserves their ratio and the equilibrium composition, but halves $\tau_{\rm chem}$ to $1.25\,\mathrm s$. The equilibrium exchange rates double to $0.15\,\mathrm{M/s}$. Faster exchange therefore coexists with the same final composition and the same <free energy> minimum.

\Image[equilibrium-vs-rate.png]
{title=Doubling both rates changes the approach time but preserves equilibrium}
{description=Left: B <concentration> for two pairs of <rate constants> with the same ratio. Right: the shared ideal-mixture <Helmholtz free energy>, shifted to zero at its constrained minimum.}

= Equilibrium constant
{title2=$K$}
{parent=Chemical equilibrium}
{wiki}

For a specified <chemical reaction> and <standard state> convention, the dimensionless <equilibrium constant> is the equilibrium value of the <reaction quotient> $Q=\prod_i a_i^{\nu_i}$, where $a_i$ denotes <thermodynamic activity> and $\nu_i$ a signed <stoichiometric vector> entry. <Thermodynamic activities> incorporate the reference state and departures from ideal behavior. See \a[https://goldbook.iupac.org/terms/view/E02177][IUPAC Gold Book, equilibrium constant]{ref}.

In the ideal concentration model for A converting to B, use $a_{\mathrm A}=[\mathrm A]/c^\circ$ and $a_{\mathrm B}=[\mathrm B]/c^\circ$ with the same reference $c^\circ=1\,\mathrm M$. Their ratio gives $Q=[\mathrm B]/[\mathrm A]$ and $K=0.75/0.25=3$. Equality $K=k_f/k_r$ applies to this elementary first-order pair; it is not a universal ratio of two measured <rate constants> for an arbitrary multistep <chemical reaction>.

At a specified constant <temperature>, with consistent <standard states>, the standard molar reaction <Gibbs free energy> is
$$
\Delta_rG^\circ=\sum_i\nu_i\mu_i^\circ=-RT\ln K,
\qquad \Delta_rG=\Delta_rG^\circ+RT\ln Q=RT\ln(Q/K).
$$
Here $\Delta_rG$ is the local slope per <mole> of <extent of reaction> at fixed <temperature> and <pressure>, rather than the finite total <Gibbs free energy> change between two mixtures. At equilibrium $\Delta_rG=0$, while $\Delta_rG^\circ$ generally remains nonzero. For $K=3$ at $298\,\mathrm K$, it is about $-2.72\,\mathrm{kJ/mol}$. For positive activities, $Q<K$ favors forward conversion and $Q>K$ favors reverse conversion; neither sign supplies a <reaction rate>. See \a[https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/5-111sc-principles-of-chemical-science-fall-2014/ec12f084d867a5e5532b12ee99bdc767_MIT5_111F14_Lec18.pdf][MIT 5.111, Lecture 18, reaction free energy and equilibrium]{ref}.

= Dissociation constant
{parent=Equilibrium constant}
{wiki}
{title2=$K_d$}

= Standard state
{parent=Chemical equilibrium}

A standard state is a specified reference state used to define a species' standard <chemical potential> $\mu_i^\circ$. The reference convention, including <temperature>, must be consistent when calculating <thermodynamic activities>, <reaction quotients>, and <equilibrium constants>.

= Thermodynamic activity
{parent=Standard state}
{title2=$a_i=\exp[(\mu_i-\mu_i^\circ)/(RT)]$}

The thermodynamic activity is the dimensionless quantity defined by $\mu_i=\mu_i^\circ+RT\ln a_i$, where $R$ is the <molar gas constant>. For an ideal dilute solution with a specified <concentration> reference, $a_i=c_i/c^\circ$; departures from ideality are represented by an <activity coefficient> multiplying this ratio.

= Activity coefficient
{parent=Thermodynamic activity}
{title2=$\gamma_i$}

= Reaction quotient
{parent=Chemical equilibrium}
{title2=$Q=\prod_i a_i^{\nu_i}$}

For a <chemical reaction> with signed <stoichiometric vector> entries $\nu_i$, positive for products and negative for reactants, the reaction quotient is the product of the species' <thermodynamic activities> raised to these powers. It equals the <equilibrium constant> at <chemical equilibrium>, with all quantities using the same <standard state> convention.

= Photon chemical potential
{parent=Chemical equilibrium}

The chemical potential of photons in thermal equilibrium is zero because their number is not conserved.

= Ideal gas
{parent=Thermodynamics}
{wiki}

= Perfect gas
{synonym}

An ideal gas is a model of a dilute <gas> whose particle centres move according to <classical mechanics> and occupy negligible <volume (thermodynamics)> compared with their container. Treating the particles as <point masses> removes the molecular-size correction; neglecting interactions between separated particles removes the intermolecular contribution to <potential energy>. The usual <kinetic theory> model retains brief <elastic collisions> with the walls and, optionally, with other particles: these transfer <momentum> while conserving the total <kinetic energy> of the colliding bodies. There is no appreciable interaction <energy> stored between <collisions>. These assumptions lead to the <ideal gas law>, $PV=Nk_BT$. \a[https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-2/pages/2-1-molecular-model-of-an-ideal-gas][OpenStax University Physics Volume 2, section 2.1: molecular model and ideal gas law]{ref} \a[https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-2/pages/2-2-pressure-temperature-and-rms-speed][OpenStax University Physics Volume 2, section 2.2: kinetic-model assumptions]{ref}

The model's attraction is its universality: at the same <temperature> and <number density>, different classical <ideal gases> have the same <pressure>, even when their particle <masses> differ. In <ideal gas pressure from wall collisions>, heavier particles move more slowly at a given <temperature>, and the two effects cancel. For example, one <mole> of any <ideal gas> at $300\,\mathrm K$ in $0.025\,\mathrm{m^3}$ has <pressure> approximately $99.774\,\mathrm{kPa}$, as computed under the <ideal gas law>.

The <ideal gas law> describes the translational contribution to <pressure>; it does not specify all of the <internal energy> or the <heat capacity>. A <monatomic gas> has three classical translational <degrees of freedom>, whereas a <gas> of <molecules> can also store <rotational kinetic energy> and <energy> in vibrational <normal modes>. An <equation of state> alone therefore does not determine how much <heat> a chosen <temperature> increase requires. It also does not select a process: <isothermal expansion> and <adiabatic expansion> need different additional constraints. \a[https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-2/pages/2-3-heat-capacity-and-equipartition-of-energy][OpenStax University Physics Volume 2, section 2.3: internal degrees of freedom and heat capacity]{ref}

A <real gas> departs from this approximation when particle size or interactions become important, especially at high <number density> or low <temperature> near a <phase transition>. The <Van der Waals equation> adds such corrections. Independently, at sufficiently low <temperature> or high <number density>, <quantum statistics> can become essential: even particles with negligible interactions need not obey the classical <ideal gas law>. The classical statistical assumption is the <nondegenerate gas> regime, which is distinct from neglecting interactions. \a[https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-2/pages/2-1-molecular-model-of-an-ideal-gas][OpenStax University Physics Volume 2, section 2.1: departures from ideal-gas behavior]{ref} \a[https://davidtong.org/teaching/statistical-physics/statmechhtml/S3][Tong, Statistical Physics, section 3: quantum gases and the classical limit]{ref}

= Partial pressure
{parent=Ideal gas}
{wiki}
{title2=$p_i$}

The <partial pressure> of a component of a <gas> <mixture> is the <pressure> it would exert alone at the same <temperature> and total <volume (thermodynamics)>. For an ideal <mixture>, $p_i=n_iRT/V$ and $p=\sum_i p_i$. The <chemical potential of an ideal gas> uses $p_i$ for that component rather than the total <pressure> $p$.

= Molar gas constant
{parent=Ideal gas}
{title2=$R=N_Ak_B$}

The molar gas constant is the product of the <Avogadro constant> and the <Boltzmann constant>. It converts the particle-number form of the <ideal gas law>, $pV=Nk_BT$, into the amount-of-substance form $pV=nRT$, using $N=nN_A$ with $n$ measured in <moles>.

= Ideal gas law
{parent=Ideal gas}
{title2=$PV=Nk_BT=n_{\rm mol}RT$}
{wiki}

The <ideal gas law> is the <equation of state> relating the absolute <pressure> $P$, <volume (thermodynamics)> $V$, particle count $N$, and absolute <thermodynamic temperature> $T$ of a classical <ideal gas>:
$$
PV=Nk_BT=n_{\rm mol}RT,\qquad
N=n_{\rm mol}N_{\mathrm A},\qquad R=N_{\mathrm A}k_B.
$$
Here $k_B$ is the <Boltzmann constant>, $N_{\mathrm A}$ is the <Avogadro constant>, $R$ is the <molar gas constant>, and $n_{\rm mol}$ is the <amount of substance> in <moles>. The distinct symbol $n_{\rm mol}$ avoids confusing <amount of substance> with the <number density> $n=N/V$, measured in $\mathrm{m^{-3}}$: the same law can be written $P=nk_BT$. Use absolute <pressure>, relative to a <vacuum>, rather than an unconverted <gauge pressure>. \a[https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-2/pages/2-1-molecular-model-of-an-ideal-gas][OpenStax University Physics Volume 2, section 2.1: particle-count and molar forms]{ref}

The exact <SI> definitions of the <Avogadro constant> and <Boltzmann constant> give
$$
R=(6.02214076\times10^{23})(1.380649\times10^{-23})
\,\mathrm{J\,mol^{-1}\,K^{-1}}
=8.31446261815324\,\mathrm{J\,mol^{-1}\,K^{-1}}.
$$
This numerical value is exact; the applicability of the <ideal gas> model to a <real gas> is an approximation. \a[https://www.bipm.org/en/si-base-units/mole][BIPM, exact Avogadro constant in the mole definition]{ref} \a[https://www.bipm.org/en/si-base-units/kelvin][BIPM, exact Boltzmann constant in the kelvin definition]{ref}

For $n_{\rm mol}=1\,\mathrm{mol}$, $T=300\,\mathrm K$, and $V=0.025\,\mathrm{m^3}$, direct substitution gives
$$
RT=2494.338785445972\,\mathrm{J\,mol^{-1}},\qquad
P=\frac{n_{\rm mol}RT}{V}
=99773.55141783888\,\mathrm{Pa}
\approx99.774\,\mathrm{kPa}.
$$
At fixed <temperature> and particle count, doubling the <volume (thermodynamics)> to $0.050\,\mathrm{m^3}$ halves the <pressure> to approximately $49.887\,\mathrm{kPa}$: this is <Boyle's law>. At fixed <volume (thermodynamics)> and particle count, doubling the <temperature> from $300\,\mathrm K$ to $600\,\mathrm K$ doubles the <pressure> to approximately $199.547\,\mathrm{kPa}$. These ratios use <kelvins>, not readings in the <degree Celsius> scale: $300\,\mathrm K$ is $26.85\,{}^\circ\mathrm C$, and $600\,\mathrm K$ is $326.85\,{}^\circ\mathrm C$.

\Image[ideal-gas-state-slices.png]
{title=Ideal gas law: pressure versus volume at fixed temperatures, and pressure versus absolute temperature at fixed volume}

The curved slices show <Boyle's law> at three <temperatures>; the straight slice shows proportionality to absolute <temperature> at one fixed <volume (thermodynamics)>. They are relations among equilibrium states, rather than a prescribed process history. Compression could hold the <temperature> fixed through <heat transfer>, or raise it during <adiabatic compression>. To calculate a process's <heat> or <work>, supplement the <ideal gas law> with the process constraint, the <first law of thermodynamics>, and the relevant <internal energy> or <heat capacity> information. The graph's extrapolation toward $T=0$ is mathematical; it does not assert that a <real gas> remains classical or gaseous there.

For a stellar <mixture> with fixed <mean molecular weight>, the <ideal gas law> can be written $P=\mathcal R\rho T/\mu$, where $\rho$ is <mass density> and $\mathcal R$ is the <stellar gas constant>. It assumes negligible interaction corrections and a <nondegenerate gas>; excitation or <ionization> can change the particle count and thermodynamic response.

In his 1834 memoir on the motive power of <heat>, Émile Clapeyron combined gas laws into a relation between <pressure>, <volume (thermodynamics)>, and <temperature> while analysing <heat engines> (printed page 164). The equation above uses modern <SI> notation and absolute <thermodynamic temperature>; it is not a transcription of his historical temperature convention. \a[https://www3.nd.edu/~powers/ame.20231/clapeyron1834.pdf][Clapeyron, Mémoire sur la puissance motrice de la chaleur (1834), page 164]{ref}

= Ideal gas pressure from wall collisions
{parent=Ideal gas law}
{title2=$P=Nm\langle v_x^2\rangle/V$}

Consider a particle of <mass> $m$ in a rigid rectangular box of length $L$ along $x$ and wall <surface area> $A$, so $V=AL$ is the <volume (thermodynamics)>. Choose $+x$ toward the right wall. For an incoming normal <velocity> $v_x=v>0$, an <elastic collision> reverses the normal <momentum>:
$$
p_{x,\rm in}=mv,\qquad p_{x,\rm out}=-mv,\qquad
\Delta p_{x,\rm particle}=-2mv,\qquad
J_{x,\rm wall}=+2mv.
$$
The <impulse> delivered to the wall points outward, toward $+x$, by <Newton's third law>. The particle's opposite <momentum> change is not the wall's <impulse>. A tangential <velocity> component is unchanged by the ideal smooth-wall <collision>.

\Image[ideal-gas-wall-momentum.png]
{title=Schematic ideal gas model: an elastic bounce transfers outward momentum to a wall; this is not a real experimental setup}

Ignoring intervening particle-particle <collisions> in this counting model, the particle travels a normal distance $2L$ before hitting the same wall again. Its hit rate is $|v_x|/(2L)$; multiplying by the <impulse> per hit gives the time-averaged normal <force> and its contribution to <pressure>:
$$
\overline F_{\perp,1}=(2m|v_x|)\frac{|v_x|}{2L}
=\frac{mv_x^2}{L},\qquad
P_1=\frac{\overline F_{\perp,1}}{A}=\frac{mv_x^2}{V}.
$$
For example, stipulate $m=4\times10^{-26}\,\mathrm{kg}$, $|v_x|=500\,\mathrm{m\,s^{-1}}$, $L=0.10\,\mathrm m$, and $A=0.010\,\mathrm{m^2}$. Then $V=0.0010\,\mathrm{m^3}$, the <impulse> per hit is $4\times10^{-23}\,\mathrm{N\,s}$, the hit rate is $2500\,\mathrm{s^{-1}}$, and $P_1=10^{-17}\,\mathrm{Pa}$. This is one particle's tiny time-averaged contribution to one wall's <pressure>, not an <isotropic> equilibrium <gas> by itself.

For $N$ particles of equal <mass>, add their <force> contributions and define $\langle v_x^2\rangle=N^{-1}\sum_{i=1}^Nv_{x,i}^2$. In the <gas>'s rest frame, <isotropy> gives $\langle v_x^2\rangle=\langle v_y^2\rangle=\langle v_z^2\rangle=\langle v^2\rangle/3$, where $v^2=v_x^2+v_y^2+v_z^2$. Hence
$$
P=\frac{Nm\langle v_x^2\rangle}{V}
=\frac{Nm\langle v^2\rangle}{3V},\qquad
\frac12m\langle v^2\rangle=\frac32k_BT
\quad\Longrightarrow\quad PV=Nk_BT.
$$
The final equality uses the classical <equipartition theorem>. The <kinetic pressure of an isotropic gas> therefore recovers the <ideal gas law> from <momentum> transfers. Doubling the normal speed would double both the <impulse> per hit and the hit rate, giving four times the <pressure> contribution; <pressure> measures a mean squared speed. Interparticle <elastic collisions> redistribute <velocities> and help establish <isotropy>; the individual round-trip times need not remain $2L/|v_x|$, but the equilibrium <momentum flux> result survives. \a[https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-2/pages/2-2-pressure-temperature-and-rms-speed][OpenStax University Physics Volume 2, section 2.2: wall-collision pressure derivation]{ref}

= Monatomic gas
{parent=Ideal gas}
{title2=$\gamma=5/3$}

A monatomic gas consists of individual atoms or ions rather than molecules. In the classical dilute ideal-gas regime, its three translational degrees of freedom give $U=3Nk_BT/2$ by <equipartition theorem>. Thus $C_V=3Nk_B/2$, while $C_P-C_V=Nk_B$ follows by differentiating the ideal-gas enthalpy $H=U+PV=5Nk_BT/2$. Consequently the <adiabatic index> is $\gamma=C_P/C_V=5/3$ and the <specific internal energy> is $e=3P/(2\rho)$. The same translational result applies to a fully ionized mixture when the particle numbers are fixed. This excludes additional excitation, ionization and radiation contributions to the heat capacity.

= Mean free path
{parent=Ideal gas}
{title2=$\ell$}

The mean free path is the average distance travelled between <collisions>. For dilute targets with <number density> $n$ and effective <collision cross-section> $\sigma_c$, $\ell\sim1/(n\sigma_c)$; identical-particle conventions introduce factors such as $\sqrt2$.

= Mean free path through randomly oriented disc absorbers
{parent=Mean free path}
{title2=$\ell^{-1}=n_{\rm com}(1+z)^3\pi R_d^2/2$}

For an unclustered population of fully covering circular disc absorbers, the inverse <mean free path> is proper number density times mean projected area. A comoving number density must first be multiplied by $(1+z)^3$. The incidence then constrains the proper radius; covering factor and multiplicity of detectable lines can change the inference.

= Mean molecular weight
{title2=$\mu$}
{parent=Ideal gas}
{wiki}

The mean molecular weight is the average mass per freely moving gas particle in units of the atomic mass unit. A fully ionized hydrogen-helium gas with mass fractions $X,Y$ has $\mu^{-1}=2X+3Y/4$.

= Stellar gas constant
{parent=Mean molecular weight}
{title2=$\mathcal R=k_B/m_u$}

In stellar <ideal gas> equations, the <stellar gas constant> $\mathcal R=k_B/m_u$ converts a dimensionless <mean molecular weight> $\mu$ into the specific thermal relation $P=\mathcal R\rho T/\mu$. Here $m_u$ is the reference atomic mass unit, commonly approximated by the proton mass in simplified hydrogen-helium models. It has units of energy per mass per temperature. It is the molar gas constant divided by the molar mass corresponding to $m_u$, rather than the molar gas constant used with a dimensional molar molecular weight.

= Mean molecular weight per ion
{parent=Mean molecular weight}
{title2=$\mu_i^{-1}=\sum_jX_j/A_j$}

= Ion mean molecular weight
{synonym}

The <mean molecular weight> per ion counts nuclei rather than nuclei plus free electrons: $\rho/(\mu_i m_u)$ is the ion number density. For species with mass fractions $X_j$ and mass numbers $A_j$, $\mu_i^{-1}=\sum_jX_j/A_j$. It is useful when ions provide a thermal <ideal gas> pressure but <electron degeneracy pressure> supplies a separate contribution.

= Mean molecular weight per electron
{title2=$\mu_e=\rho/(m_un_e)$}
{parent=Mean molecular weight}

The mean molecular weight per electron is the mass carried per free electron in units of atomic mass $m_u$. Fully ionized hydrogen has $\mu_e=1$, while helium, carbon, and oxygen have approximately $\mu_e=2$. It converts <electron number density> to bulk <mass density> in the <equation of state of a cold electron gas>.

= Heat capacity ratio
{title2=$\gamma$}
{parent=Ideal gas}
{wiki}

= Specific-heat ratio
{synonym}

= Adiabatic exponent
{synonym}

= Adiabatic index
{synonym}

The specific-heat ratio of an ideal gas is $\gamma=C_p/C_V>1$.

= Nondegenerate gas
{parent=Ideal gas}

A gas is nondegenerate when its occupation numbers are low enough that <quantum statistics> reduce to the <Maxwell-Boltzmann distribution>. A common criterion is $n\lambda_{\rm th}^3\ll1$, where $\lambda_{\rm th}$ is the thermal de Broglie wavelength.

For an <ideal gas>, this is the classical statistical regime; nondegeneracy alone does not assert the absence of interactions.

= Internal energy of an ideal gas
{parent=Ideal gas}

For a fixed amount of ideal gas, internal energy depends only on temperature. If $C_V$ is constant, $dE=C_V\,dT$ and hence $E=C_VT$ after a choice of energy zero.

= Mayer's relation
{c}
{parent=Ideal gas}
{wiki}

= Mayer relation
{c}
{synonym}

For an ideal gas of $N$ particles,
$$
C_p-C_V=Nk_B.
$$
Thus, with $\gamma=C_p/C_V$, one has $Nk_B/C_V=\gamma-1$.

= Real gas
{parent=Thermodynamics}
{wiki}

A <real gas> departs from the <ideal gas> law because interactions and molecular volume matter. Nonideal <equations of state>, including the <Dieterici equation>, model such departures.

= Dieterici equation
{parent=Real gas}
{c}

The Dieterici equation of state
$$
p=\frac{k_BT}{v-b}\exp\left(-\frac{a}{k_BTv}\right)
$$
models excluded volume through $b$ and attraction through $a$. Its critical point has $v_c=2b$ and $T_c=a/(4bk_B)$.

= Intensive and extensive thermodynamic quantities
{parent=Thermodynamics}

An extensive quantity scales in proportion to the amount of material, while an intensive quantity is unchanged when the system is replicated. Energy, entropy, volume, and particle number are extensive; temperature, pressure, and chemical potential are intensive.

= Extensive quantity
{parent=Intensive and extensive thermodynamic quantities}

= Extensive variable
{synonym}

An extensive quantity scales in proportion to the size of a thermodynamic system: combining $n$ independent identical copies multiplies it by $n$. <Energy>, <entropy> and <volume> are examples.

= Euler relation in thermodynamics
{c}
{parent=Extensive quantity}
{title2=$E=TS-PV+\mu N$}

= Thermodynamic Euler relation
{synonym}

For a single-component equilibrium system with extensive <internal energy> $E(S,V,N)$, scaling its <entropy>, <volume> and particle number together gives $E(\lambda S,\lambda V,\lambda N)=\lambda E(S,V,N)$. The <Euler theorem for homogeneous functions> therefore gives $E=S\partial_SE+V\partial_VE+N\partial_NE$. The <first law of thermodynamics> identifies these derivatives as $T,-P,\mu$, respectively, proving $E=TS-PV+\mu N$. Dividing by volume yields the <entropy density> formula $s=(\rho+P-\mu n)/T$. This step fixes the entropy normalization rather than merely identifying its differential. Differentiating the Euler relation and subtracting $dE=T\,dS-P\,dV+\mu\,dN$ gives the <Gibbs-Duhem equation> $S\,dT-V\,dP+N\,d\mu=0$. The argument requires extensivity; it is not an assumption about arbitrary nonadditive systems.

= Gibbs-Duhem equation
{c}
{parent=Thermodynamics}
{wiki=Gibbs–Duhem_equation}

The <Euler theorem for homogeneous functions> applied to an <extensive quantity>, together with $dE=T\,dS-p\,dV+\mu\,dN$ give
$$
E=TS-pV+\mu N,
\qquad
S\,dT-V\,dp+N\,d\mu=0.
$$

= Conjugate variables
{disambiguate=thermodynamics}
{parent=Thermodynamics}
{wiki}

= Thermodynamic conjugate variable
{synonym}

Two thermodynamic variables are conjugate when their product has units of energy and they occur as a paired term in a thermodynamic differential, such as pressure and volume or tension and length.

= First law of thermodynamics
{parent=Thermodynamics}
{title2=$dU=\delta Q-\delta W$}
{wiki}

The first law applies <conservation of energy> to a thermodynamic system. For a <closed system> with unchanged bulk <kinetic energy> and <potential energy>, write
$$
dU=\delta Q-\delta W,\qquad \Delta U=Q-W.
$$
Here $U$ is <internal energy>, $Q>0$ is <heat> entering the system and $W>0$ is <work> done by the system on its surroundings. Compression therefore gives negative $W$. Some references define <work> positive when done on the system; their plus sign represents the same balance with a different convention. If bulk <kinetic energy> or <potential energy> changes, include it in the total <energy> balance. \a[https://web.mit.edu/16.unified/www/FALL/thermodynamics/notes/node15.html][MIT, First law and work sign convention]{ref}

Supplying $1000\,\mathrm J$ of <heat> while a gas does $300\,\mathrm J$ of <work> increases its <internal energy> by $700\,\mathrm J$. Compressing it with $200\,\mathrm J$ of input <work> in an <adiabatic process> instead gives $Q=0$, $W=-200\,\mathrm J$ and $\Delta U=200\,\mathrm J$.

The distinction between $d$ and $\delta$ records state versus path: <internal energy> is a <state function>, whereas <heat> and <work> depend on the process. For an <ideal gas> expanding reversibly at constant <temperature>, $W=Nk_BT\log(V_2/V_1)$ and $Q=W$, since <internal energy of an ideal gas> depends only on <temperature>. Choose $Nk_BT=1000\,\mathrm J$ and $V_2=2V_1$: then $Q=W=693.15\,\mathrm J$. An insulated <free expansion> into vacuum between the same equilibrium endpoints has $Q=W=0$. Both have $\Delta U=0$, yet their transfers differ. Their common <entropy> increase is $Nk_B\log2$. \a[https://web.mit.edu/16.unified/www/FALL/thermodynamics/notes/node40.html][MIT, Ideal-gas entropy differences]{ref}

For a simple compressible equilibrium system of fixed composition, combining the laws gives
$$
dU=T\,dS-p\,dV.
$$
Here $p$ is <pressure> and $V$ is <volume>. This identity relates neighboring equilibrium states. Identifying $T\,dS$ with actual <heat> and $p\,dV$ with actual <work> requires a suitable <reversible thermodynamic process>; an irreversible piston calculation uses the external resisting <pressure> for boundary <work>. This distinction prevents applying a <reversible ideal-gas adiabat> to an <irreversible adiabatic piston compression>.

= Adiabatic process
{parent=First law of thermodynamics}
{wiki}

An adiabatic process exchanges no heat with its surroundings, so $\delta Q=0$. A reversible adiabatic process is isentropic.

= Adiabatic compression
{parent=Adiabatic process}

= Adiabatic expansion
{parent=Adiabatic process}

= Isentropic process
{parent=Adiabatic process}
{wiki}

An isentropic process keeps the <entropy> constant. Every reversible <adiabatic process> is isentropic, while an irreversible adiabatic process can increase entropy.

= Reversible ideal-gas adiabat
{parent=Adiabatic process}

For a fixed amount of ideal gas with constant heat capacities, the first law and $\delta Q=0$ give
$$
TV^{\gamma-1}=\text{constant},
\qquad
pV^\gamma=\text{constant},
$$
where $\gamma=C_p/C_V$.

= Enthalpy
{parent=Thermodynamics}
{title2=$H=U+pV$}
{wiki}

Enthalpy is the <state function> obtained by adding <pressure> times <volume (thermodynamics)> to <internal energy>:
$$
H=U+pV.
$$
Here $U$ denotes <internal energy>, also written $E$ in some references. Like <internal energy>, <enthalpy> is measured in <joules> and is an <extensive variable>. Its definition packages the <internal energy> change and expansion <work> into one useful quantity for fixed-<pressure> calculations. It does not define a body's stored <heat>. \a[https://goldbook.iupac.org/terms/view/E02141/plain][IUPAC Gold Book, enthalpy]{ref}

For a fixed amount and composition of a <simple compressible system>, the relation between neighboring equilibrium states is
$$
dH=T\,dS+V\,dp.
$$
Here $T$ is absolute <temperature> and $S$ is <entropy>. Thus <entropy> and <pressure> are the natural variables of this <thermodynamic potential>, with <partial derivatives> $T=(\partial H/\partial S)_p$ and $V=(\partial H/\partial p)_S$. Fixed composition matters: changing composition adds further terms. The formula follows in one line from the equilibrium <first law of thermodynamics> identity:
$$
dH=dU+p\,dV+V\,dp=(T\,dS-p\,dV)+p\,dV+V\,dp=T\,dS+V\,dp.
$$
The cancellation of $p\,dV$ is the reason <enthalpy> replaces <volume (thermodynamics)> with <pressure> as a natural variable. This identity concerns equilibrium states; it does not identify $T\,dS$ with actual irreversible <heat> transfer. \a[https://davidtong.org/teaching/statistical-physics/statmechhtml/S4#S4.SS4.SSS1][David Tong, Statistical Physics, section 4.4.1: enthalpy]{ref}

For a <closed system> undergoing a <quasistatic process> at constant <pressure>, require fixed amount and composition, only <pressure>-<volume (thermodynamics)> <work>, no <friction>, and unchanged bulk <kinetic energy> and <potential energy>. Then the actual <first law of thermodynamics> balance gives
$$
\delta Q=dU+p\,dV=dH-V\,dp,\qquad
dp=0\ \Longrightarrow\ Q_p=\Delta H.
$$
The symbol $Q_p$ means transferred <heat> for this fixed-<pressure> process. If the piston example in <heat> additionally meets these conditions, its $20\,\mathrm{kJ}$ of incoming <heat> equals $\Delta H$, while $\Delta U=15\,\mathrm{kJ}$ and $p\Delta V=W=5\,\mathrm{kJ}$. The example shows exactly what <enthalpy> adds to <internal energy>: the expansion <work> required at the imposed <pressure>.

Within a single <thermodynamic phase>, <heat capacity at constant pressure> gives
$$
C_p=\left(\frac{\partial H}{\partial T}\right)_p,\qquad
\Delta H=\int_{T_1}^{T_2}C_p(T)\,dT
$$
with the amount and composition fixed. Constant $C_p$ gives $\Delta H=C_p\Delta T$; dividing by <mass> gives <specific heat capacity at constant pressure>. At equilibrium <melting>, <enthalpy> can instead change through <latent heat> while <temperature> stays fixed. The <sensible heat> example therefore adds two single-<thermodynamic phase> <enthalpy> changes and one <latent heat> contribution.

The usefulness of <enthalpy> lies in matching the imposed constraint: a fixed-<pressure> experiment naturally reports $\Delta H$, while fixed-<volume (thermodynamics)> heating with no other <work> reports $\Delta U$. Neither equality is universal. Additional <work> or changing <pressure> alters the relation between supplied <heat> and $\Delta H$; irreversible <entropy> production also prevents identifying actual $\delta Q$ with $T\,dS$. The separate <Joule-Thomson coefficient> branch uses <enthalpy> to describe throttling under its own assumptions.

= Joule-Thomson coefficient
{c}
{parent=Enthalpy}
{title2=$\mu=(\partial T/\partial p)_H$}

The temperature change per pressure change at fixed enthalpy is $\mu=(\partial T/\partial p)_H=[T(\partial V/\partial T)_p-V]/C_p$. A positive coefficient means cooling under an adiabatic throttle pressure drop.

= Virial criterion for Joule-Thomson cooling
{parent=Joule-Thomson coefficient}

For $p=k_BT[n+B_2(T)n^2]$, the coefficient is $\mu=N[TB_2'-B_2]/[C_p(1+2B_2n)]$. On the stable dilute branch it is positive precisely when $d(B_2/T)/dT>0$.

= Specific enthalpy
{title2=$h$}
{parent=Enthalpy}

Specific enthalpy is <enthalpy> per unit mass. For a simple compressible fluid of fixed composition, the <first law of thermodynamics> gives
$$
dh=T\,ds+\frac{dp}{\rho},
$$
where $T$ is <temperature>, $s$ is <specific entropy>, $p$ is <pressure>, and $\rho$ is <mass density>. Along an <isentropic flow>, $dh=dp/\rho$. For a <perfect gas> with constant <specific-heat ratio> $\gamma$, $h=\gamma p/[(\gamma-1)\rho]$.

= Polytropic enthalpy
{parent=Specific enthalpy}
{title2=$Q=(n+1)p/\rho$}

For $p=K\rho^{1+1/n}$ with $K,n>0$, the specific <enthalpy> relative to vacuum is $Q=(n+1)K\rho^{1/n}=(n+1)p/\rho$. It obeys $\nabla Q=\nabla p/\rho$ and $nD_tQ=-Q\nabla\cdot\mathbf u$. The vacuum reference is fixed when these equations are combined with the <polytropic equation of state>.

= Enthalpy Maxwell relation
{parent=Enthalpy}

Since $T=(\partial H/\partial S)_p$ and $V=(\partial H/\partial p)_S$, equality of mixed derivatives gives $(\partial T/\partial p)_S=(\partial V/\partial S)_p$.

= Heat capacity at constant pressure
{parent=Enthalpy}
{title2=$C_p=(\partial H/\partial T)_p$}

= Constant-pressure heat capacity
{synonym}

For a fixed-composition <closed system>, the <heat capacity> at fixed <pressure> is $C_p=(\partial H/\partial T)_p$, with <enthalpy> $H$. Under the <quasistatic process> assumptions in <heat capacity>, only <pressure>-<volume (thermodynamics)> <work> is permitted, so $dp=0$ gives $\delta Q=dH=C_p\,dT$. Thus supplied <heat> is $Q=\Delta H$, or $Q=C_p\Delta T$ within a single <thermodynamic phase> when $C_p$ is constant. For an <ideal gas>, the expansion <work> explains why this exceeds the required <heat> at fixed <volume (thermodynamics)> for the same <temperature> increase. Its per-unit-<mass> counterpart is <specific heat capacity at constant pressure>.

= Difference between constant-pressure and constant-volume heat capacities
{title2=$C_P-C_V$}
{parent=Heat capacity at constant pressure}

For a simple compressible system,
$$
C_P-C_V
=T\left(\frac{\partial V}{\partial T}\right)_P
\left(\frac{\partial P}{\partial T}\right)_V.
$$
This follows by expressing the entropy as a function of temperature and volume and using a Maxwell relation.

= Diatomic ideal-gas enthalpy
{parent=Thermodynamics}

With translational and rotational modes active but vibration frozen, a diatomic ideal gas has $E=5Nk_BT/2$ and $H=7Nk_BT/2$.

= Irreversible adiabatic piston compression
{parent=Thermodynamics}

After a sudden increase to constant external pressure $p_1$, an insulated gas obeys $\Delta E=p_1(V_0-V_1)$ rather than a reversible adiabatic power law.

= Constant-pressure heating of an ideal gas
{parent=Thermodynamics}

At fixed pressure, $Q=\Delta H$ and the expansion work is $p\Delta V=Nk_B\Delta T$.

= Thermodynamic cycle
{parent=Thermodynamics}
{wiki}

A thermodynamic cycle returns a working substance to its initial state. Its net internal-energy change is zero, so the net work output equals net heat input.

= Thermal efficiency
{title2=$\eta=W/Q_{\rm in}$}
{parent=Thermodynamic cycle}
{wiki}

The thermal efficiency of a heat engine is the net work output divided by heat input. For a cycle absorbing $Q_{\rm in}$ and rejecting the positive amount $Q_{\rm out}$,
$$
\eta=1-\frac{Q_{\rm out}}{Q_{\rm in}}.
$$

= Otto cycle
{c}
{parent=Thermodynamic cycle}
{wiki}

The ideal Otto cycle has adiabatic compression, constant-volume heat addition, adiabatic expansion, and constant-volume heat rejection. For an ideal gas with compression ratio $r=V_1/V_2$ and specific-heat ratio $\gamma$,
$$
\eta=1-\frac1{r^{\gamma-1}}.
$$

= Diesel cycle
{c}
{parent=Thermodynamic cycle}
{wiki}

The ideal Diesel cycle consists of reversible adiabatic compression, constant-pressure heat addition, reversible adiabatic expansion, and constant-volume heat rejection.
